0.5c1.7 1.2 3.3 1.2 5 0s3.3-1.2 5 0 3.3 1.2 5 0 3.3-1.2 5 0"/></svg></span><span><strong>Houses for sale in the flood zone</strong><span class="t">8 in 10 listings do not say so</span></span></li><li><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 8.5h10.5a3 3 0 1 0-3-3M3 12.5h15a3 3 0 1 1-3 3M3 16.5h6"/></svg></span><span><strong>One flood outside the zone</strong><span class="t">$26,400 paid</span></span></li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 12, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">The costliest mistake is paying the second-home 6 percent tax on a home you live in: about $2,930 a year on a $350,000 house. Nine more follow, by cost.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Of people who bought a house in Horry County in 2024 and have its tax bill mailed to that house, about 1 in 7 are still taxed at 6 percent. That is the rate for second homes and rentals. It is the costliest mistake here, and it comes back every year until they apply for the 4 percent rate for the home they live in.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller's low tax bill, flood insurance and wind cover come next.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy)