width:100%}main#main .hx-fb-ai,main#main .hx-fb-q{min-width:0!important;max-width:100%!important;flex-wrap:wrap;box-sizing:border-box}main#main .hx-fb-q textarea{min-width:0;flex:1 1 100%;width:100%}main#main .hx-fb-go{flex:1 1 100%}}@media (min-width:1000px) and (max-width:1100px){.header-ctas .hdr-phone{display:none!important}}main#main>section[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:has(>.wrap>h2){padding:.9rem 0!important}main#main>section[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:has(>.wrap>h2)>.wrap{display:block!important;text-align:center!important;background:linear-gradient(160deg,rgba(42,48,64,.95),rgba(28,32,40,.97))!important;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.14)!important;box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.14),0 26px 60px -30px rgba(28,32,40,.6)!important;border-radius:28px!important;padding:2.2rem 1.6rem!important;margin-left:auto!important;margin-right:auto!important}@media (max-width:699px){main#main>section[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:has(>.wrap>h2)>.wrap{padding:1.6rem 1.2rem 1.7rem!important;border-radius:24px!important}}</style><style>main#main .hx-strip:empty{display:none}main#main .hx-strip:not(:empty)+.hx-fallback{display:none}</style></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 12, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">You can still buy a Myrtle Beach condo in a building with a lawsuit, with cash or a loan made outside Fannie Mae's rules.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, you can buy a condo in a building that is in a lawsuit. Cash works, because the building rules of Fannie Mae, Freddie Mac and FHA apply to the loans they buy or insure. Most of those loans cannot be used while the suit runs, unless it is a minor kind.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A building that has already made the repairs it sued over can be a real chance. FHA loans usually cannot be used there, and a conventional loan works only after the lender finds the suit minor. That leaves fewer buyers to compete for each unit. A suit over a structural problem not yet repaired can mean a large bill for every owner.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Heard the building is in a lawsuit? We find out what it is about before you offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Ask about a building</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#mortgage" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Getting a loan</a></li><li><a href="#what-works" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What still works</a></li><li><a href="#find-out" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How we find out</a></li><li><a href="#good-deal" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">When it is a deal</a></li><li><a href="#how-long" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How long suits last</a></li><li><a href="#condo-in-litigation-faq" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Condo in litigation FAQ</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="mortgage"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Getting a loan">Can you get a mortgage on a condo in litigation?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Usually you cannot use a conventional or FHA loan, unless the suit is minor. <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae</a> will not buy a loan on a unit in a building whose association is named in a pending lawsuit. The same goes when the developer is in a suit about the building's safety, structure, livability or use.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The exception is a suit the lender finds minor, in one of the ways Fannie Mae's guide lists. <a href="https://guide.freddiemac.com/app/guide/section/5701.3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac's rule</a> is close to Fannie Mae's. Arbitration or mediation that is expected to become a lawsuit counts the same as a suit.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Which lawsuits count as minor?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If the lender finds the suit touches none of the four things named above, these suits can be minor:</p><ul class="cl-plain"><li><b>The association sues an owner for unpaid dues</b>Or it forecloses on a unit for them. Our <a href="/hoa/liens-and-foreclosure/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">guide to HOA liens and foreclosure</a> covers those suits.</li><li><b>The insurer defends it and pays</b>The association's insurer has agreed to defend the suit, and the association's insurance covers the amount.</li><li><b>Small next to the savings</b>The expected damages and legal costs are no more than 10 percent of the building's funded reserves, the money it has saved for repairs.</li><li><b>Repairs already made</b>The association is suing to recover money for repairs already made, and losing would not hurt it.</li><li><b>Damage to one unit</b>The suit is about damage to a single unit.</li><li><b>No money at stake</b>A dispute between neighbors, for example, where no one is asking for money.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A suit the association brings against the builder over defects is not minor while the repairs are still to be made.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What does FHA allow?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/default/files/Housing/documents/40001-hsgh-Update-18.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHA</a> turns down a building in a suit about the same four things as Fannie Mae's rule, or one with a risk the association's insurance does not cover. For an FHA loan on one unit, such a suit still counts for 12 months after it ends.</p><figure class="cl-card cl-tw"><table class="cl-tab"><caption>Which loans work, by kind of lawsuit</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Kind of lawsuit</th><th scope="col">Conventional loan</th><th scope="col">FHA loan</th><th scope="col">Cash</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">The association sues an owner for unpaid dues</th><td class="yes"><span class="l">Conventional loan</span>Yes</td><td class="yes"><span class="l">FHA loan</span>Yes</td><td class="yes"><span class="l">Cash</span>Yes</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The insurer defends the suit, and insurance covers it</th><td class="yes"><span class="l">Conventional loan</span>Yes</td><td class="yes"><span class="l">FHA loan</span>Yes</td><td class="yes"><span class="l">Cash</span>Yes</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The association sues the builder over repairs already made</th><td class="yes"><span class="l">Conventional loan</span>Yes, if the lender finds it minor</td><td class="no"><span class="l">FHA loan</span>Usually not</td><td class="yes"><span class="l">Cash</span>Yes</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A suit about the building's safety or structure</th><td class="no"><span class="l">Conventional loan</span>No</td><td class="no"><span class="l">FHA loan</span>No</td><td class="yes"><span class="l">Cash</span>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>A conventional loan here means one sold to Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac. A "Yes" for a loan means the lender finds the suit minor and not about the building's safety or structure. Cash has no loan for these rules to govern; the risk of a large bill stays with you.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A building can fail <a href="/invest/non-warrantable-condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Fannie Mae's rules for a building</a> for other reasons too, such as how many units are rentals.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Told the building is in a lawsuit?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We find out what the suit is about and tell you which loans can still be used, before you offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Find out what the suit is about</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-works"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What still works">Which loans still work on a condo in litigation?</h2><ul class="cl-plain"><li><b>Cash</b>The building rules of Fannie Mae, Freddie Mac and FHA apply to the loans they buy or insure. A cash purchase has no such loan.</li><li><b>A loan kept outside Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac</b>Some lenders keep a loan or sell it to other investors. Each writes its own building rules. One published program reviews lawsuits case by case, and will not consider one about the building's structure.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a suit about the structure, cash may be the only way to buy. Cash does not remove the risk. A large repair bill still comes to you as an owner, and your next buyer needs a loan too, unless they pay cash.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our affiliated lender, BrickWood Mortgage, a separate company, can tell you which loans fit the building. How those loans work is in our <a href="/invest/condotel-financing/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">guide to condo loans outside Fannie Mae's rules</a>.</p><p class="cl-disc">Chapter3 Realty Corp has a business relationship with BrickWood Mortgage (NMLS #189497). Because of this relationship, this referral may provide Chapter3 Realty a financial or other benefit. You are never required to use BrickWood. You are free to shop any lender.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="find-out"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How we find out">How do you find out if a condo is in litigation?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We find out for you, before you make an offer. Our building review on every condo purchase covers any lawsuit. These papers show whether a building is in a suit, and why:</p><ul class="cl-plain"><li><b>The questionnaire lenders send</b>It asks the association whether it is in a lawsuit, and asks for papers from its attorney. More on that form is in our <a href="/hoa/documents/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">guide to HOA documents</a>.</li><li><b>The attorney's letter</b>For a suit the lender finds minor but whose cost is unknown, Freddie Mac asks for the complaint and a letter from the association's attorney. If the letter says insurance will likely not pay, the building fails Freddie Mac's rule.</li><li><b>What FHA asks</b>To approve a building with a suit, FHA wants a signed explanation. It gives the reason, the risk, when the suit may end, whether insurance can pay, and what it does to the association's money.</li><li><b>The court record</b>Horry County's court cases are listed in the state's public index, linked from the <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/clerk-of-court/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Clerk of Court's page</a>. A suit in federal court, like Renaissance Tower's, is listed elsewhere.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller's state form asks about common-area problems and special assessments, not about a lawsuit, so we ask the association.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Send us the building's name.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We find out whether its association is in a lawsuit, what the suit is about, and what it could cost you, before you offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Ask about a building's lawsuits</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="good-deal"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="When it is a deal">When is a condo in litigation a good deal?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A building in a suit can be a good deal when its association is suing the builder for repairs it has already made. The building has been fixed, and the owners are trying to get back money they already spent. With most FHA buyers out while the suit runs, there is less competition for each unit.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.3rem 0 1.5rem;max-width:720px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 720px" width="1200" height="675" alt="Three tall oceanfront condo towers behind the dunes in Myrtle Beach, with smaller condo buildings across a curving road to the right and the beach and ocean in front" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: USGS. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: USGS</a></span></div><figcaption>Oceanfront condo towers in Myrtle Beach, with their pools behind the dunes, seen from a plane over the ocean.</figcaption></figure><ul class="cl-plain"><li><b>A chance: the builder is sued, and the repairs are done</b>Before you offer, have a lender review the suit. If it finds the suit minor, a conventional loan can work there.</li><li><b>A warning: a structural problem not yet repaired</b>A large repair bill may come to every owner as a special assessment. Our guide explains <a href="/hoa/special-assessments/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">how a special assessment is set</a>.</li><li><b>A warning: owners suing their own board</b>In the Renaissance Tower case, owners sued the board members and the management company over repairs and the savings for them.</li></ul><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can a builder still be sued over an older building?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, in some cases. A defect suit must be brought within three years of when the problem was or should have been found. For a building finished after mid-2005, the limit is eight years after completion; older buildings had 13.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Neither limit protects a builder guilty of fraud, gross negligence or recklessness. A suit claiming gross negligence can come years later, as Wedgewood's did.</p><div class="cl-card cl-stat"><span class="big">73</span><p>condos in buildings built in 2018 or later were for sale in the local MLS on November 10, 2026, out of 2,860 in all.</p></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most condos for sale here are in buildings past the usual time limit for a builder suit. A new builder suit is less likely there, but we still check the record for one before you offer.</p><div class="hx-strip" data-place="myrtle-beach-condos" data-name="Myrtle Beach" data-head="Your Myrtle Beach condo, its building checked for lawsuits first"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Myrtle%20Beach">Get the condos for sale in Myrtle Beach<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="how-long"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How long suits last">How long does condo litigation last on the Grand Strand?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the Grand Strand condo cases we read in the court record, 7 to 14 years passed from the first filing to the last ruling. A suit just filed can run for years. A suit near its end can be worth waiting out.</p><figure class="c3-gchart cl-tl"><div role="table" aria-label="Grand Strand condo lawsuits ran 7 to 14 years"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Grand Strand condo lawsuits ran 7 to 14 years</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Condo complex and its years in court</span><span role="columnheader">Years from filing to the last ruling</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Riverwalk at Arrowhead</span><span class="f">Sued 2003, jury verdict 2009, last ruling 2017</span></span><span role="cell"><svg class="tk" role="img" aria-label="Riverwalk at Arrowhead: Sued 2003, jury verdict 2009, last ruling 2017, 14 years" viewBox="0 0 1000 14" preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false"><rect x="0" y="0" width="1000" height="14" fill="rgba(28,32,40,.07)"/><rect x="115" y="0" width="539" height="14" fill="#c4783a"/></svg><span class="v">14 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Magnolia North</span><span class="f">Sued 2003, jury verdict 2009, last ruling 2017</span></span><span role="cell"><svg class="tk" role="img" aria-label="Magnolia North: Sued 2003, jury verdict 2009, last ruling 2017, 14 years" viewBox="0 0 1000 14" preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false"><rect x="0" y="0" width="1000" height="14" fill="rgba(28,32,40,.07)"/><rect x="115" y="0" width="539" height="14" fill="#c4783a"/></svg><span class="v">14 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Wedgewood</span><span class="f">Sued 2018, last ruling 2025</span></span><span role="cell"><svg class="tk" role="img" aria-label="Wedgewood: Sued 2018, last ruling 2025, 7 years" viewBox="0 0 1000 14" preserveAspectRatio="none" focusable="false"><rect x="0" y="0" width="1000" height="14" fill="rgba(28,32,40,.07)"/><rect x="692" y="0" width="270" height="14" fill="#c4783a"/></svg><span class="v">7 years</span></span></div><div class="ax" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span class="axt"><i style="left:0%">2000</i><i style="left:38.5%">2010</i><i style="left:76.9%">2020</i></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Each bar runs from the year the association sued to the last court ruling we read. For Riverwalk and Magnolia North, the last ruling decided what the builder's insurer had to pay.</figcaption></figure><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Riverwalk at Arrowhead Country Club and Magnolia North</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">These two condo complexes in Myrtle Beach were built from 1997 to 2002. Both associations sued the builder in 2003. Juries found for both in 2009, and the Court of Appeals affirmed in 2011 and 2012.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At trial, the associations' experts put Magnolia North's repairs at about $9.2 million, and the defense's experts at about $2.4 million. While a suit runs, no one knows the size of the bill.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The builder had gone out of business in 2003. Whether its insurer had to pay the verdicts was decided by the state Supreme Court in 2017.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Wedgewood</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Wedgewood is a nine-building condo complex in Horry County, built from 2000 to 2001. The association sued in 2018, about 17 years after it was built. A jury found gross negligence, which lifts the time limit, and the Court of Appeals affirmed in 2025.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Renaissance Tower</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Renaissance Tower is a 21-story oceanfront condo building in Myrtle Beach. In October 2022, an engineer found it not structurally sound and ordered it evacuated. Owners sued in federal court that month.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The owners' lawsuit says some owners could not sell, and some buyers backed out of contracts. It says owners faced an assessment of more than $2 million for the steel repairs, and the association had less than $1.3 million in <a href="/hoa/reserves/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">reserves</a> in 2022.</p><div class="cl-card cl-stat"><span class="big">51</span><p>Renaissance Tower units changed hands in the past year, after the evacuation and the owners' suit.</p></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Units changed hands at the other three complexes too: 37 at Magnolia North, 23 at Riverwalk and 8 at Wedgewood. A building with a suit in its past still sells.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want a building's court record before you offer?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the building. We find out whether a suit is just starting or near its end, and what that means for your loan and your bill.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Ask about one building</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="condo-in-litigation-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Condo in litigation FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you get an FHA loan on a condo in litigation?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not when the suit is about the building's safety, structure, livability or use, or carries a risk its insurance does not cover. For an FHA loan on one unit, such a suit still counts for 12 months after it ends.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does a lawsuit against an owner for unpaid dues stop a condo loan?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. Fannie Mae and FHA do not count a suit the association brings to collect past-due dues or to foreclose on a unit.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does the seller have to tell you the building is in a lawsuit?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The state's disclosure form asks about common-area problems and special assessments, not about a lawsuit. We ask the association instead.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How long does condo litigation last?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">From 7 to 14 years. In three Grand Strand condo cases we read in the court record, that was the time from the association's suit to the last ruling.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you sell a condo that is in litigation?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Your buyer will need cash or a loan made outside Fannie Mae's and FHA's rules, unless the suit is a minor kind. Units changed hands in the past year in every building on this page.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-03</a>, <a href="https://guide.freddiemac.com/app/guide/section/5701.3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac 5701.3</a>, <a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/default/files/Housing/documents/40001-hsgh-Update-18.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD 4000.1</a>, <a href="https://www.newrezwholesale.com/media/b0hbzabm/smart-product-project-checklist-3-2026.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Newrez checklist</a>, <a href="https://www.usalliance.org/hubfs/assets/documents/forms/usalliance-condo-questionnaire.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Questionnaire</a>, <a href="https://www.llr.sc.gov/re/recpdf/Updated_Property_Disclosure_Form.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC disclosure</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t15c003.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 15-3</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/clerk-of-court/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Clerk of Court</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/media/opinions/HTMLFiles/COA/4888.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Op. 4888</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/media/opinions/HTMLFiles/COA/4943.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Op. 4943</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/media/opinions/HTMLFiles/SC/27698.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Op. 27698</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/media/opinions/HTMLFiles/COA/6124.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Op. 6124</a>, <a href="https://www.classaction.org/media/liberty-property-holdings-sc-llc-et-al-v-richardson-et-al.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Renaissance complaint</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycounty.org/parcelapp/rest/services/HorryCountyGISApp/MapServer" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">County parcels</a>. Our counts: condos for sale in our listing feed on November 10, 2026, by year built, in total. Units whose last deed came in the year before that day, from county parcels. Renaissance Tower figures are the owners' claims.</p><style>.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Oceanfront condo towers in Myrtle Beach. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them.</div></div></div><script type="ap