-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Florida to Myrtle Beach FAQ</h2><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Why do people move from Florida to Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Four reasons repeat in the national data and reporting: the insurance renewal, condo assessments under Florida&#39;s post-Surfside inspection rules, and day-to-day costs that grew larger than the income-tax savings. Some want to be closer to family up north without giving up a mild winter. Home prices matter too: a typical home here cost about $342,000 in July 2026 against about $378,000 statewide there. It is usually not taxes, and the tax section on this page says so plainly.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">What is a halfback move?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">A halfback is a person who moved from the Northeast or Midwest to Florida and then moved partway back north, to the Carolinas, Georgia or Tennessee. The usual reasons are to keep a mild winter and the ocean, pay less to own a home, and live closer to family. In a national count of 2025 moves, South Carolina gained about 5,400 net residents 65 and older, more than any state; more of them came from North Carolina than anywhere else, and Florida was next at nearly 1,900.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Is Myrtle Beach cheaper than Florida overall?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">On average, yes. The price data behind our cost of living page puts the Myrtle Beach area about nine index points below the Florida statewide average. Housing is the widest gap: a typical home costs about $342,000 here against about $378,000 statewide in Florida. Groceries and gas cost less here, and home insurance averages about $4,500 a year against about $6,500 across Florida. One category is higher: state income tax, which Florida does not charge. For most Florida households the house and the insurance outweigh the income tax, and the calculator on this page runs your own numbers.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Will I pay state income tax after moving from Florida to South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Yes. Florida has none and South Carolina taxes income at 1.99 percent up to $30,000 of taxable income and 5.21 percent past it, after a deduction of up to $30,000 for a married couple that shrinks as income rises. A couple on $120,000 of wages pays about $4,300 a year. Social Security is not taxed, and the deductions at 65 leave a modest pension taxed lightly or not at all.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Do I keep my Save Our Homes cap or homestead exemption if I leave Florida?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">No. The cap&#39;s benefit can transfer to another Florida homestead, but not across the state line. Selling ends it either way: the buyer of your house is reassessed at market value. Your South Carolina house is assessed under South Carolina&#39;s rules, including the 4 percent owner-occupied rate and, at 65 and older after a year of residency, a homestead exemption on the first $50,000 of value.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Is homeowners insurance cheaper in Myrtle Beach than in Florida?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">On average, yes. Priced on the same $300,000 house, a Myrtle Beach policy averages about $4,500 a year against about $6,500 across Florida, about $15,600 in Miami, about $10,400 in Naples and about $6,300 in Tampa. Premiums are set per address, so buy through us and we get you written wind, hail and flood quotes on the exact house before you make an offer.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Will my car cost more to own in South Carolina than in Florida?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">The tax on it, yes. Florida registration is a flat fee set by the car&#39;s weight. South Carolina bills every vehicle yearly on its value, roughly $360 to $460 on a $30,000 car plus a $50 road fee. Each vehicle also pays a one-time $250 fee at its first registration here, due within 45 days of the move.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);margin-top:2.2rem;padding-top:1.5rem;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Who wrote this</p><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;max-width:720px">Chapter 3 Realty is a real estate brokerage in Myrtle Beach, built as a partnership around a simple idea: you should be as informed as possible on the biggest purchase of your life, every single time. Tim Nash is the Broker-in-Charge, South Carolina broker license 43182, with more than 30 years selling real estate on the Grand Strand. He reviews every page we publish and runs our market analyses himself. His father, Fred Nash, also sold real estate here, and Fred Nash Boulevard by Myrtle Beach International Airport is named for him. Devin Day wrote this page. He is our Operations Officer and a relocator himself, from Ohio. Meet the team on <a href="/about/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">the about page</a> or read <a href="/why-chapter-3/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">why buyers work with us</a>. The direct line is <a href="tel:+18543332135" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">854.333.2135</a>.</p></div><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6;max-width:720px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.flsenate.gov/Laws/Constitution" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Florida Constitution, Article VII</a>, <a href="https://floridarevenue.com/property/Documents/pt112.pdf" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Florida DOR on the assessment cap</a>, <a href="https://www.flsenate.gov/Laws/Statutes/2025/320.08" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Florida vehicle registration fees</a>, <a href="https://dor.sc.gov/news/information-about-h-4216" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">South Carolina 2026 income tax</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/vehicle-tax/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Horry County vehicle tax</a>, <a href="https://www.hireahelper.com/moving-statistics/retirement-study-2026/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">2025 retiree moving study</a>, <a href="https://www.flsenate.gov/Laws/Statutes/2025/553.899" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Florida inspection law</a>, <a href="https://insurify.com/homeowners-insurance/states/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none" rel="noopener" target="_blank">home insurance averages by state and city</a>. We are not tax advisers; rates change and your situation decides the answer. Verify with a professional before you plan around any of it.</p></div></section>
