 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The income tax</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Five percent flat sounds close to ours. It mostly is.</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Massachusetts charges 5 percent on nearly everything, plus 4 percent more on income above about $1.08 million. South Carolina&#39;s top rate is 5.21 percent. On rates alone this is the closest matchup of any state we cover.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina gives a couple a deduction of up to $30,000 where Massachusetts has none, only an $8,800 personal exemption. The South Carolina deduction shrinks above $80,000 of income and is gone at $190,000, so a middle income keeps more here before any rate applies. Massachusetts taxes short-term capital gains at 8.5 percent where South Carolina treats them as ordinary income. The 9 percent top combined rate above the million-dollar line has no counterpart here.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">If a business sale, vested stock or a large short-term gain falls in the same year as your move, the timing of the sale against the change of domicile could mean large savings. That is a question for your accountant before you sign anything.</p></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Retirees, both directions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Check your pension type before you decide</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most of this page runs in South Carolina&#39;s favor, so read this section twice, because it does not. Massachusetts does not tax its own contributory public pensions: a retired Massachusetts teacher, police officer or state worker collects that pension free of Massachusetts income tax. South Carolina treats it like any other pension. The deduction reduces it: once you are 65, up to $15,000 of income per person is deducted, and the retirement income deduction of up to $10,000 counts against that same $15,000 rather than adding to it. Above that amount, the pension is taxed here.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a couple with one large Massachusetts public pension, that single line can offset much of what the property tax gives back. It does not usually make South Carolina the more expensive state, because the house, the heat and the property tax all still cost less here. It narrows the saving, and nobody should discover that in April. Run both states against your actual pension before you list the house; the <a href="/buyers/relocating/cost-of-living/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">calculator</a> takes a minute and we will happily check the arithmetic with you.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Everything else lands the usual way. Social Security is untaxed in both states. Private pensions, 401(k) and IRA withdrawals face the Massachusetts flat rate with no senior exclusion