-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">If you keep a foot in Ohio</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Ohio has a residency test with real teeth</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Ohio presumes you are still a resident until you show otherwise, and its test is specific. You cannot hold an Ohio driver&#39;s license at any point in a year you claim to be gone. Your days back in Ohio are counted, and at 213 or more the presumption becomes hard to beat. The non-residency statement has a hard deadline: filed by October 15 of the following year, it makes your non-resident status final; missed, Ohio presumes you never left.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">If the plan is a clean move, surrender the license early and the rest follows. If the plan is a winter here and a summer there, bring in a CPA before you set up the year, not after.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">If you keep a foot in Ohio</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Ohio has a residency test with real teeth</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Ohio presumes you are still a resident until you show otherwise, and its test is specific. You cannot hold an Ohio driver&#39;s license at any point in a year you claim to be gone. Ohio counts your nights in Ohio. A night counts when you are away overnight from your home outside Ohio and in Ohio for part of both days. At 213 or more in a year, the presumption that you have left no longer applies. The non-residency statement has a hard deadline: filed by October 15 of the following year, it makes your non-resident status final; missed, Ohio presumes you never left.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">If the plan is a clean move, surrender the license early and the rest follows. If the plan is a winter here and a summer there, bring in a CPA before you set up the year, not after.</p></div></section>
 
