hey are attachments to this form.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County asks for the application by May 31 so the bill mailed in the fall prints at 4 percent. File later and you can still qualify for that year, but you will be dealing with a corrected bill or a refund instead of a right bill the first time.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Homestead exemption at 65: not in year one</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">South Carolina exempts the first $50,000 of a legal residence&#39;s value for owners who are 65 or older, totally and permanently disabled, or legally blind. It requires the 4 percent status and one full year as a South Carolina legal resident as of December 31, so a retiree who moves this year applies for it next year, at the county Auditor.</p></div></section>
 
-<section id="utilities" style="background:var(--ivory);scroll-margin-top:120px"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Utilities</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Who provides what depends on which side of a line you are on</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">There is no single utility for the Grand Strand. Which company serves your house depends on the address, and neither electric provider publishes a street-level map. The fastest way to find out is to ask the seller for a recent bill or ask the closing attorney. Failing that, call and give them the address.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Electricity</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two providers cover almost all of Horry County. Santee Cooper, the state-owned utility, serves the City of Myrtle Beach and the other coastal municipalities. Horry Electric Cooperative, which is member-owned, serves most of the unincorporated county, including Carolina Forest, Socastee, Longs, and the rural areas.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Both run a credit check and may want a deposit. Santee Cooper sets its deposit from the two highest consecutive bills at that address, with a floor of $100, and refunds it after 13 months of on-time payments. A prepaid option costs $70 up front and skips the deposit and credit check entirely. Horry Electric charges $35 in one-time fees to open an account, a $5 membership, a $10 application and a $20 connection, and adds a deposit only if the credit check calls for one. Both want proof you own or rent the place, so have the settlement statement or lease handy.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Know about the demand charge before your first bill arrives. On their published 2026 residential schedules, both Santee Cooper and Horry Electric Cooperative bill a demand charge as well as the power you use. That means part of your bill is set by your single highest hour of use rather than your monthly total. The peak window is 3 p.m. to 6 p.m. from April through October and 6 a.m. to 9 a.m. from November through March. Running several heavy appliances at once inside that window sets a charge that applies to the whole month, so it is worth spreading the load. Our <a href="/buyers/relocating/cost-of-living/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">cost of living page</a> has the rates.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Water and sewer</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Outside the city limits, most of the coast is served by Grand Strand Water &amp; Sewer Authority, which asks for 24 hours notice before it turns anything on. Apply on a Friday and you are waiting until Monday, so make this call before the truck arrives, not after. Its new service fee is $40, and deposits run $40 to $90 for owners and $60 to $140 for tenants depending on whether you need water, sewer, or both.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Inside city limits you deal with the city. Myrtle Beach bills water, sewer, sanitation, and stormwater on one monthly statement and sets the deposit by meter size. North Myrtle Beach charges a meter deposit, $180 for the common three-quarter-inch meter, plus a $50 service charge. Conway runs its own utility billing. On the Georgetown side, Pawleys Island and Litchfield are served by the Georgetown County Water and Sewer District.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Gas, internet, and the rest</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Natural gas is not everywhere. Dominion Energy is the gas utility, but the mains reach only some neighborhoods, so availability is checked address by address. Plenty of homes here are all electric or run propane, which matters if you were planning on a gas range.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">For internet, HTC is a member-owned fiber cooperative covering much of the county, with published starting prices around $50 for 500 Mbps and about $75 for a gig. Spectrum is the incumbent cable provider and advertises lower first-year promotional rates that step up afterward. Brightspeed has fiber in parts of the city. Budget roughly $40 to $75 a month depending on the speed you need and whether you are still inside a first-year promotion.</p></div></section>
+<section id="utilities" style="background:var(--ivory);scroll-margin-top:120px"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Utilities</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Who provides what depends on which side of a line you are on</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">There is no single utility for the Grand Strand. Which company serves your house depends on the address, and neither electric provider publishes a street-level map. The fastest way to find out is to ask the seller for a recent bill or ask the closing attorney. Failing that, call and give them the address.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;max-width:720px;margin:1.2rem 0 1.4rem"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.88rem;color:var(--navy)"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.6rem .5rem;font-weight:600;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Provider and who it serves</th><th style="padding:.6rem .5rem;font-weight:600;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Deposit and fees</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;width:42%"><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Santee Cooper</strong><br/><span style="color:var(--muted)">State-owned. The City of Myrtle Beach and the other coastal municipalities.</span></td><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;color:var(--muted)">A deposit set from the two highest consecutive bills at that address, with a floor of $100, refunded after 13 months of on-time payments. A prepaid option costs $70 up front and skips the deposit and credit check.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;width:42%"><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Horry Electric Cooperative</strong><br/><span style="color:var(--muted)">Member-owned. Most of the unincorporated county, including Carolina Forest, Socastee, Longs, and the rural areas.</span></td><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;color:var(--muted)">$35 in one-time fees: a $5 membership, a $10 application and a $20 connection. A deposit only if the credit check calls for one.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;width:42%"><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Grand Strand Water &amp; Sewer Authority</strong><br/><span style="color:var(--muted)">Water and sewer for most of the coast outside the city limits.</span></td><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;color:var(--muted)">A $40 new service fee. Deposits run $40 to $90 for owners and $60 to $140 for tenants, depending on whether you need water, sewer, or both.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;width:42%"><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">City of Myrtle Beach</strong><br/><span style="color:var(--muted)">Water, sewer, sanitation, and stormwater inside the city, on one monthly statement.</span></td><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;color:var(--muted)">A deposit set by meter size.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;width:42%"><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">North Myrtle Beach</strong><br/><span style="color:var(--muted)">Water and sewer inside the city.</span></td><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;color:var(--muted)">A meter deposit, $180 for the common three-quarter-inch meter, plus a $50 service charge.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;width:42%"><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Conway</strong><br/><span style="color:var(--muted)">City of Conway. Water and sewer inside the city, and in some areas outside it.</span></td><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;color:var(--muted)">Inside the city, a $100 deposit and a $50 service charge. Outside the city, a $150 deposit for water and sewer or $75 for water only, plus the $50 charge. The city sets up service only after the closing.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;width:42%"><strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Georgetown County Water and Sewer District</strong><br/><span style="color:var(--muted)">Pawleys Island and Litchfield, on the Georgetown side.</span></td><td style="padding:.7rem .5rem;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);line-height:1.55;color:var(--muted)">A transfer fee to put the account in your name: $161 for water and sewer if you own, $261 if you rent.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Electricity</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two providers cover almost all of Horry County. Santee Cooper and Horry Electric Cooperative both run a credit check and may want a deposit. Both want proof you own or rent the place, so have the settlement statement or lease handy.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Know about the demand charge before your first bill arrives. On their published 2026 residential schedules, both Santee Cooper and Horry Electric Cooperative bill a demand charge as well as the power you use. That means part of your bill is set by your single highest hour of use rather than your monthly total. The peak window is 3 p.m. to 6 p.m. from April through October and 6 a.m. to 9 a.m. from November through March. Running several heavy appliances at once inside that window sets a charge that applies to the whole month, so it is worth spreading the load. Our <a href="/buyers/relocating/cost-of-living/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">cost of living page</a> has the rates.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Water and sewer</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Grand Strand Water &amp; Sewer Authority asks for 24 hours notice before it turns anything on. Apply on a Friday and you are waiting until Monday, so make this call before the truck arrives, not after. Inside city limits you deal with the city.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Gas, internet, and the rest</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Natural gas is not everywhere. Dominion Energy is the gas utility, but the mains reach only some neighborhoods, so availability is checked address by address. Plenty of homes here are all electric or run propane, which matters if you were planning on a gas range.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">For internet, HTC is a member-owned fiber cooperative covering much of the county, with published starting prices around $50 for 500 Mbps and about $75 for a gig. Spectrum is the incumbent cable provider and advertises lower first-year promotional rates that step up afterward. Brightspeed has fiber in parts of the city. Budget roughly $40 to $75 a month depending on the speed you need and whether you are still inside a first-year promotion.</p></div></section>
 
 <section id="trash" style="background:var(--ivory-2);scroll-margin-top:120px"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The one nobody warns you about</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Outside the city limits, no truck comes for your trash</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Inside the City of Myrtle Beach, trash is easy: about $34 a month on the same utility statement as water and sewer. That buys a garbage cart, a recycling cart, and two cubic yards of bulk and yard waste a week, all collected the same day. Carts go out no earlier than 6 p.m. the night before and come in by 11 p.m. on collection day, and that is a code the city enforces. Yard waste has to be in paper bags, not plastic.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In unincorporated Horry County there is no curbside collection at all. The county solid waste authority says so plainly, and nothing starts automatically when you close. You have three options: haul your own to one of the 25 recycling and convenience centers, hire a private hauler, or rely on a neighborhood association contract that covers it. The centers are open to unincorporated residents only and cap you at four cubic yards a day. Ask which of those three applies to the specific address before you close. People move here from cities and discover this on the second weekend.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">One more line item: sales tax runs 9 percent inside the City of Myrtle Beach and 8 percent everywhere else in Horry County. Unprepared groceries are taxed at zero either way.</p></div></section>
 
 <section id="parking" style="background:var(--ivory);scroll-margin-top:120px"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Free beach parking</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">The resident parking decal, and who qualifies</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The City of Myrtle Beach gives residents a free parking decal that covers every city-owned paid space: the on-street meters and pay stations, the street-end beach accesses, the decal-only areas in The Avenues, and events at the convention center. It does not cover the Pavilion garage or any private lot. What you can do on the sand once you park, town by town, is on our <a href="/buyers/relocating/beaches/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">beach rules page</a>. Given that beach access parking runs $3 an hour or $15 for the day in season, it is worth doing in your first month. One of our team lived here more than a year before he learned the decal existed.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Fewer people qualify than assume they do. It is not for all Horry County residents. It is for vehicles that live inside the city limits and on which <em>city</em> vehicle property tax has been paid. The city treats that tax as your parking payment. If you live in Carolina Forest or Socastee or Surfside, you do not qualify, no matter how close you are.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Apply in person the first time, at the parking office in the Pavilion Parking Garage, 914 North Kings Highway, at the Ninth Avenue North entrance; the number is 843-626-7275 and hours are worth a call first. Renewals can be done online. Bring, for each vehicle, your South Carolina registration, your driver&#39;s license, and the current Horry County vehicle tax receipt, and note that the address on the license has to match the address on the tax receipt. Golf cart owners bring the state permit number too. The decal runs two years and replacements cost $20.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">The other towns work differently and the details do not transfer. North Myrtle Beach has no physical decal at all; enforcement reads your license plate, registration renews every year, and its paid season runs March through October, 9 to 5. Myrtle Beach&#39;s paid season is shorter, March through September, but runs 9 a.m. to midnight. North Myrtle Beach does sell a limited number of annual passes to Horry County residents who live outside its limits, but the allocation is small and sells out early in the year, so it is not something to count on.</p></div></section>
 
-<section id="golfcart" style="background:var(--ivory);scroll-margin-top:120px"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Getting around</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Golf cart rules, because this is a golf cart town</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Many neighborhoods here run on golf carts, and the rules changed in 2025. To drive one on a public road you need four things. A $5 state permit from the DMV, good for five years or until you move. Proof of ownership and liability insurance. A driver who is at least 16 with a valid license. The registration and insurance in the cart. Statewide, a permitted cart may run in daylight, on roads posted 35 mph or less, within four miles of the address on the permit. In a gated community the four miles are measured from the gate. It may cross a faster road at an intersection. Every passenger under 12 must be belted.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Towns can add rules, and three of them have. North Myrtle Beach allows carts sunrise to sunset on 35 mph roads and lets them cross, but not drive along, Ocean Boulevard, 27th Avenue South, Highway 17, and Sea Mountain Highway; the fine is $100 and up to 30 days. Surfside Beach is the exception on hours: carts with permanently mounted headlights and taillights may run at night, carts without lights only from 30 minutes before sunrise to 30 minutes after sunset. The City of Myrtle Beach keeps daylight hours, bars carts from sidewalks and multi-use paths, and closes Ocean Boulevard to carts over Memorial Day weekend. Outside city limits the state rules apply.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Before you buy in a neighborhood because it is &ldquo;golf cart friendly,&rdquo; check the posted speed limits on the roads between the house and wherever you plan to drive. A 45 mph road you cannot cross at an intersection ends the trip.</p></div></section>
