 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Small stuff that is not small</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Bugs, and the rest of coastal life</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Mosquitoes are more noticeable than in a dense city, because much of the county is low, wet and rural. Horry County runs a mosquito control program with truck and aerial spraying that starts in early May, and residents outside city limits can request treatment. Near marsh you also meet biting midges, which everyone here calls no-see-ums. They are small enough to pass through a standard screen, so a finer mesh and a fan on the porch do more than repellent. The big outdoor bugs locals call palmetto bugs live in trees and mulch, not inside houses, and one wanders indoors now and then. None of this is a reason not to move. All of it is a reason to budget for pest control and look at the screens.</p></div></section>
 
