--sans);flex:none}main#main .c3-wf-num::before{content:attr(data-n)}main#main .c3-wf-h3{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:.6rem;font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.35rem;color:var(--navy);margin:2.4rem 0 .7rem;line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-wf-h3 .c3-wf-num{position:relative;top:-.1rem}main#main .c3-wf-rules{list-style:none;padding:0;margin:1rem 0 1.4rem;max-width:720px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16)}main#main .c3-wf-rules li{margin:0;padding:.7rem 0 .75rem;border-top:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16);color:var(--muted);line-height:1.55}main#main .c3-wf-rules strong{color:var(--navy)}main#main .c3-wf-story{max-width:720px;margin:1.2rem 0 1.4rem;padding:.2rem 0 .2rem 1.2rem;border-left:4px solid var(--brass);color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7}main#main .c3-gchart{max-width:720px!important;box-sizing:border-box}main#main .c3-wf-entry+.hx-strip,main#main .c3-wf-entry+.hx-strip+.hx-fallback{margin-bottom:2.2rem}main#main .c3-wf-entry+.hx-strip:not(:empty){margin-top:1.4rem}@media (min-width:1100px){main#main .c3-wf-entry+.hx-strip{max-width:none!important}}main#main>section[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:last-of-type:not(.c3-x)>.wrap{display:block!important;text-align:center!important;background:linear-gradient(160deg,rgba(42,48,64,.95),rgba(28,32,40,.97))!important;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.14)!important;border-radius:28px!important;padding:2.2rem 1.6rem!important;margin-left:auto!important;margin-right:auto!important;width:calc(100% - 32px);box-sizing:border-box}@media (max-width:699px){main#main>section[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:last-of-type:not(.c3-x)>.wrap{padding:1.6rem 1.2rem 1.7rem!important;border-radius:24px!important}}@media (min-width:980px) and (max-width:1099px){header nav.primary{gap:2.4rem!important}}@media (max-width:359px){.hx-feed .hx-fb-ai,.hx-feed label.hx-fb-q{min-width:0!important;max-width:100%!important;flex-wrap:wrap!important;box-sizing:border-box}.hx-feed .hx-fb-ai>*{min-width:0}.hx-feed .hx-pop{flex-wrap:wrap!important;max-width:100%}}</style><style>main#main .hx-strip:empty{display:none}main#main .hx-strip:not(:empty)+.hx-fallback{display:none}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The kind of water makes the biggest difference to the price. A house on the beach sold for about six times the middle house in Horry County. A house on the Waccamaw River sold for less than half of what one on the waterway did.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="On the beach, a house sold for the most and a condo for the least"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">On the beach, a house sold for the most and a condo for the least</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Kind of home and water</span><span role="columnheader">Middle sale price</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A house on the beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$2.2 million</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A house on the Intracoastal Waterway</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.609" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$1.3 million</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A house on the salt marsh, Garden City</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.57" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$1.24 million</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A house on the Cherry Grove channels</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.329" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$715,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A house on the Waccamaw River</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.247" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$537,000</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Any house in Horry County</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.172" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$375,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A condo in an oceanfront building</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.092" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$200,000</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>The middle sale price in the past year, from Horry County deed records. The gray bar is every house in the county, for comparison.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Lake houses have no bar, because the deeds do not show reliably which houses sit on a lake. On the day we counted, the 429 lake and pond houses for sale asked about $450,000 at the middle.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="which"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Seven kinds of water">Which kind of waterfront home fits you?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Waterfront here means seven different purchases. Pick what you want from the water, and go to its entry.</p><ol class="c3-wf-pick" aria-label="The seven kinds of waterfront"><li><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="w">The sun coming up out of the ocean, from your own porch:</span> <a href="#oceanfront-houses">Oceanfront houses</a></span></li><li><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="2" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="w">The same sunrise from a balcony, for far less:</span> <a href="#oceanfront-buildings">A condo in an oceanfront building</a></span></li><li><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="3" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="w">A boat at your own dock that can travel north or south behind the coast:</span> <a href="#intracoastal-waterway">The Intracoastal Waterway</a></span></li><li><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="4" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="w">Crabbing off your own dock on the tide:</span> <a href="#salt-marsh">The salt marsh and tidal creeks</a></span></li><li><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="5" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="w">A boat out back, with a city boat ramp and an inlet to the ocean:</span> <a href="#cherry-grove-channels">The Cherry Grove channels</a></span></li><li><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="6" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="w">Cypress, dark river water and a wildlife refuge:</span> <a href="#waccamaw-river">The Waccamaw River</a></span></li><li><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="7" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="w">A pontoon boat on a lake, for less:</span> <a href="#lakes">A lake or pond</a></span></li></ol><div class="c3-wf-entry" style="--wf-rows:auto auto auto auto auto 1fr"><h3 id="oceanfront-houses" data-toc="Oceanfront houses" class="c3-wf-h3"><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Oceanfront houses: the sun comes up out of the ocean</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">From central Myrtle Beach south to Garden City, the beach faces the southeast. The sun rises over open sea there on every morning of the year.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Farther north the shore curves. From Windy Hill to Cherry Grove, the sun comes up over the North Myrtle Beach shore on about 100 to 125 mornings around June.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-b/buyers-waterfront-homes/oceanfront-houses-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-b/buyers-waterfront-homes/oceanfront-houses-from-the-ocean-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-b/buyers-waterfront-homes/oceanfront-houses-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="782" alt="Two-story houses on wide green lawns behind the dunes, with wooden walkways to the beach and oak trees behind, seen from a plane over the ocean in Myrtle Beach" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: U.S. Geological Survey. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: U.S. Geological Survey</a></span></div><figcaption>Houses on wide lawns behind the dunes, facing the beach in Myrtle Beach, with wooden walkways down to the sand.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">An oceanfront house has no other house between its lot and the beach. More than 400 houses in Horry County stand in the first row like this.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the past year, at least 20 of them resold, and the middle price was about $2.2 million. Other houses within about two-thirds of a mile, off the water, resold for about $615,000. Want the sand a walk away instead? Our guide to <a href="/buyers/homes-near-the-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">houses near the beach</a> counts them by distance, and our guide to <a href="/buyers/pool-homes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">homes with a pool</a> shows where the pools are.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On the day we counted, the houses for sale listed as on the ocean asked about $3.4 million at the middle. About 6 in 10 first-row lots reach FEMA's VE zone, where waves are expected.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="wf-oceanfront-houses" data-name="oceanfront houses" data-head="Wake up on the beach: oceanfront houses for sale"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=the%20oceanfront">Book a showing on the oceanfront<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div><div class="c3-wf-entry" style="--wf-rows:auto auto auto auto auto 1fr"><h3 id="oceanfront-buildings" data-toc="A condo in an oceanfront building" class="c3-wf-h3"><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="2" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>A condo in an oceanfront building: the same sunrise for less</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A condo in a building on the beach gives you the same sunrise from a balcony, with the sand right below. It costs far less than a house on the beach.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-b/invest-condo-buildings/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-from-the-beach-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-b/invest-condo-buildings/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-from-the-beach-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-b/invest-condo-buildings/myrtle-beach-oceanfront-towers-from-the-beach-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Tall oceanfront towers with rows of balconies behind the dune in Myrtle Beach, with more towers lined up along the beach into the distance under a blue sky" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: DiscoA340. License: CC BY-SA 4.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 4.0">Photo: DiscoA340</a></span></div><figcaption>Oceanfront condo towers along the beach in Myrtle Beach.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">More than 600 condos in oceanfront buildings resold in the past year, for about $200,000 at the middle.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On the day we counted, about 1,100 condos were for sale in oceanfront buildings, and about 2 in 3 asked under $300,000.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not every unit in an oceanfront building faces the ocean. Of those condos for sale, about 700 list a unit that faces it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each building has its own monthly fee and its own rules on renting. We compare <a href="/invest/condo-buildings/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the HOA fees of the oceanfront buildings</a> for you, and if you plan to rent, <a href="/invest/condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the numbers on an oceanfront condo</a>.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="wf-oceanfront-condos" data-name="condos that face the ocean" data-head="The ocean from your balcony: condos facing the ocean"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=condos%20that%20face%20the%20ocean">Get the condos that face the ocean<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div><div class="c3-wf-entry" style="--wf-rows:auto auto auto auto auto 1fr"><h3 id="intracoastal-waterway" data-toc="The Intracoastal Waterway" class="c3-wf-h3"><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="3" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Intracoastal Waterway homes: your boat at your own dock</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">From a dock on the Intracoastal Waterway here, your boat can head north to Norfolk, Virginia, or south to Florida without going out on the ocean.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The waterway runs behind the Atlantic coast. When you want the ocean, the way out is Little River Inlet, at the North Carolina line.</p><p class="c3-wf-story">Our clients from New York bought beachfront in North Myrtle Beach near the waterway, so they could use their boat.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-little-river/little-river-waterfront-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-little-river/little-river-waterfront-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/submarkets-little-river/little-river-waterfront-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="900" alt="The Intracoastal Waterway at Little River from above: a slip basin full of boats ringed by condo buildings, labelled Mariner's Pointe, and docks with boats at the end of a street, labelled Waterfront" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo</a></span></div><figcaption>The Intracoastal Waterway at Little River from above, with boats at the docks at the end of Mineola Avenue and in the slip basin at Mariner's Pointe.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Flooding is lighter here than on the beach, the channels or the river. About 3 in 10 waterway house lots are wholly outside FEMA's high-risk flood zone.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 985 houses in Horry County have a lot that touches the waterway. In the past year, 55 houses on it sold for about $1.3 million at the middle, about twice the houses nearby, in every stretch.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="wf-waterway" data-name="the Intracoastal Waterway" data-head="Your boat out back: homes on the Intracoastal Waterway"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=the%20waterway">Book a showing on the waterway<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div><div class="c3-wf-entry" style="--wf-rows:auto auto auto auto auto 1fr"><h3 id="salt-marsh" data-toc="The salt marsh and tidal creeks" class="c3-wf-h3"><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="4" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Marsh front homes: crabs off your own dock</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On salt water in South Carolina, you need no fishing license to go crabbing with three or fewer drop nets, fold-up traps or hand lines with bait. People call it chicken necking, and a dock on the marsh is a place to do it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The marsh fills and drains with the tide twice a day. In Murrells Inlet's creeks, the water at a dock drops about 4 feet from high tide to low.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At low tide, a dock on a small creek may sit on mud. The width of the creek also limits the size of the dock the state allows. We check the depth at the dock at low tide, not at the showing.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-b/buyers-waterfront-homes/garden-city-marsh-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-b/buyers-waterfront-homes/garden-city-marsh-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-b/buyers-waterfront-homes/garden-city-marsh-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Aerial photo of the salt marsh at Garden City at high tide, with long private docks running out from houses on the mainland at left and on the beach strip at lower right" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo</a></span></div><figcaption>The salt marsh behind Garden City Beach from above, with private docks running out from the houses on both edges.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In <a href="/submarkets/garden-city/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Garden City</a>, about 260 houses are on the salt marsh or a tidal creek. The 13 that sold in the past year went for about $1.24 million at the middle, about twice the other houses on that side of the beach road.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In <a href="/submarkets/murrells-inlet/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Murrells Inlet</a>, about 220 houses are on the salt marsh, almost all on the creek side of US 17 Business. Over three years, 21 of them sold for about $930,000 at the middle.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="wf-marsh" data-name="the salt marsh" data-head="Crabbing water out back: homes on the salt marsh"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=a%20marsh%20dock">Have us check the depth at a marsh dock<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Not sure which water fits your boat?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us its height and how deep it sits in the water. We name the waters, the docks and the bridges that work for it.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us match the water to your boat</a></div><div class="c3-wf-entry" style="--wf-rows:auto auto auto auto 1fr"><h3 id="cherry-grove-channels" data-toc="The Cherry Grove channels" class="c3-wf-h3"><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="5" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Cherry Grove channel homes: a boat out the back door</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In Cherry Grove, in <a href="/submarkets/north-myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">North Myrtle Beach</a>, rows of houses line channels dug through the marsh behind the beach. Many have a dock behind the house.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Cherry Grove has the city's boat ramp on the marsh, with boat access to the marsh and the ocean through Hog Inlet. Inside Cherry Grove, the water moves about 4.5 feet between high and low tide.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-north-myrtle-beach/cherry-grove-channels-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-north-myrtle-beach/cherry-grove-channels-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/submarkets-north-myrtle-beach/cherry-grove-channels-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="857" alt="Cherry Grove from above: rows of houses on narrow lots between dug channels, with docks behind them, the marsh beyond, and the ocean along the bottom, labelled Boat ramp, Heritage Shores, Channel houses and Atlantic Ocean" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo, 2023. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo, 2023</a></span></div><figcaption>Houses on the Cherry Grove channels from above, many with a dock behind them, and the marsh and the city's boat ramp beyond.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The trade-off is flooding. Of about 680 houses on the channels and the marsh edge, all but 3 are in FEMA's high-risk flood zone.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A house on the channels resold for about $715,000 at the middle in the past year. A Cherry Grove house off the channels sold for about $665,000, a group that includes oceanfront houses.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="wf-cherry-grove-channels" data-name="the Cherry Grove channels" data-head="A boat out the back door: Cherry Grove channel homes"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Cherry%20Grove">Get the Cherry Grove channel homes for sale<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div><div class="c3-wf-entry" style="--wf-rows:auto auto auto auto 1fr"><h3 id="waccamaw-river" data-toc="The Waccamaw River" class="c3-wf-h3"><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="6" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Waccamaw River homes: dark water, cypress and a wildlife refuge</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Waccamaw National Wildlife Refuge, set up in 1997, protects tidal freshwater wetlands and old rice fields along the Waccamaw and Great Pee Dee rivers.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Fish and Wildlife Service calls them some of the most diverse freshwater wetlands in North America. Visitors paddle a blackwater swamp, walk among cypress trees and look for swallow-tailed kites and more than 200 other bird species.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-murrells-inlet/murrells-inlet-wacca-wache-landing-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-murrells-inlet/murrells-inlet-wacca-wache-landing-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/submarkets-murrells-inlet/murrells-inlet-wacca-wache-landing-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Aerial photo of the Waccamaw River off Wachesaw Road, labelled: the dark river, Wacca Wache Landing on its bank, a marina basin full of boats, and woods and buildings around it" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo</a></span></div><figcaption>The dark water of the Waccamaw River at Wacca Wache Landing, with a marina basin beside it, on the Georgetown County side.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Even at <a href="/submarkets/conway/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Conway</a>, the tide still raises and lowers the river about a foot. Nearly every river lot reaches the high-risk flood zone: all but 4 of about 460.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the past year, 14 houses on the river sold for about $537,000 at the middle, against about $330,000 for houses nearby that are not on the water.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="wf-river" data-name="the river" data-head="Wake up on the river: riverfront homes for sale"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=the%20Waccamaw%20River">Get the homes on the Waccamaw River<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div><div class="c3-wf-entry" style="--wf-rows:auto auto auto auto auto 1fr"><h3 id="lakes" data-toc="A lake or pond" class="c3-wf-h3"><span class="c3-wf-num" data-n="7" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Lakefront homes: a pontoon boat out back</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In <a href="/buyers/best-neighborhoods/#plantation-lakes" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Plantation Lakes</a> in Carolina Forest, owners take pontoon boats out on a lake with about 15 miles of shoreline. Many homes back onto the lake.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The owners' amenity area has day and overnight docks and a boat ramp. Boats on the lake are limited to 19 feet, with small motors.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/buyers/best-neighborhoods/plantation-lakes-docks-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/buyers/best-neighborhoods/plantation-lakes-docks-600w.webp 600w, /images/buyers/best-neighborhoods/plantation-lakes-docks-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Plantation Lakes from above: houses with back yards on a dark winding lake labelled Lake, and a small boat dock, marked Boat docks, beside two pools" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo</a></span></div><figcaption>Plantation Lakes from above: houses along the lake, and the boat docks at the owners’ amenity area.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On the day we counted, 9 houses on the lake there were for sale, asking about $780,000 to $1.3 million.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most lake and pond houses for sale ask far less. Every waterfront house for sale under $300,000 was on a lake or pond.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The state's coastal dock permit covers salt water and the marsh, not a lake. On a lake, we read the community's rules on docks and boats before you offer.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="wf-lakes-under-500k" data-name="lakefront homes" data-head="Lake living under $500,000"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=lakefront%20homes">Get the lakefront homes under $500,000<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="docks"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Docks">Can you build a dock on your waterfront lot?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On salt water, you can if the lot and the creek are wide enough and the state issues a permit. Only 52 of the 506 waterfront houses for sale list a dock. On canals, creeks, the marsh, rivers and the waterway, more than half do.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A dock over the tidal marsh or a saltwater creek needs a permit from the state's coastal permit office, SCDES, before it is built. That office used to be part of DHEC.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The permit covers tidal salt water, the marsh and other land the average high tide covers, and the beach. Docks on lakes and fresh water fall under other rules.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is the lot wide enough?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On a lot created after May 23, 1993, a private dock needs at least 75 feet of frontage at the marsh edge. On such a lot, under 50 feet means no dock of its own. Two neighbors with at least 50 feet each can share one.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A waterfront subdivision may have a dock master plan, recorded with its map of lots, that shows which lots may get a dock and where. A plan is a guide, not a promise of a permit.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How big can the dock be?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Under the state rule in force now, a dock on a tidal creek is sized by the creek's width, from marsh grass to marsh grass.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Creek width</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Dock the state usually allows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Under 10 feet</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">No dock</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">10 to 19 feet</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">No dock, except 50 square feet with no boat lift on a lot with over 500 feet of frontage, or where no dock is possible from the other side</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">20 to 50 feet</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">120 square feet</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">51 to 150 feet</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">160 square feet</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Over 150 feet</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">600 square feet</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Walkways, ramps and mooring piles do not count, and neither does a lift 8 by 20 feet or smaller. The table is not for docks on the waterway itself. The state has proposed new widths and sizes, and they need the legislature's approval.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What about the fee, the wait, repairs and selling?</h3><ul class="c3-wf-rules"><li><strong>The fee.</strong> The state charges $150 to apply for a private dock with a walkway 100 feet or shorter, and $250 for a longer one.</li><li><strong>The wait.</strong> Once the file is complete, the state posts a 15-day public notice. By law, the state has 30 days from the complete file to decide.</li><li><strong>Repairs.</strong> Normal repair of a standing dock, kept to its old size, needs only a request and a letter back from the state.</li><li><strong>Storms.</strong> A dock a storm destroys can be rebuilt to its old layout if the work is done within five years.</li><li><strong>Selling.</strong> An active permit can be transferred to the buyer for $25. Both owners sign.</li><li><strong>Other approvals.</strong> The owner may also need the Army Corps of Engineers, the city or county, and the HOA to approve.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you would rather not ask an HOA about a dock or a boat, 86 waterfront houses for sale listed <a href="/buyers/no-hoa-homes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">no HOA fee</a> on the day we counted.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Buying a house for its dock?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We find the dock's permit, make sure it is active and can transfer to you, and read the subdivision's dock master plan before you offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us look up the dock's permit before you offer</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="flood"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Flood">Do you need flood insurance on a waterfront home?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A federally regulated lender requires <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">flood insurance</a> on a waterfront home in FEMA's high-risk zone. How many lots are in that zone depends on the water.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Water</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Lots that reach the high-risk flood zone</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Cherry Grove channels</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Over 99%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Waccamaw River</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">About 99%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Oceanfront houses</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">About 98%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Oceanfront houses, in the zone where waves are expected (VE)</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">About 60%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Intracoastal Waterway</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">About 70%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For one-family houses with a FEMA policy in Horry County, the middle yearly cost was:</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Where the house is</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Middle cost a year</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Outside the high-risk zone, a home you live in</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">About $550</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Zone A or AE, a home you live in</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">About $1,070</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Zone A or AE, a home you do not live in</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">About $1,370</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Zone V, where waves are expected</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">About $3,200</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The zone of the lot is not always the zone of the house. FEMA and the lender go by where the house stands and by a survey of how high its floor is. We get both for the house you like.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We also look for past flooding at the house, in the records listed in <a href="/buyers/undisclosed-flooding/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">how to find out if a house flooded</a>. Wind and homeowners insurance are in <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">coastal home insurance</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="by-boat"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="By boat">Can you get from your dock to the ocean by boat?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, from the waterway, the channels and the ocean-side creeks. From the waterway between Little River and Socastee, a marked channel about 2 miles long leads to the jetties of Little River Inlet.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The sandbar at the inlet's mouth shifts, and its buoys are moved often. Murrells Inlet opens straight to the ocean between its own jetties, and boaters new to it are advised to enter with local knowledge.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Which bridges does your boat pass under?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Thirteen crossings span the waterway in about 30 waterway miles from the state line to Socastee. Every fixed bridge has a reported clearance of 65 feet, so a boat under 65 feet tall passes each one.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A boat taller than a swing bridge or the railroad bridge when it is closed waits for it to open.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Crossing, north to south</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Room under it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">US 17 bridge, Little River</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">65 feet, as reported</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Little River swing bridge</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">7 feet closed; it opens</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Robert Edge Parkway bridge</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">65 feet, as reported</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Barefoot Landing swing bridge</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">31 feet closed; it opens</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">SC 22 bridge</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">65 feet, as reported</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Cable car, about a mile south of SC 22</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">67 feet</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Grande Dunes Boulevard bridge</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">65 feet, as reported</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Grissom Parkway bridge</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">65 feet, as reported</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Railroad bridge, next to US 501</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">16 feet closed; it opens</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">US 501 bridge</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">65 feet, as reported</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Fantasy Harbour bridge</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">65 feet, as reported</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Socastee swing bridge</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">11 feet closed; it opens</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Highway bridge at Socastee</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">65 feet, as reported</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Under the federal rule, the Little River swing bridge opens on signal, except from 7 to 9 a.m. and 4 to 6 p.m. on weekdays that are not federal holidays. Then it opens only on the hour and the half hour.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Barefoot Landing and Socastee swing bridges have no special schedule in the federal rules, so they open when a boat signals.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/buyers-relocating-which-town/socastee-swing-bridge-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/buyers-relocating-which-town/socastee-swing-bridge-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/buyers-relocating-which-town/socastee-swing-bridge-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Aerial photo of a blue steel truss swing bridge carrying a two-lane road over the Intracoastal Waterway at Socastee, with a newer four-lane bridge at the lower left and houses at the upper left" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo, 2023. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo, 2023</a></span></div><figcaption>The old Socastee swing bridge, the blue steel span, on the Intracoastal Waterway, with the newer high bridge at the lower left.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The federal channel is designed for at least 12 feet of depth. The federal chart guide still warns of rock ledges along its edges from about Little River to US 501.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much does the water drop at the dock?</h3><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="At an ocean-side dock the water drops 4 feet or more, on the waterway about 2"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">At an ocean-side dock the water drops 4 feet or more, on the waterway about 2</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Where the dock is</span><span role="columnheader">High tide to low</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">The ocean, at Springmaid Pier</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.686" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 4.8 feet</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Cherry Grove, Little River and Murrells Inlet creeks</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.639" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">4 to 4.5 feet</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">The middle of the waterway</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.283" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">1.6 to 2 feet</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">The Waccamaw River at Conway</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.161" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">about 1 foot</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>The average change in water level from high tide to low, in NOAA's tide predictions. It happens about twice a day.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On the ocean side, a lot that floats a boat at high tide may sit on mud at low tide.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you keep a boat without a dock?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. A deeded boat slip is another way to keep a boat on the water. Horry County has 322 slips sold as real estate, 223 of them in <a href="/submarkets/little-river/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Little River</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the past year, slips at Mariner's Pointe there sold for about $16,500 at the middle.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Will your boat make it to the ocean from this house?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the boat's height and the house. We list every bridge between the dock and the inlet, and check the depth at the dock at low tide.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Ask us which bridges stand between a house and the inlet</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="listing-words"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Listing words">Oceanfront or ocean view: what does waterfront mean in a listing?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Oceanfront means the first row, with no house between the lot and the beach. A house with an ocean view can be across the street or a few rows back.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A listing that names a water is often near it, not on it. Of 117 houses for sale whose listing names the Intracoastal Waterway, only 22 are marked as waterfront.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Every house that names a lake or a canal is marked as waterfront. Before you pay for water, we check the lot line, the deed and the flood map.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="waterfront-home-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Waterfront home FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does the sun rise over the ocean in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. From central Myrtle Beach south to Garden City, the sun rises over open sea every morning of the year. Farther north, from Windy Hill to Cherry Grove, it comes up over the shore on many mornings around June.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is the cheapest waterfront in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A condo in an oceanfront building, which sold for about $200,000 at the middle. For a house, it is a lake or pond: every waterfront house for sale under $300,000 was on one.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much more does a waterfront home cost?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On the beach, a house resold for about $2.2 million at the middle, against about $615,000 for houses nearby off the water. On the Intracoastal Waterway, a house sold for about twice the houses nearby. On Garden City's salt marsh, a house sold for about twice the other houses on that side of the beach road.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I build a dock on my waterfront lot?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On salt water, yes, if the lot and the creek are wide enough and the state issues a permit. Lots created since 1993 need 75 feet of frontage at the marsh edge.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I keep a boat without a dock?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. A deeded boat slip is another way, and most of the slips sold as real estate in Horry County are in Little River.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do I need flood insurance on a waterfront home?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A federally regulated lender requires it in FEMA's high-risk zone. Nearly every lot on the beach, the Cherry Grove channels and the Waccamaw River reaches that zone. On the Intracoastal Waterway, about 3 in 10 lots are wholly outside it.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.horrycounty.org/parcelapp/rest/services/HorryCountyGISApp/MapServer" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County GIS</a>, <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">county deeds</a>, <a href="https://www.fema.gov/openfema-data-page/fima-nfip-redacted-policies-v2" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenFEMA policies</a>, <a href="https://nauticalcharts.noaa.gov/publications/coast-pilot/files/cp4/CPB4_C12_WEB.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NOAA Coast Pilot 4</a>, <a href="https://tidesandcurrents.noaa.gov/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NOAA tides</a>, <a href="https://gml.noaa.gov/grad/solcalc/solareqns.PDF" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NOAA solar equations</a>, <a href="https://des.sc.gov/programs/bureau-coastal-management" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SCDES coastal permits</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/coderegs/Chapter%2030.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Reg. 30-12</a>, <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-33/chapter-I/subchapter-J/part-117/subpart-B/section-117.911" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">33 CFR 117.911</a>, <a href="https://www.dnr.sc.gov/fish/swfaqs.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SCDNR</a>, <a href="https://www.fws.gov/refuge/waccamaw/about-us" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fish and Wildlife Service</a>, <a href="https://www.parks.nmb.us/473/Parks" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">North Myrtle Beach parks</a>. Our counts: county parcels, flood maps and the past year's deeds; CCMLS listings in aggregate, counted November 10, 2026.</p><style>.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Houses on wide lawns behind the dunes, facing the beach in Myrtle Beach, with wooden walkways down to the sand. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>. Chapter3 Realty resized the photo.</div><div role="listitem">Oceanfront condo towers along the beach in Myrtle Beach. Photo: DiscoA340, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hotels_along_the_Grand_Strand_Beachfront.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty resized the photo and cut 1x1, 4x3 and 16x9 crops from its lower part.</div><div role="listitem">The Intracoastal Waterway at Little River from above, with boats at the docks at the end of Mineola Avenue and in the slip basin at Mariner's Pointe. Photo: USDA Farm Service Agency, NAIP, <a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://planetarycomputer.microsoft.com/dataset/naip" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Microsoft Planetary Computer</a>. Chapter3 Realty cut this view from the NAIP image, raised the contrast a little and added labels placed from county and Census coordinates.</div><div role="listitem">The salt marsh behind Garden City Beach from above, with private docks running out from the houses on both edges. Photo: USDA Farm Service Agency, NAIP, <a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://imagery.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSNAIPImagery/ImageServer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The National Map (USGS)</a>. Chapter3 Realty cut this view from the NAIP service and added labels placed from map data.</div><div role="listitem">Houses on the Cherry Grove channels from above, many with a dock behind them, and the marsh and the city's boat ramp beyond. Photo: USDA Farm Service Agency, NAIP, <a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://imagery.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSNAIPImagery/ImageServer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">USGS The National Map</a>. Chapter3 Realty exported this view from the NAIP image service, raised the contrast a little and added labels placed from OpenStreetMap, Horry County and Census coordinates.</div><div role="listitem">The dark water of the Waccamaw River at Wacca Wache Landing, with a marina basin beside it, on the Georgetown County side. Photo: USDA Farm Service Agency, NAIP, <a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://imagery.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSNAIPImagery/ImageServer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The National Map (USGS)</a>. Chapter3 Realty cut this view from the NAIP service and added labels placed from map data.</div><div role="listitem">Plantation Lakes from above: houses along the lake, and the boat docks at the owners’ amenity area. Photo: USDA Farm Service Agency, NAIP, <a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://imagery.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSNAIPImagery/ImageServer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The National Map (USGS)</a>. Chapter3 Realty cut this view from the NAIP service, adjusted the colors and added labels placed from county data.</div><div role="listitem">The old Socastee swing bridge, the blue steel span, on the Intracoastal Waterway, with the newer high bridge at the lower left. Photo: USDA Farm Service Agency, NAIP, <a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://imagery.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSNAIPImagery/ImageServer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The National Map (USGS)</a>.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them. Each photo keeps the license listed with it.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/ima