-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/master-insurance-ho6/#article","headline":"Condo master insurance vs HO-6: where the line falls","description":"Two policies cover a condo. Where the association's coverage stops, who gets billed for the deductible, and what to read before you buy.","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/master-insurance-ho6/","author":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/about/#timmy-nash"},"reviewedBy":{"@type":"Person","name":"Devin Day","jobTitle":"Operations Officer"},"publisher":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"datePublished":"2026-07-29","dateModified":"2026-10-28","inLanguage":"en-US","image":"https://chapter3realty.com/og-image.jpg","mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/master-insurance-ho6/#webpage"},"about":{"@type":"Thing","name":"Condo master insurance vs HO-6: where the line falls"},"keywords":"condo master insurance vs HO-6"}</script>
-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/hoa/master-insurance-ho6/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between condo master insurance and an HO-6?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The association buys the master policy for the building and your dues pay for it. You buy the HO-6 for your own unit. The building's recorded papers say where one policy stops and the other starts, and every building sets that line differently."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does the condo master policy cover the inside of my unit?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sometimes. Some master policies cover the finishes that came with the unit. Others stop at the bare walls and cover nothing inside them. Read the insurance section of the recorded papers next to the association's policy. Those two documents together give you the answer."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who pays the master policy deductible in a South Carolina condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The association pays the insurer, then bills the owners for it as a shared cost. Your share is usually the same share you pay of the regular dues. An unpaid amount becomes a lien on your unit, so the bill does not go away."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need an HO-6 to get a mortgage on a Myrtle Beach condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually yes. Your lender will require one if the association's policy leaves any part of your unit uncovered, or if it charges a deductible for each unit. The lender checks the amount too. A policy sized for furniture alone can fail that check."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much loss assessment coverage should I have on my HO-6?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"There is no legal minimum, so the number printed on your policy is the number you have. Compare it to the deductibles on the association's policy. If it is smaller, ask your insurance agent what it costs to raise it."}}]}</script>
