 cover the inside of my unit, how much HO-6 coverage do I need for my mortgage, loss assessment coverage, condo storm deductible South Carolina, RCBAP, condo flood insurance Horry County"}</script>
+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need an HO-6 if the HOA has a master policy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"For most conventional loans, yes, when the master policy leaves out any part of the inside of your unit or charges a deductible to each unit. Your belongings are yours to insure under every kind of master policy."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is HO-6 insurance required in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not by state law. South Carolina's condo law requires the association to insure the building and lets each owner insure their own unit. A lender usually requires an HO-6."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much is HO-6 insurance in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Owners in Horry County pay about $800 a year for their own condo policy. A flood policy on the unit, if you buy one, cost a middle of about $495 a year."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How much loss assessment coverage should I have?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Enough to pay your share of the building's storm deductible. There is no legal minimum, so the number printed on your policy is the number you have. In a typical Horry building of 10 or more units, each owner's even share of a 5 percent storm deductible is about $6,500."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does my HO-6 cover flood?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Most likely not. Most homeowners insurance does not cover flood damage, and the building's flood policy does not insure your belongings. A flood policy on your unit can, and here it cost a middle of about $495 a year."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is an RCBAP?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Residential Condominium Building Association Policy, the federal flood policy a condo association buys on its building. It does not insure owners' furniture or belongings."}}]}</script>
