+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the South Carolina Homeowners Association Act?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The HOA Act is a 2018 state law, SC Code Title 27, Chapter 30. It requires an HOA's papers to be recorded at the county to be enforced, and gives owners the budget and owner list. It lets magistrate court hear money disputes under the Act, and requires Consumer Affairs to record complaints. It has been changed once, in 2019."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who regulates HOAs in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No state agency enforces South Carolina's HOA Act. Consumer Affairs records complaints and sends each one to the HOA, and the law bars it from acting as a referee. An HOA that is a nonprofit files its papers with the Secretary of State. The rest is in each community's recorded papers."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I complain about my HOA to the state?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. File with SC Consumer Affairs online or by mail, with the HOA Supplemental Questionnaire. The agency sends the complaint to the HOA, but its process is voluntary mediation. Of 416 Horry County complaints from 2022 to 2025, 10 ended with the owner satisfied."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do HOA documents have to be recorded in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Under the HOA Act, an HOA's governing papers must be recorded at the county's register of deeds to be enforceable. A new rule stays enforceable only if it is recorded by January 10 of the year after it was adopted."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a seller have to disclose an HOA in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, unless the sale is exempt. The seller fills in the state's disclosure form before the contract is signed. If the home is in an association, the form includes a one-page HOA addendum."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I sue my HOA in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A money dispute under the HOA Act of $7,500 or less can go to magistrate court. For anything larger, or any other dispute, talk to a South Carolina attorney. The SC Bar's lawyer referral service is at 800-868-2284."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is condo law different from HOA law in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. A condo follows the HOA Act and also the Horizontal Property Act, the state's condo law. The condo law puts a lien on a unit for unpaid assessments and opens the condo's account book to owners. It also requires a condo's founding papers to state any limits on leasing."}}]}</script>
