.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:820px}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A special assessment is a bill the association sends every owner, once, for a cost its budget and its reserve fund cannot pay. Dues pay the yearly budget. The <a href="/hoa/reserves/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">reserve fund</a> is money the association saves for big repairs, such as a roof. A special assessment pays the rest.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When a part of a condo building the association must insure is damaged, the repair is paid in this order.</p><ol class="sa-flow" aria-label="Who pays to repair damage to an insured part of a South Carolina condo building, in order"><li><span class="sa-n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><b>The building's insurance</b><span class="t">The association must insure the building.</span></li><li><span class="sa-n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><b>The reserve fund</b><span class="t">The money the association has saved for big repairs.</span></li><li class="sa-you"><span class="sa-n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><b>The owners</b><span class="t">The rest, divided among the owners. This can be a special assessment.</span></li></ol><p class="sa-cap">Who pays to repair damage to an insured part of a South Carolina condo building, in order. A building's bylaws can split the owners' part another way. The board can bill that part as a special assessment, or add it to the yearly budget.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In a neighborhood of houses, the recorded rules of the homeowners association (HOA) say whether it can charge one. Our page on <a href="/hoa/south-carolina-hoa-laws/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">South Carolina's HOA laws</a> covers both kinds.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="how-much"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How much">How much is a condo special assessment?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The size depends on the repair, what the reserve fund holds, and your unit's percentage. At one Pawleys Island condo, the board proposed about $65,000 to $88,000 a unit. In our example below, it is $5,850 to $10,350.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What did one Pawleys Island condo's board ask each owner to pay?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">At Shipyard Village, a condo in Pawleys Island, south of Myrtle Beach, the board said two of its buildings needed extensive repairs. It said every unit's windows and sliding doors had to be replaced, at $12 million to $13 million. A consultant found corroded reinforcing steel and an outer shell that was not weather tight.</p><div class="sa-case sa-card"><p class="k">Shipyard Village, Pawleys Island</p><p class="big">About $88,000</p><p class="sub">The special assessment its board proposed in 2009 for each unit in two of its buildings.</p><div class="row"><span>The owners voted it down. The board then put the repair cost into the next two yearly budgets instead.</span></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Owners sued the association, and the South Carolina Supreme Court ruled on the case, Fisher v. Shipyard Village. For a buyer, this means a no vote can still lead to a bigger yearly budget, so the budget is on our list too.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How is each owner's share of a special assessment worked out?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong>Example, with our numbers:</strong> a 120-unit building needs a $1.2 million roof and has $300,000 saved for it. The $900,000 still needed is split by each unit's percentage.</p><div class="sa-ex"><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Unit</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Bill</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong style="color:var(--navy)"><span style="white-space:nowrap">One‑bedroom</span></strong><br>60 units at 0.65%</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">$5,850</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong style="color:var(--navy)"><span style="white-space:nowrap">Two‑bedroom</span></strong><br>40 units at 0.95%</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">$8,550</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong style="color:var(--navy)"><span style="white-space:nowrap">Three‑bedroom</span></strong><br>20 units at 1.15%</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">$10,350</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong style="color:var(--navy)"><span style="white-space:nowrap">All 120 units</span></strong><br>100% together</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">$900,000</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The average is $7,500 a unit. Each unit's percentage is based on its value against the whole property when the condo was created. A unit with a higher percentage pays more of every assessment.</p><div class="sa-calc"><p class="k">Work out your share</p><div class="sa-in"><label><span>What the repair costs</span><span class="u"><span class="s" aria-hidden="true">$</span><input id="saCost" type="text" size="1" value="1,200,000" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" oninput="c3Sa()" onblur="c3SaF(this)" aria-label="What the repair costs"><span class="s" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></label><label><span>What insurance and the reserve fund pay</span><span class="u"><span class="s" aria-hidden="true">$</span><input id="saSaved" type="text" size="1" value="300,000" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" oninput="c3Sa()" onblur="c3SaF(this)" aria-label="What insurance and the reserve fund pay"><span class="s" aria-hidden="true"></span></span></label><label><span>Your unit's percentage</span><span class="u"><span class="s" aria-hidden="true"></span><input id="saPct" type="text" size="1" value="0.95" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" oninput="c3Sa()" onblur="c3SaF(this)" aria-label="Your unit's percentage"><span class="s" aria-hidden="true">%</span></span></label></div><div class="out"><span>Your share</span><strong id="saShare">$8,550</strong></div><p class="note" id="saNote">The owners together owe $900,000.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">What would your share be?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the unit you are looking at. We find its percentage in the founding papers, so you can work out your share of any assessment.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us find your unit's percentage</a></div></div><script>function c3SaN(t){var v=parseFloat(String(t).replace(/[^0-9.]/g,""));return isFinite(v)&&v>0?v:0}function c3SaF(e){if(e&&e.id!=="saPct"&&e.value.trim()!==""){e.value=Math.round(c3SaN(e.value)).toLocaleString("en-US")}}function c3Sa(){var g=function(id){var e=document.getElementById(id);return e?c3SaN(e.value):0};var m=function(n){return "$"+Math.round(n).toLocaleString("en-US")};var s=document.getElementById("saShare"),t=document.getElementById("saNote");if(!s||!t)return;var rest=Math.max(0,g("saCost")-g("saSaved")),p=g("saPct");s.textContent=m(rest*p/100);t.textContent=rest>0?"The owners together owe "+m(rest)+".":"Insurance and the reserve fund pay all of it, so no special assessment is needed.";}document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",c3Sa);</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="building-age"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Building age">How old are the condo buildings for sale on the Grand Strand?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most are more than 25 years old. About two in three condos for sale here are in buildings built before 2000.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Most condos for sale here are in buildings built before 2000"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Most condos for sale here are in buildings built before 2000</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Year built</span><span role="columnheader">share of condos for sale</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built before 1980</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.324" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">11%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built in the 1980s</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">34%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built in the 1990s</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.559" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">19%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built in the 2000s</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.912" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">31%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built in the 2010s</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.059" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">2%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Built in 2020 or later</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.059" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">2%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Our count of the condos for sale in the local listing service (MLS), by the year built each listing gives. A building's parts may have been replaced since.</figcaption></figure><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How long do a condo building's roof, elevator and windows last?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) publishes how long building parts last when it plans repairs.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="How many years HUD plans for each building part to last"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">How many years HUD plans for each building part to last</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Building part</span><span role="columnheader">years</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Flat roof</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.3" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">15 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Shingle roof</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.4" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">20 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Elevator machinery</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.4" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">20 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Aluminum windows</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.7" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">35 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Concrete or steel balconies</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.8" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">40 years</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Metal railings</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">50 years</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>HUD's estimated useful life table for family housing.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A building built before 1990 is older than HUD expects a flat or shingle roof, elevator machinery or aluminum windows to last. That does not mean those parts were never replaced. We find out which ones were from the minutes and the reserve study, if there is one. A reserve study is a list of the building's big parts, with when each needs replacing and what it will cost.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="votes-and-limits"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Votes and limits">Can a condo HOA charge a special assessment without a vote in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, if the building's bylaws allow it. Under South Carolina's condo law, each building's recorded bylaws say how owners are billed. Any limit on the amount is in those bylaws and the founding papers.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does South Carolina require a reserve study or regular structural inspections?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. The state's condo and HOA laws require neither.</p><ul class="sa-law" aria-label="What South Carolina's condo and HOA laws do not require"><li><span class="m" aria-hidden="true">&#10005;</span><span><b>No reserve study</b>required</span></li><li><span class="m" aria-hidden="true">&#10005;</span><span><b>No periodic structural inspection</b>required</span></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the Shipyard Village case, the South Carolina Supreme Court said a court will not overrule a board that acts within its powers and in good faith. A court can rule against a board that breaks its own founding papers or bylaws.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do owners in Horry County dispute special assessments?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. More of South Carolina's HOA complaints are about associations in Horry County than in any other county. Associations there are also the subject of about 2 in 5 of the complaints over fees or special assessments. For a buyer, that is a reason to see how a building's last assessment was voted. That vote is in the minutes we read.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Can this board bill you without a vote?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the building. We read its bylaws and tell you whether the board can charge an assessment without a vote, and any limit on the amount.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us read the bylaws</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="how-we-spot-one"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How to spot one">How can I tell if a condo has a special assessment coming?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We read the building's papers, the same ones Fannie Mae, which buys home loans from lenders, tells a lender to read. The right to see them belongs to owners, so we ask the seller for them before you make an offer.</p><ol style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Board minutes.</strong> An association that is a nonprofit corporation must keep minutes of every board meeting, so a repair being priced shows up there.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The association's list of special assessments and needed repairs.</strong> It shows what is billed now and what repairs are planned.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Any engineer's or inspection report.</strong> A lender must read a structural or mechanical inspection done in the last 3 years, and so do we.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The reserve study, if there is one, against the building's age.</strong> We compare what the fund holds with the parts HUD expects to be worn out. If a building has none, the minutes and the budget carry more weight. Our <a href="/hoa/reserves/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">reserves page</a> shows how much a building should have saved.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The budget and the record of every expense.</strong> A condo board must keep a detailed record of every receipt and expense, open to all owners.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The seller's disclosure.</strong> It asks: "Has the owners association levied any special assessments or similar charges?" It also asks about "any known common area problems".</li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In most resales the seller fills in that disclosure form before a contract. The answer can be yes, no or No Representation, which means the seller does not know.</p><div class="sa-words"><p>Words in the minutes we look for</p><ul><li>special assessment</li><li>engineer's report</li><li>structural inspection</li><li>water intrusion</li><li>balconies</li><li>waterproofing</li><li>necessary repairs</li><li>added to the operating budget</li></ul></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What if the papers come after I sign?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Then we read them inside the contract's due diligence period. The standard South Carolina sales contract has one, for a length you and the seller agree on. Inside it, you can end the contract for any reason, with written notice and the fee the contract names. After it ends, that right ends.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="/hoa/documents/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our page on HOA documents</a> explains each paper a buyer gets.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="who-pays-at-sale"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Who pays at sale">Who pays a special assessment when a condo sells?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The seller pays what was billed before closing. It comes out of the sale price.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If the seller does not pay, the association can collect from the buyer too, for everything owed up to closing. The buyer owes no more of the seller's bill than the association's written statement shows. If it shows nothing owed, the buyer owes none of it. We get that statement and find out what the seller owes before closing, so you do not get stuck with it. <a href="/hoa/estoppel-and-transfer-fees/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our page on that statement and its fee</a> says what it costs.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>Where the assessment stands at closing, and who pays</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Where it stands at closing</th><th scope="col">Who pays</th><th scope="col">What we do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Billed before closing, not yet paid</th><td data-label="Who pays">The seller, from the sale price</td><td data-label="What we do">We get the association's statement</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Voted before closing, billed after</th><td data-label="Who pays">You, unless the offer says otherwise</td><td data-label="What we do">We write who pays into the offer</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Talked about, not yet voted</th><td data-label="Who pays">Usually you, the owner when it is billed</td><td data-label="What we do">We look for it in the minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When owners vote for one before closing and the bill comes after, the association bills you, as the owner then, unless the offer says the seller pays it. The offer can say the same for installments due after closing.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If the minutes show one coming, we tell you. Before you offer, you can offer less, ask the seller for a credit at closing, or not make an offer. The seller's disclosure form asks only about assessments already levied, so the minutes are where a coming one shows up.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Selling a condo with an assessment pending? See <a href="/sell/sell-my-condo/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">selling a condo in Myrtle Beach</a>.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Leave the seller's bill with the seller</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We get the association's statement of what the seller owes, and we write who pays a voted assessment into your offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us write it into your offer</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="loans"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Loans">Can a special assessment stop a condo loan?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. If a building's special assessment pays for a critical repair that has not been fixed, Fannie Mae will not buy a loan on any unit in that building. For a buyer, that rules out a regular loan your lender plans to sell to Fannie Mae, until the repair is done. Cash still works. FHA, VA and other loans have their own rules, and our page on <a href="/invest/non-warrantable-condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">condos most lenders will not lend on</a> explains them.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Which repairs does Fannie Mae count as critical?</h3><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Repairs to items such as sea walls, elevators, waterproofing, stairwells, balconies, foundations, electrical systems and parking structures.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Mold, water intrusion and damaging leaks in the buildings.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">A repair over $10,000 per unit, due within 12 months, with no money set aside for it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">A full or partial evacuation order for an unsafe condition. The building is ineligible until it is safe.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Damage to only one or a few units does not count.</p><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-10-d/hoa-special-assessments/north-myrtle-beach-oceanfront-roofs-and-balconies-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-10-d/hoa-special-assessments/north-myrtle-beach-oceanfront-roofs-and-balconies-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-10-d/hoa-special-assessments/north-myrtle-beach-oceanfront-roofs-and-balconies-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="525" alt="Two oceanfront mid-rise buildings in North Myrtle Beach with stacked balconies and railings, a flat roof with rooftop equipment and a pool behind the dunes" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: U.S. Geological Survey. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: U.S. Geological Survey</a></span></div><figcaption>Stacked balconies with railings on oceanfront buildings in North Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane. Balconies and waterproofing are on Fannie Mae's list.</figcaption></figure></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Buying with a loan? Know the repair list first.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the building and your loan. Before you offer, we check its papers for a repair on Fannie Mae's critical list.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us check it for your loan</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="not-paying"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Not paying">Can I refuse to pay a special assessment, or pay it over time?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No owner can refuse a lawful share. Not using the shared areas, or giving up the unit, does not end it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No South Carolina statute gives an owner a right to a payment plan. Whether one is offered is the board's choice.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What happens if a special assessment goes unpaid in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">An unpaid condo assessment is a lien on the unit. If the unit is sold to pay its debts, property taxes and the mortgage are paid first, then the association. The association can foreclose its lien in court like a mortgage, or sue for the money without foreclosing. Our page on <a href="/hoa/liens-and-foreclosure/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">HOA liens and foreclosure</a> has each step.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A neighbor's unpaid share can become every owner's cost. When a lender forecloses on a unit, some of that unit's unpaid assessments are split among all the owners.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Association assessments are not deductible as real estate tax, because an association, not a government, charges them. Our page on whether <a href="/hoa/tax-deductible/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">HOA fees are tax deductible</a> has the detail.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="special-assessment-questions"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Special assessment questions</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can the board change my unit's percentage to raise my share?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. Your unit's percentage is set in the condo's founding papers. It cannot be changed without every owner's agreement, except as a phased condo's founding papers provide or when owners vote not to rebuild a unit.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much say does one owner have in a vote on a special assessment?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">As much as the unit's percentage. Where owners vote, a majority means owners holding at least 51 percent of the building's value, not 51 percent of the units.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can a condo board borrow money instead of charging a special assessment?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, if the association is a nonprofit corporation and its articles do not forbid it. Under South Carolina law, it may borrow money and pledge its income to repay the loan.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does a special assessment matter if I pay cash?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. You owe your share like every other owner. Fannie Mae's loan rules do not stop a cash purchase, but they apply to a later buyer of your unit who needs a loan.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-31</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c030.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-30</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-50</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t33c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 33-31</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/opinions/HTMLFiles/SC/27603.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fisher v. Shipyard Village</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-03</a>, <a href="https://www.llr.sc.gov/re/recpdf/Updated_Property_Disclosure_Form.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC disclosure form</a>, <a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/documents/eul_for_cna_e_tool.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD useful lives</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Pub. 527</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p530" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Pub. 530</a>, <a href="https://consumer.sc.gov/sites/consumer/files/Documents/HOA/2026_HOA_Complaint_Report.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SCDCA HOA report</a>. Condos for sale: our count of active listings by year built. Not legal or tax advice; a South Carolina attorney can advise on your documents.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Stacked balconies with railings on oceanfront buildings in North Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>. Chapter3 Realty cut 1600 by 700 pixels around the two buildings in the center left of the 3008 by 1960 original, and resized it for the page. The 1x1, 4x3 and 16x9 share crops are cut from the center of the full frame.</div><div role="listitem"></div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-10-d/hoa-special-assessments/north-myrtle-beach-oceanfront-roofs-and-balconies-2011-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-10-d/hoa-special-assessments/north-myrtle-beach-oceanfront-roofs-and-balconies-2011-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":525,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Stacked balconies with railings on oceanfront buildings in North Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane","caption":"Stacked balconies with railings on oceanfront buildings in North Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane. Balconies and waterproofing are on Fannie Mae's list.","description":"Two oceanfront mid-rise buildings in North Myrtle Beach with stacked balconies and railings, a flat roof with rooftop equipment and a pool behind the dunes","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"U.S. Geological Survey"},"creditText":"U.S","copyrightNotice":"Public domain (U.S. Geological Survey)","license":"https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","isBasedOn":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"Windy Hill section, oceanfront, North Myrtle Beach, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Want to know what a building's papers say?</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Tell us the condo you like. One of our agents reads its minutes, its list of special assessments and its reserve study, if it has one. Then we tell you what they show, before you make an offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have an agent read the minutes</a></div></section></main>
