-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Distribution</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Top 10% of Myrtle Beach Airbnbs gross $6,043+ a month; the median does $2,115</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Monthly revenue percentiles for the Myrtle Beach market, per AirROI: the top 10 percent of listings gross $6,043 or more, the top quartile $3,803 or more, the median $2,115, and the bottom quartile $1,034 or less. The top quartile out-earns the bottom by roughly 3.7 times. Occupancy shows the same spread: the top decile runs 73 percent or better against a 32 percent median.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Averages are not useless. The property and the operation set your percentile. Bedroom count, walkability, the building's rental rules, pricing discipline, and the management split move a unit between those rows. That screening is what our <a href="/invest/str-tools/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">STR analysis</a> is for.</p></div></section>
