-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Market size</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">The Myrtle Beach vacation rental market in public records</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The demand base: 18.2 million visitors spent $13.3 billion on the Grand Strand in 2025. Horry County generated $31.2 million in state accommodations tax in 2024, roughly 30 percent of the entire state's collections, and the county's hospitality fee brought in another $59.3 million. This is the largest lodging market in South Carolina by a wide margin.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">The rules differ by jurisdiction. In January 2025 the City of Myrtle Beach passed an ordinance protecting a 114-block oceanside district's short-term rental stock from long-term conversion. A study had valued 1,000 converted units at $2.48 million a year in lost city revenue. Which cities allow rentals, which license them, and which tax them is covered in <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">short-term rental rules by city</a>.</p></div></section>
