-<main id="main"><div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/invest/">Invest</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Canadian buyers</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">Buying from Canada</p><h1 class="detail-h1">How does a Canadian buy a rental in Myrtle Beach?<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">The loan, the tax number, the two withholdings.</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated September 16, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">A Canadian can buy a Myrtle Beach rental with cash or a foreign-national loan. The rent needs a U.S. tax number. The sale has two withholdings.</p><div style="margin-top:1.8rem"><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Ask about buying from Canada</a></div></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A Canadian can own a house in South Carolina with no residency and no visa. The buying part is the same as it is for anyone.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most Canadian investors here pay cash. There are mortgage options here in America, including options at our preferred lender. A Canadian living in Canada uses a foreign-national loan, which is qualified on the rent the house brings in.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You will need a U.S. tax number. Without one, whoever collects your rent must send 30 percent of it to the government. One form stops that. Then you pay tax on your profit instead of on all the rent. When you sell, money is held back again, and you claim the extra back on a return. Canada taxes the same money and subtracts what you already paid here.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each of those steps has a section below, in the order you will meet them.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Can a Canadian buy a house in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Nothing in South Carolina requires a buyer to be a citizen or a resident. <a href="https://www.nar.realtor/newsroom/foreign-buyers-purchased-45-3-billion-worth-of-u-s-homes-from-april-25-to-march-26" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Canada is the top country of origin for foreign buyers of U.S. homes</a>, and about half of foreign buyers pay cash. <a href="https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/can-am-days/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Myrtle Beach has held Can-Am Days for Ontario families every March since 1961</a>. <a href="https://www.visitmyrtlebeach.com/plan/international-travelers/canadian-travelers" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The area's visitor bureau calls Myrtle Beach the top beach in America for Canadian visitors</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Every closing in South Carolina is run by a closing attorney. You can sign in person or by power of attorney from Canada. What you need before you offer:</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">What</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A passport</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Identity for the attorney, the lender if any, and the utilities.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A U.S. bank account</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Two Canadian banks open U.S. accounts from Canada with a Canadian ID. The closing attorney gives wiring instructions for the funds.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A U.S. tax number, or a plan to get one</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Needed to file the return on the rent, to stop the 30 percent withholding, and to sell. Not needed to buy.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">An accountant on each side of the border</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The rent and the sale are taxed in both countries. Canada credits the U.S. tax, but only on a return that claims it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">How does a Canadian pay for a Myrtle Beach rental?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Three ways.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Way</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Who it is for</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">What it needs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Cash</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Most Canadian investors, in Chapter3's files and in the national data.</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Funds wired to the closing attorney. No U.S. credit, no tax number, no loan file.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A foreign-national loan</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A Canadian who lives in Canada and wants a loan on a rental.</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A passport, foreign bank statements and reserves. No U.S. credit score. Some programs ask for a U.S. tax number, most do not. The loan is qualified on the property's rent, and it is for rentals only.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A standard U.S. loan</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A Canadian who lives in the U.S. with work authorization and a Social Security number or a U.S. tax number.</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The same loan as a U.S. buyer. Not open to a Canadian living in Canada.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-2-02/non-us-citizen-borrower-eligibility-requirements" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The standard loan rules cover non-citizens who are residents of the United States</a>. A Canadian in Canada is outside that sentence, so the loan that fits is <a href="/invest/strategies/dscr-loans/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">one qualified on the rent</a>. <a href="https://www.americamortgages.com/ultimate-dscr-loan-faq-foreign-nationals-expats/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">One foreign-national lender's public list</a>: a passport, six to twelve months of foreign bank statements, and no U.S. credit required. <a href="https://www.hud.gov/news/hud-no-25-048" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHA loans are closed to non-permanent residents</a>, so a work permit opens a standard loan but not an FHA one.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In Chapter3's experience the cash buyer closes fastest. A few of our Canadian investors have closed with a U.S. loan. If you want one, tell us early, because the loan file takes longer from outside the country.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Buying a rental from Canada?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the town and the budget. We tour by video, read the association documents, and put you with the closing attorney and, if you want a loan, a loan officer at our preferred lender.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Ask about buying from Canada</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">What is the U.S. tax number, and when does a Canadian owner need it?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/individual-taxpayer-identification-number" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">It is a nine-digit number for people who are not eligible for a Social Security number</a>. You apply with Form W-7, by mail or in person, and you attach the tax return it is for unless an exception applies. A landlord receiving rent and a seller of U.S. property both qualify for an exception. Allow seven weeks, and nine to eleven from overseas or in tax season.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You do not need it to buy. You need it three times after that. It goes on the U.S. return for the rent. It goes on the form that stops the 30 percent withholding, which you give to whoever collects your rent. It goes on the withholding certificate application when you sell. It expires if it is not used on a return for three years. Apply the year you buy, not the week you sell.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">How is the rent taxed for a Canadian owner?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two ways, and you choose.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase"></th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">The default</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">The election</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">What is taxed</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The gross rent, with no deductions</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The net rent, after every expense the property has</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The rate</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A flat 30 percent</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The normal U.S. rates on the net</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Who takes it</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p515" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Whoever collects the rent withholds it</a>: a manager</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Nobody. You file Form 1040-NR and pay what the return shows</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">How do you make the election? You sign <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/iw8eci" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Form W-8ECI</a> and give it to whoever collects your rent, usually the property manager. It does not go to the government. The form carries your U.S. tax number, so get the number first. The manager keeps it on file and stops withholding. You then file a U.S. return each year and pay on the profit. The form runs out at the end of the third year after you sign it, so you send a fresh one.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p519" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The election treats the rent as business income</a>, so the mortgage interest, the taxes, the insurance, the manager and the depreciation all count. Almost every owner makes it. Without the form, the person collecting your rent must withhold 30 percent of every payment and is personally liable if they do not. Managers here will ask for the form before the first tenant pays.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The U.S. return is due June 15. Canada taxes the same rent as world income and <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/international-non-residents/canadian-residents-going-down-south.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">credits the U.S. tax you paid</a>. <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/international-non-residents/information-been-moved/foreign-reporting/foreign-income-verification-statement.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">A rental costing more than C$100,000 also goes on Form T1135</a> each year; a vacation home used mostly by your family may not. The treaty does not exempt the rent. It lets the U.S. tax it first and makes Canada credit that tax.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">What happens when a Canadian sells a Myrtle Beach rental?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two withholdings at the closing table, then two returns. Both withholdings are deposits against the tax, not the tax itself. The excess comes back through the returns.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Withholding</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">How much</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">On what</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Who takes it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Federal</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/firpta-withholding" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">15 percent</a></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The sale price, not the gain</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The buyer, through the closing attorney</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">South Carolina</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c008.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The state's top income tax rate</a></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The gain, if you sign an affidavit stating it. The sale price if you do not</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The buyer, remitted on Form I-290 by the fifteenth of the next month</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The federal residence exception, for sales at $300,000 or less, needs a buyer who will live in the house. A buyer who will rent it cannot give that, so a rental sold to another investor gets the full 15 percent. <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/withholding-certificates" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">A withholding certificate can cut it to the tax that is due</a>. The application, Form 8288-B, needs the tax numbers of everyone in the deal and takes about 90 days, so it is filed before the closing, not after.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The state affidavit needs your U.S. tax number too. Without it, the state share is taken from the whole price. Then Canada taxes the gain as world income and credits the U.S. tax. <a href="/sell/rental-property/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">How selling a rental works here</a>, and <a href="/sell/capital-gains/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the capital gains rules</a> that apply to every seller.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Selling a Myrtle Beach rental from Canada?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the address. We run the sale price, both withholdings and the closing costs before you list, and we start the certificate early.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/sell/net-proceeds/">Have us run your net proceeds</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">How long can a Canadian stay, and when does U.S. tax residency start?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://travel.gc.ca/destinations/united-states" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Six months without a visa</a>, declared at the border. <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/alienregistration" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">A stay of 30 days or longer must be registered</a> with the U.S. government, and a Canadian who drove in without an entry record does that online.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Tax residency is a separate count. <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/substantial-presence-test" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The substantial presence test</a> adds every day this year, a third of last year's days, and a sixth of the year before. At 183 you are a U.S. tax resident. About four months every year, every year, is enough to reach it. A snowbird who reaches it but keeps a home and a closer connection to Canada <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8840" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">files Form 8840 by the return deadline</a> to stay a nonresident. Miss the form and the exception is lost for that year. Canada keeps taxing you as a resident the whole time.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">What is different for a Canadian owner after closing?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two things. The rest of owning a rental here is the same for you as for anyone, and <a href="/invest/property-management/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the pages on running a rental</a> cover it.</p><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The utilities want a deposit.</strong> <a href="https://www.santeecooper.com/residential/start-move-stop-service/start-service/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The power company runs a credit check to size it</a> and takes a passport number as identification. The check finds nothing. Credit files do not cross the border. Equifax and TransUnion run separate databases in each country and do not share them. A thirty-year Canadian credit record cannot be seen here. No file means the largest deposit. <a href="https://www.santeecooper.com/residential/ways-to-pay/pay-as-you-go/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Prepaid service skips the credit check and the deposit</a> for an upfront amount instead.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The estate is taxed here too.</strong> <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/some-nonresidents-with-us-assets-must-file-estate-tax-returns" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. real estate is a U.S. asset for estate tax</a>, and a nonresident's estate files when its U.S. assets pass $60,000. A house here clears that on its own. <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-trty/canada.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The treaty gives a Canadian resident's estate a credit pro-rated to the U.S. share of the whole estate</a>. The return is due nine months after death. Put the title together with an accountant on each side before you buy, not after.</li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Canadian buyer FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can a Canadian get a mortgage on a Myrtle Beach rental?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A standard U.S. loan is for people who live in the U.S. with a Social Security number or a U.S. tax number. A Canadian living in Canada uses a foreign-national loan qualified on the rent, with a passport and foreign bank statements and no U.S. credit. Most Canadian investors here pay cash.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do Canadians pay U.S. tax on Myrtle Beach rent?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. The default is a flat 30 percent of the gross rent, withheld by whoever collects it. Almost every owner instead elects to be taxed on the net rent, files Form 1040-NR, and gives the rent collector Form W-8ECI. Canada taxes the same rent and credits the U.S. tax.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much is withheld when a Canadian sells a house in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two withholdings. The federal one is 15 percent of the sale price, taken by the buyer. The state one is the state's top income tax rate on the gain, or on the price without a seller's affidavit. Both are deposits; the excess comes back through the returns.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do I need an ITIN to buy a house in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. You need it to file the U.S. return on the rent, to stop the 30 percent withholding, and to get a withholding certificate when you sell. Apply the year you buy. It takes seven to eleven weeks.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How long can a Canadian stay in Myrtle Beach each year?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Six months without a visa, declared at the border, with online registration for a stay of 30 days or more. About four months every year can make you a U.S. tax resident under the substantial presence test unless you file Form 8840 by the return deadline.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-2-02/non-us-citizen-borrower-eligibility-requirements" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Selling Guide B2-2-02</a>, <a href="https://www.hud.gov/news/hud-no-25-048" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD 25-048</a>, <a href="https://www.americamortgages.com/ultimate-dscr-loan-faq-foreign-nationals-expats/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">a foreign-national lender's FAQ</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/individual-taxpayer-identification-number" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS, ITIN</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p519" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Pub 519</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/iw8eci" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Form W-8ECI</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/firpta-withholding" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS, FIRPTA</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/withholding-certificates" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS, withholding certificates</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/some-nonresidents-with-us-assets-must-file-estate-tax-returns" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS, nonresident estates</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/substantial-presence-test" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS, substantial presence</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8840" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Form 8840</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-trty/canada.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S.-Canada treaty</a>, <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/international-non-residents/information-been-moved/foreign-reporting/foreign-income-verification-statement.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CRA, T1135</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c008.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-8-580</a>, <a href="https://dor.sc.gov/forms-site/Forms/I290.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Form I-290</a>, <a href="https://www.santeecooper.com/residential/start-move-stop-service/start-service/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Santee Cooper</a>, <a href="https://travel.gc.ca/destinations/united-states" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Canada travel advice</a>, <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/alienregistration" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">USCIS registration</a>, <a href="https://www.nar.realtor/newsroom/foreign-buyers-purchased-45-3-billion-worth-of-u-s-homes-from-april-25-to-march-26" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAR 2026</a>. Read September 16, 2026. Educational only, not tax, legal or loan advice. Chapter3 is a real estate brokerage, not a lender.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Buying in Myrtle Beach from Canada?</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">One call. We tour by video, read the documents, and line up the attorney and the accountant before you offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="tel:+18543332135">Call a specialized agent</a></div></section></main>
+<main id="main"><div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/invest/">Invest</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Canadian buyers</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">Buying from Canada</p><h1 class="detail-h1">How does a Canadian buy a rental in Myrtle Beach?<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">The loan, the tax number, the two withholdings.</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 12, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">A Canadian can buy a Myrtle Beach rental with cash or a foreign-national loan. The rent needs a U.S. tax number. The sale has two withholdings.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A Canadian can own a house in South Carolina with no residency and no visa. The buying part is the same as it is for anyone.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most Canadian investors here pay cash. There are mortgage options here in America, including options at our preferred lender. A Canadian living in Canada uses a foreign-national loan, which is qualified on the rent the house brings in.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You will need a U.S. tax number. Without one, whoever collects your rent must send 30 percent of it to the government. One form stops that. Then you pay tax on your profit instead of on all the rent. When you sell, money is held back again, and you claim the extra back on a return. Canada taxes the same money and subtracts what you already paid here.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each of those steps has a section below, in the order you will meet them.</p><div style="margin-top:1.4rem"><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Ask about buying from Canada</a></div><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:2rem;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#can-a-canadian-buy-a-house-in-myrtle-beach" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Can a Canadian buy a house in Myrtle Beach?</a></li><li><a href="#how-does-a-canadian-pay-for-a-myrtle-beach-rental" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How does a Canadian pay for a Myrtle Beach rental?</a></li><li><a href="#what-is-the-u-s-tax-number-and-when-does-a-canadian-owner" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What is the U.S. tax number, and when does a Canadian owner need it?</a></li><li><a href="#how-is-the-rent-taxed-for-a-canadian-owner" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How is the rent taxed for a Canadian owner?</a></li><li><a href="#what-happens-when-a-canadian-sells-a-myrtle-beach-rental" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What happens when a Canadian sells a Myrtle Beach rental?</a></li><li><a href="#how-long-can-a-canadian-stay-and-when-does-u-s-tax-residency" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How long can a Canadian stay, and when does U.S. tax residency start?</a></li><li><a href="#what-is-different-for-a-canadian-owner-after-closing" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What is different for a Canadian owner after closing?</a></li><li><a href="#canadian-buyer-faq" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Canadian buyer FAQ</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="can-a-canadian-buy-a-house-in-myrtle-beach"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Can a Canadian buy a house in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Nothing in South Carolina requires a buyer to be a citizen or a resident. <a href="https://www.nar.realtor/newsroom/foreign-buyers-purchased-45-3-billion-worth-of-u-s-homes-from-april-25-to-march-26" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Canada is the top country of origin for foreign buyers of U.S. homes</a>, and about half of foreign buyers pay cash. <a href="https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/can-am-days/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Myrtle Beach has held Can-Am Days for Ontario families every March since 1961</a>. <a href="https://www.visitmyrtlebeach.com/plan/international-travelers/canadian-travelers" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The area's visitor bureau calls Myrtle Beach the top beach in America for Canadian visitors</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Every closing in South Carolina is run by a closing attorney. You can sign in person or by power of attorney from Canada. What you need before you offer:</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">What</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A passport</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Identity for the attorney, the lender if any, and the utilities.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A U.S. bank account</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Two Canadian banks open U.S. accounts from Canada with a Canadian ID. The closing attorney gives wiring instructions for the funds.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A U.S. tax number, or a plan to get one</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Needed to file the return on the rent, to stop the 30 percent withholding, and to sell. Not needed to buy.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">An accountant on each side of the border</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The rent and the sale are taxed in both countries. Canada credits the U.S. tax, but only on a return that claims it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="how-does-a-canadian-pay-for-a-myrtle-beach-rental"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">How does a Canadian pay for a Myrtle Beach rental?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Three ways.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Way</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Who it is for</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">What it needs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Cash</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Most Canadian investors, in Chapter3's files and in the national data.</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Funds wired to the closing attorney. No U.S. credit, no tax number, no loan file.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A foreign-national loan</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A Canadian who lives in Canada and wants a loan on a rental.</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A passport, foreign bank statements and reserves. No U.S. credit score. Some programs ask for a U.S. tax number, most do not. The loan is qualified on the property's rent, and it is for rentals only.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A standard U.S. loan</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A Canadian who lives in the U.S. with work authorization and a Social Security number or a U.S. tax number.</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The same loan as a U.S. buyer. Not open to a Canadian living in Canada.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-2-02/non-us-citizen-borrower-eligibility-requirements" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The standard loan rules cover non-citizens who are residents of the United States</a>. A Canadian in Canada is outside that sentence, so the loan that fits is <a href="/invest/strategies/dscr-loans/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">one qualified on the rent</a>. <a href="https://www.americamortgages.com/ultimate-dscr-loan-faq-foreign-nationals-expats/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">One foreign-national lender's public list</a>: a passport, six to twelve months of foreign bank statements, and no U.S. credit required. <a href="https://www.hud.gov/news/hud-no-25-048" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHA loans are closed to non-permanent residents</a>, so a work permit opens a standard loan but not an FHA one.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In Chapter3's experience the cash buyer closes fastest. A few of our Canadian investors have closed with a U.S. loan. If you want one, tell us early, because the loan file takes longer from outside the country.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Buying a rental from Canada?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the town and the budget. We tour by video, read the association documents, and put you with the closing attorney and, if you want a loan, a loan officer at our preferred lender.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Ask about buying from Canada</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-is-the-u-s-tax-number-and-when-does-a-canadian-owner"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">What is the U.S. tax number, and when does a Canadian owner need it?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/individual-taxpayer-identification-number" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">It is a nine-digit number for people who are not eligible for a Social Security number</a>. You apply with Form W-7, by mail or in person, and you attach the tax return it is for unless an exception applies. A landlord receiving rent and a seller of U.S. property both qualify for an exception. Allow seven weeks, and nine to eleven from overseas or in tax season.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You do not need it to buy. You need it three times after that. It goes on the U.S. return for the rent. It goes on the form that stops the 30 percent withholding, which you give to whoever collects your rent. It goes on the withholding certificate application when you sell. It expires if it is not used on a return for three years. Apply the year you buy, not the week you sell.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="how-is-the-rent-taxed-for-a-canadian-owner"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">How is the rent taxed for a Canadian owner?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two ways, and you choose.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase"></th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">The default</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">The election</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">What is taxed</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The gross rent, with no deductions</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The net rent, after every expense the property has</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The rate</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A flat 30 percent</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The normal U.S. rates on the net</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Who takes it</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p515" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Whoever collects the rent withholds it</a>: a manager</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Nobody. You file Form 1040-NR and pay what the return shows</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">How do you make the election? You sign <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/iw8eci" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Form W-8ECI</a> and give it to whoever collects your rent, usually the property manager. It does not go to the government. The form carries your U.S. tax number, so get the number first. The manager keeps it on file and stops withholding. You then file a U.S. return each year and pay on the profit. The form runs out at the end of the third year after you sign it, so you send a fresh one.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p519" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The election treats the rent as business income</a>, so the mortgage interest, the taxes, the insurance, the manager and the depreciation all count. Almost every owner makes it. Without the form, the person collecting your rent must withhold 30 percent of every payment and is personally liable if they do not. Managers here will ask for the form before the first tenant pays.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The U.S. return is due June 15. Canada taxes the same rent as world income and <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/international-non-residents/canadian-residents-going-down-south.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">credits the U.S. tax you paid</a>. <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/international-non-residents/information-been-moved/foreign-reporting/foreign-income-verification-statement.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">A rental costing more than C$100,000 also goes on Form T1135</a> each year; a vacation home used mostly by your family may not. The treaty does not exempt the rent. It lets the U.S. tax it first and makes Canada credit that tax.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-happens-when-a-canadian-sells-a-myrtle-beach-rental"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">What happens when a Canadian sells a Myrtle Beach rental?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two withholdings at the closing table, then two returns. Both withholdings are deposits against the tax, not the tax itself. The excess comes back through the returns.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Withholding</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">How much</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">On what</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Who takes it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Federal</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/firpta-withholding" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">15 percent</a></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The sale price, not the gain</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The buyer, through the closing attorney</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">South Carolina</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c008.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The state's top income tax rate</a></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The gain, if you sign an affidavit stating it. The sale price if you do not</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The buyer, remitted on Form I-290 by the fifteenth of the next month</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The federal residence exception, for sales at $300,000 or less, needs a buyer who will live in the house. A buyer who will rent it cannot give that, so a rental sold to another investor gets the full 15 percent. <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/withholding-certificates" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">A withholding certificate can cut it to the tax that is due</a>. The application, Form 8288-B, needs the tax numbers of everyone in the deal and takes about 90 days, so it is filed before the closing, not after.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The state affidavit needs your U.S. tax number too. Without it, the state share is taken from the whole price. Then Canada taxes the gain as world income and credits the U.S. tax. <a href="/sell/rental-property/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">How selling a rental works here</a>, and <a href="/sell/capital-gains/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the capital gains rules</a> that apply to every seller.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Selling a Myrtle Beach rental from Canada?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the address. We run the sale price, both withholdings and the closing costs before you list, and we start the certificate early.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/sell/net-proceeds/">Have us run your net proceeds</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="how-long-can-a-canadian-stay-and-when-does-u-s-tax-residency"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">How long can a Canadian stay, and when does U.S. tax residency start?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="https://travel.gc.ca/destinations/united-states" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Six months without a visa</a>, declared at the border. <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/alienregistration" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">A stay of 30 days or longer must be registered</a> with the U.S. government, and a Canadian who drove in without an entry record does that online.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Tax residency is a separate count. <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/substantial-presence-test" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The substantial presence test</a> adds every day this year, a third of last year's days, and a sixth of the year before. At 183 you are a U.S. tax resident. About four months every year, every year, is enough to reach it. A snowbird who reaches it but keeps a home and a closer connection to Canada <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8840" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">files Form 8840 by the return deadline</a> to stay a nonresident. Miss the form and the exception is lost for that year. Canada keeps taxing you as a resident the whole time.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-is-different-for-a-canadian-owner-after-closing"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">What is different for a Canadian owner after closing?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two things. The rest of owning a rental here is the same for you as for anyone, and <a href="/invest/property-management/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the pages on running a rental</a> cover it.</p><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The utilities want a deposit.</strong> <a href="https://www.santeecooper.com/residential/start-move-stop-service/start-service/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The power company runs a credit check to size it</a> and takes a passport number as identification. The check finds nothing. Credit files do not cross the border. Equifax and TransUnion run separate databases in each country and do not share them. A thirty-year Canadian credit record cannot be seen here. No file means the largest deposit. <a href="https://www.santeecooper.com/residential/ways-to-pay/pay-as-you-go/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Prepaid service skips the credit check and the deposit</a> for an upfront amount instead.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The estate is taxed here too.</strong> <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/some-nonresidents-with-us-assets-must-file-estate-tax-returns" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. real estate is a U.S. asset for estate tax</a>, and a nonresident's estate files when its U.S. assets pass $60,000. A house here clears that on its own. <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-trty/canada.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The treaty gives a Canadian resident's estate a credit pro-rated to the U.S. share of the whole estate</a>. The return is due nine months after death. Put the title together with an accountant on each side before you buy, not after.</li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="canadian-buyer-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Canadian buyer FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can a Canadian get a mortgage on a Myrtle Beach rental?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A standard U.S. loan is for people who live in the U.S. with a Social Security number or a U.S. tax number. A Canadian living in Canada uses a foreign-national loan qualified on the rent, with a passport and foreign bank statements and no U.S. credit. Most Canadian investors here pay cash.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do Canadians pay U.S. tax on Myrtle Beach rent?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. The default is a flat 30 percent of the gross rent, withheld by whoever collects it. Almost every owner instead elects to be taxed on the net rent, files Form 1040-NR, and gives the rent collector Form W-8ECI. Canada taxes the same rent and credits the U.S. tax.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much is withheld when a Canadian sells a house in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two withholdings. The federal one is 15 percent of the sale price, taken by the buyer. The state one is the state's top income tax rate on the gain, or on the price without a seller's affidavit. Both are deposits; the excess comes back through the returns.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do I need an ITIN to buy a house in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. You need it to file the U.S. return on the rent, to stop the 30 percent withholding, and to get a withholding certificate when you sell. Apply the year you buy. It takes seven to eleven weeks.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How long can a Canadian stay in Myrtle Beach each year?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Six months without a visa, declared at the border, with online registration for a stay of 30 days or more. About four months every year can make you a U.S. tax resident under the substantial presence test unless you file Form 8840 by the return deadline.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-2-02/non-us-citizen-borrower-eligibility-requirements" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Selling Guide B2-2-02</a>, <a href="https://www.hud.gov/news/hud-no-25-048" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD 25-048</a>, <a href="https://www.americamortgages.com/ultimate-dscr-loan-faq-foreign-nationals-expats/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">a foreign-national lender's FAQ</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/individual-taxpayer-identification-number" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS, ITIN</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p519" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Pub 519</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/instructions/iw8eci" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Form W-8ECI</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/firpta-withholding" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS, FIRPTA</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/withholding-certificates" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS, withholding certificates</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/some-nonresidents-with-us-assets-must-file-estate-tax-returns" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS, nonresident estates</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/international-taxpayers/substantial-presence-test" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS, substantial presence</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8840" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Form 8840</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-trty/canada.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S.-Canada treaty</a>, <a href="https://www.canada.ca/en/revenue-agency/services/tax/international-non-residents/information-been-moved/foreign-reporting/foreign-income-verification-statement.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CRA, T1135</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c008.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-8-580</a>, <a href="https://dor.sc.gov/forms-site/Forms/I290.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Form I-290</a>, <a href="https://www.santeecooper.com/residential/start-move-stop-service/start-service/" style="color:var(--navy);text-d