on[style^="background:var(--navy)"]:has(>.wrap>h2)>.wrap{padding:1.6rem 1.2rem 1.7rem!important;border-radius:24px!important}}</style><style>main#main .hx-strip:empty{display:none}main#main .hx-strip:not(:empty)+.hx-fallback{display:none}</style></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 12, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">Fannie Mae's condo rules, checked against county records for 43 Myrtle Beach oceanfront buildings, and the loans that still close.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A non-warrantable condo is in a building that fails one of Fannie Mae's project rules, so the usual conventional loan will not work there. You can still buy it, with cash or with a loan made outside those rules.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On the Myrtle Beach oceanfront, the common sign is a building run for renting. In 42 of the 43 oceanfront buildings we checked, at least 3 in 4 units are not taxed as the owner's home. Fannie Mae tells lenders to look harder at that.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Send us the building. We read its papers before you make an offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Ask about a building</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#what-it-means" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What it means</a></li><li><a href="#reasons" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What makes one</a></li><li><a href="#why-so-many" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Why so many here</a></li><li><a href="#which-buildings" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Which buildings</a></li><li><a href="#loans" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The loans that work</a></li><li><a href="#should-you-buy" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Should you buy one</a></li><li><a href="#how-we-check" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How we check</a></li><li><a href="#non-warrantable-condo-faq" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Non-warrantable condo FAQ</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-it-means"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What it means">What is a non-warrantable condo?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A warrantable condo is in a building that meets Fannie Mae's project rules. A non-warrantable condo is in a building that fails one of them. Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/about/fannie-mae-freddie-mac" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the two companies Congress created to buy mortgages from lenders</a>, will not buy a loan in a building that fails their project rules.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The rule is about the building, so a strong credit score does not change it.</p><ul class="nw-plain"><li><b>You can still buy it</b>Pay cash, or use a loan the lender keeps or sells outside Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. Each of those lenders makes its own building rules.</li><li><b>There is no list to look up</b>Fannie Mae publishes no list for buyers to search. A lender checks each building in Fannie Mae's own system, from the papers the HOA (homeowners association) gives it.</li><li><b>The answer can change</b>A building that fixes its problem can be reviewed again. A building's approval, or the lender's own certification of it, must be current on the day each loan closes.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Every condotel, a condo building run like a hotel, is non-warrantable. Many non-warrantable buildings are not condotels. <a href="/invest/condotel-financing/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our condotel financing guide</a> covers the hotel-run buildings.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="reasons"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What makes one">What makes a condo non-warrantable?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One failed rule is enough. These are among the reasons <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae lists</a>, grouped by what you would see in the building. The first group is the one most oceanfront buildings here meet. It is about who owns the units and how they use them.</p><p class="nw-sub">The building is run for renting</p><ul class="nw-plain"><li><b>Mostly investors and second homes</b>When 75 percent or more of the units are owned as investments or second homes, the lender must look harder at whether the building runs as a hotel. That alone does not turn the building down.</li><li><b>Run like a hotel</b>Any one of these counts: an HOA licensed as a hotel or resort, or papers that limit when owners may stay. Papers that require a rental pool or that owners share rental profits count too.</li><li><b>Hotel signs</b>Hotel-type services, resort marketing and a hotel rating count too. A name with hotel, motel or resort in it counts, unless the word only refers to the building's past use.</li><li><b>One owner with too many units</b>In a building of 21 or more units, no one owner may hold more than 20 percent. Rented units count. A developer's vacant units that are for sale do not.</li><li><b>Timeshare weeks</b>Fannie Mae and <a href="https://guide.freddiemac.com/app/guide/section/5701.3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac</a> will not take a timeshare building, where owners buy weeks.</li></ul><p class="nw-sub">The building's money</p><ul class="nw-plain"><li><b>Dues not paid</b>In Fannie Mae's <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.2-01/full-review-process" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">full review</a> of a condo building, no more than 15 percent of units may be 60 days or more behind on dues.</li><li><b>Thin savings for repairs</b>The budget must put at least 10 percent of dues income into reserves. <a href="https://guide.freddiemac.com/app/guide/bulletin/2026-C" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac raises the test to 15 percent</a> for applications from January 4, 2027. It says it made the change with its regulator and in alignment with Fannie Mae. Our <a href="/hoa/reserves/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">guide to HOA reserves</a> has the details.</li><li><b>Short master insurance</b>The building's <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b7-3-03/master-property-insurance-requirements-project-developments" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">master policy</a> must insure the full cost to rebuild. Its deductible can be at most 5 percent of that amount, a wind or storm deductible included, and at most $50,000 a unit.</li><li><b>Too much business</b>More than 35 percent of the space is commercial. Or the HOA earns more than 10 percent of its income from a business that serves the public, such as a restaurant or a spa.</li></ul><p class="nw-sub">Hard stops</p><ul class="nw-plain"><li><b>Critical repairs</b>Problems that could cause a failure within a year count. Unfunded repairs over $10,000 a unit due within 12 months count too, unless an owner makes them or a special assessment pays for them. Sea walls, elevators, waterproofing and balconies are among the examples.</li><li><b>A lawsuit</b>A pending suit that names the HOA stops the loan. A suit that names the developer over safety or structure stops it too. Some minor suits are allowed when the lender finds no effect on the building's safety, structure, use or livability. Our <a href="/buyers/condo-in-litigation/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">guide to buying a condo in litigation</a> covers suits.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For most condo loans, the building's budget and reserves are now checked in a full review.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Heard "non-warrantable" from a lender?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us which building. We find which rule it fails and what that means for the loans you can use.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Find the reason</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="why-so-many"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Why so many here">Why are so many Myrtle Beach oceanfront condos non-warrantable?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most of them are run for renting. Our directory has 43 oceanfront buildings with a county count, in Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach. By the county's tax records, at least three of four units in 42 of them are not the owner's home. That is one of the signs Fannie Mae tells a lender to look harder at.</p><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.3rem 0 1.5rem;max-width:720px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 720px" width="1200" height="675" alt="Three tall oceanfront condo towers behind the dunes in Myrtle Beach, with smaller condo buildings across a curving road to the right and the beach and ocean in front" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: USGS. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: USGS</a></span></div><figcaption>Oceanfront condo towers in Myrtle Beach, with their pools behind the dunes, seen from a plane over the ocean.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">An inland condo community on FHA's approved list makes the difference plain. At Seagate Village, about half the units are taxed as the owner's home.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="At the beach, few condos are the owner's home. Inland, about half are."><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">At the beach, few condos are the owner's home. Inland, about half are.</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Condo building</span><span role="columnheader">Units taxed as the owner's home</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Maisons Sur-Mer, the most of any</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.556" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">28%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Ocean Forest Plaza</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.476" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">24%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Margate Tower</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.366" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">18%</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">All 43 oceanfront buildings</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.092" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">5%</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Seagate Village, inland, on FHA's list</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.984" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">49%</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Share of condo units taxed at South Carolina's 4 percent rate for an owner's main home, from Horry County's parcel records. An owner who lives in the unit but never applied is counted as not.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A lender asks whether a building full of rentals is run like a hotel. To a buyer who wants a rental, it is a good sign: the building is run for exactly that, even when the loan costs more.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The listings say the same. About 8 in 10 condos for sale in these buildings say the unit is in a rental program.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Timeshare weeks and big owners</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Timeshare weeks are sold by recorded deed in Anderson Ocean Club, Towers on the Grove and SeaWatch's towers. Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac both list timeshare buildings among the ones they will not take.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In three other buildings, one company holds more than 1 in 5 of the units in at least one part of the building. That is over Fannie Mae's 20 percent limit, unless they are a developer's vacant units for sale.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The master insurance rule</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Take a building that would cost $20 million to rebuild. Its master policy's deductible can be at most $1 million, which is 5 percent. A larger wind deductible fails the rule, and the lender checks the policy.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">What your own condo owner's policy pays when the building's deductible comes due is in <a href="/hoa/master-insurance-ho6/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our guide to the master policy and your own condo policy</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="which-buildings"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Which buildings">Which Myrtle Beach condo buildings are non-warrantable?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Fannie Mae publishes no list for buyers to search. A lender decides for each building from its papers. What we can show you is the public signs a lender's review looks for, building by building.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Three oceanfront buildings have the most units taxed as the owner's home. If you hope to use a regular loan, start with these. A lender's review of the papers still decides, and we read them before you offer.</p><div class="nw-b" id="margate-tower"><h3 class="nw-bh"><span class="nw-n">1</span><span>Margate Tower: the tallest building in Myrtle Beach</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Margate Tower rises about 330 feet, the tallest building in Myrtle Beach, with units up to the 29th floor. It stands in Kingston Plantation, on the oceanfront.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 1 in 5 of its units are taxed as the owner's home, among the highest of any oceanfront building. Four of the eight units for sale say they are in a rental program.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="margate-tower" data-name="Margate Tower" data-head="Margate Tower condos, high up in Myrtle Beach's tallest building"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Margate%20Tower">Send me condos at Margate Tower<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div><div class="nw-b" id="maisons-sur-mer"><h3 class="nw-bh"><span class="nw-n">2</span><span>Maisons Sur-Mer: 24 floors up, near a course the PGA Tour played</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Maisons Sur-Mer rises about 300 feet on Shore Drive, with homes up to the 24th floor. It is under half a mile from the course of the Dunes Golf and Beach Club, which hosted the PGA Tour's 2026 Myrtle Beach Classic.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">More than 1 in 4 of its units are taxed as the owner's home, the most of any oceanfront building in the county records. By the tax records, it is the only oceanfront building under the 75 percent investor share that makes a lender look harder.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Only three of the 14 units for sale say they are in a rental program. Every one is a two-bedroom of about 1,400 square feet.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="maisons-sur-mer" data-name="Maisons Sur-Mer" data-head="Make Maisons Sur-Mer your two-bedroom beach home"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Maisons%20Sur-Mer">Send me condos at Maisons Sur-Mer<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div><div class="nw-b" id="ocean-forest-plaza"><h3 class="nw-bh"><span class="nw-n">3</span><span>Ocean Forest Plaza: an indoor pool, near Myrtle Beach's first golf course</span></h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Ocean Forest Plaza rises on the oceanfront with condos up to the 23rd floor. It is under half a mile from Pine Lakes, which opened in 1927 as Myrtle Beach's first golf course and is known as "The Granddaddy."</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For January, there is the indoor pool. All 14 condos for sale on November 9 listed one and allowed pets, and 12 said the HOA fee pays the power bill.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 1 in 4 units are taxed as the owner's home. Only five of those for sale list a rental program, and most are one-bedrooms of about 600 square feet.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="ocean-forest-plaza" data-name="Ocean Forest Plaza" data-head="Bring the dog: oceanfront condos at Ocean Forest Plaza"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><p class="c3-offer"><a class="c3-offer-pill" href="/contact/?place=Ocean%20Forest%20Plaza">Send me condos at Ocean Forest Plaza<span class="c3-offer-ar" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></a></p></div><div class="nw-after"><div class="nw-tw" id="every-building"><table class="nw-tab"><caption>The 43 oceanfront buildings with a county count: units taxed as the owner's home, and condos for sale in a rental program on November 10, 2026</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Building</th><th scope="col">Owner's home</th><th scope="col">Rental program</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Anderson Ocean Club</th><td>None</td><td>4 of 5</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Avista Resort</th><td>1%</td><td>10 of 11</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Bay View Resort</th><td>1%</td><td>10 of 10</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Bay Watch Resort</th><td>4%</td><td>44 of 46</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Beach Colony</th><td>10%</td><td>19 of 21</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Beach Cove Resort</th><td>Under 1%</td><td>29 of 29</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Bluewater Resort</th><td>12%</td><td>31 of 37</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Boardwalk Oceanfront Tower</th><td>Under 1%</td><td>12 of 12</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Breakers Resort</th><td>Under 1%</td><td>4 of 4</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Brighton Tower</th><td>8%</td><td>6 of 9</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Camelot by the Sea</th><td>2%</td><td>7 of 9</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Caravelle Resort</th><td>2%</td><td>44 of 45</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Caribbean Resort</th><td>Under 1%</td><td>25 of 25</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Carolina Winds</th><td>5%</td><td>11 of 13</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Carolinian Beach Resort</th><td>8%</td><td>7 of 11</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Compass Cove</th><td>Under 1%</td><td>35 of 35</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Coral Beach Resort</th><td>Under 1%</td><td>38 of 40</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Crescent Shores</th><td>10%</td><td>7 of 14</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Dunes Village Resort</th><td>None</td><td>25 of 26</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Grande Cayman Resort</th><td>Under 1%</td><td>19 of 22</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Holiday Inn Pavilion</th><td>None</td><td>12 of 15</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Holiday Sands</th><td>11%</td><td>13 of 15</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Landmark Resort</th><td>4%</td><td>82 of 92</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="#maisons-sur-mer" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Maisons Sur-Mer</a></th><td>28%</td><td>3 of 14</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mar Vista Grande</th><td>10%</td><td>3 of 6</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="#margate-tower" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Margate Tower</a></th><td>18%</td><td>4 of 8</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Meridian Plaza</th><td>4%</td><td>12 of 17</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Monterey Bay Suites</th><td>4%</td><td>23 of 29</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">North Beach Towers</th><td>2%</td><td>10 of 10</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><a href="#ocean-forest-plaza" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Ocean Forest Plaza</a></th><td>24%</td><td>5 of 14</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Ocean Reef Resort</th><td>None</td><td>27 of 32</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Oceans One</th><td>2%</td><td>9 of 11</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Patricia Grand</th><td>Under 1%</td><td>8 of 8</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Prince Resort</th><td>5%</td><td>6 of 7</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Royale Palms</th><td>7%</td><td>9 of 14</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Sand Dunes Resort</th><td>4%</td><td>45 of 51</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Sands Ocean Club</th><td>3%</td><td>11 of 16</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Sandy Beach Resort</th><td>2%</td><td>10 of 11</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Sea Mist Resort</th><td>7%</td><td>24 of 33</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">SeaWatch Resort</th><td>2%</td><td>4 of 5</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">South Hampton</th><td>8%</td><td>9 of 13</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The Palace</th><td>11%</td><td>21 of 25</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Towers on the Grove</th><td>Under 1%</td><td>3 of 3</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each building's fee, amenities and recent sales are in <a href="/invest/condo-buildings/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our oceanfront condo building directory</a>.</p></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Picked a building? Send it to us.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We pull its budget, its master policy and its meeting minutes, and tell you what a lender will see, before you offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us check my building</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="loans"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The loans that work">Can you get a loan on a non-warrantable condo?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Cash works, and so do loans a lender keeps or sells outside Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. Each of those lenders writes its own building rules. One published program shows how far apart the rules can be.</p><div class="nw-tw"><table class="nw-tab nw-cmp"><caption>Where one non-warrantable loan program says yes</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">The test</th><th scope="col">Fannie Mae</th><th scope="col">One published program</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">One owner's share of the units, in a building of 21 or more</th><td><span class="l">Fannie Mae</span>Up to 20 percent</td><td class="yes"><span class="l">One published program</span>Up to 49 percent, with conditions</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Units 60 days or more behind on dues</th><td><span class="l">Fannie Mae</span>Up to 15 percent</td><td class="yes"><span class="l">One published program</span>Up to 25 percent</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Space used for business</th><td><span class="l">Fannie Mae</span>Up to 35 percent</td><td class="yes"><span class="l">One published program</span>Up to 50 percent</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A lawsuit</th><td><span class="l">Fannie Mae</span>A suit naming the HOA, unless it is minor</td><td class="yes"><span class="l">One published program</span>Case by case, never one about the structure</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Rules the building can miss</th><td><span class="l">Fannie Mae</span>None</td><td class="yes"><span class="l">One published program</span>Up to 2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class="nw-plain"><li><b>A loan made outside Fannie Mae's rules</b>The lender keeps the loan or sells it to other investors. These loans often cost more than a conventional loan.</li><li><b>A DSCR loan</b>It qualifies on the unit's rent instead of your income. Our <a href="/invest/strategies/dscr-loans/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">DSCR loan guide</a> covers it.</li><li><b>Cash</b>No lender reviews the building.</li><li><b>FHA</b>FHA works only in a building on <a href="https://entp.hud.gov/idapp/html/condlook.cfm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD's approved list</a>. Of its 117 Horry County records, 9 are approved. In our directory of oceanfront buildings, the only approved one is Sea Mark Tower, one of the Caravelle's nine buildings. FHA will not insure a loan on a condotel, and it will not approve a condo building that is a condo hotel or has a required rental pool.</li><li><b>VA</b>A VA loan is for a home you will live in. At least 6 of the 47 buildings in our full oceanfront directory have an accepted record on <a href="https://lgy.va.gov/lgyhub/condo-report" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the VA's condo list</a>. Our <a href="/buyers/va-loans/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">VA loan guide</a> and <a href="/buyers/programs/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">buyer programs guide</a> have more.</li></ul><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much do you need down?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a rental you will not stay in more than 14 days a year, plan on about 25 percent down in a non-warrantable building. In a warrantable one, plan on about 20 percent. Figures from BrickWood Mortgage.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a home or a second home you will use yourself, plan on a larger down payment than a conventional loan asks.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who makes these loans</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our affiliated lender, BrickWood Mortgage, a separate company, arranges these loans through wholesale lenders that accept non-warrantable buildings.</p><p class="nw-disc">Chapter3 Realty Corp has a business relationship with BrickWood Mortgage (NMLS #189497). Because of this relationship, this referral may provide Chapter3 Realty a financial or other benefit. You are never required to use BrickWood. You are free to shop any lender.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="should-you-buy"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Should you buy one">Should you buy a non-warrantable condo?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, when the building's reason is one you can live with and the loan fits your plan. What you gain, and what it costs you:</p><p class="nw-sub">What you gain</p><ul class="nw-plain"><li><b>A building run for renting</b>A building full of investors is run for what a rental owner wants.</li><li><b>Units that sell</b>About 780 units sold one at a time by recorded deed in a year, in the 43 directory buildings with a county count.</li><li><b>A reason that can be fixed</b>A new master policy, a lawsuit that ends or dues collected can bring a building back. A lender then reviews it again.</li></ul><p class="nw-sub">What it costs you</p><ul class="nw-plain"><li><b>More cash at closing</b>Plan on a larger down payment than a conventional loan, and often a higher cost.</li><li><b>Fewer lenders, now and when you sell</b>Fewer lenders make these loans, and each has its own limits. Your next buyer needs one too, unless they pay cash.</li><li><b>Some reasons stop every loan</b>A critical repair or a suit over the structure stops Fannie Mae loans. One published non-warrantable program will not take a suit over the structure either.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Whether a beach condo makes a good rental at all is in <a href="/invest/condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our guide to Myrtle Beach condos as investments</a>.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Weighing one building as a rental or a beach home?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the building and how you will use it. We tell you its reason, the loans that fit and what we would watch, before you offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Help me weigh a building</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="how-we-check"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How we check">How do you find out if a condo is warrantable before you make an offer?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We find out for you, before you make an offer. Our agents read the same papers a lender's review reads:</p><ul class="nw-plain"><li><b>The lender questionnaire</b>The HOA's answers to the questions a lender asks about the building.</li><li><b>The budget and reserves</b>What goes into savings for repairs, against the 10 percent test.</li><li><b>The master policy</b>What it insures and how big its deductible is, wind included.</li><li><b>The minutes and any special assessment</b>Repairs the board has talked about, and what owners have been asked to pay.</li><li><b>Any lawsuit</b>Who is suing whom, and over what.</li><li><b>The county's records</b>Whether one owner holds a block of units, and whether timeshare weeks are sold in the building.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A loan officer at our preferred lender then tells us how a lender will read it. You know before you pay for an appraisal or an inspection. The papers themselves are in <a href="/hoa/documents/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our guide to HOA documents</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="non-warrantable-condo-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Non-warrantable condo FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is the difference between a warrantable and a non-warrantable condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A warrantable condo is in a building that meets Fannie Mae's project rules, so a conventional loan there can be sold to Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac. A non-warrantable condo is in a building that fails one of those rules.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is there a list of non-warrantable condos in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Fannie Mae publishes no list for buyers to search. A lender reviews each building from its papers. Our table shows the public signs for 43 oceanfront buildings.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I use an FHA or VA loan on a non-warrantable condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Only in a building on that agency's own list. Of the oceanfront buildings in our directory, only Sea Mark Tower at the Caravelle is FHA-approved, and at least 6 have an accepted VA record.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much do you need down for a non-warrantable condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a rental you will not stay in more than 14 days a year, about 25 percent down, against about 20 percent in a warrantable building. Figures from BrickWood Mortgage. For a home you will use, plan on a larger down payment.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can a non-warrantable condo become warrantable?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. When the building fixes the reason, such as a new master policy or a lawsuit that ends, a lender can review it again.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can I buy a non-warrantable condo as a second home?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, with cash or a loan made outside Fannie Mae's rules. Plan on a larger down payment than for a conventional loan.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-03</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.2-01/full-review-process" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.2-01</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b7-3-03/master-property-insurance-requirements-project-developments" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B7-3-03</a>, <a href="https://guide.freddiemac.com/app/guide/section/5701.3" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac 5701.3</a>, <a href="https://guide.freddiemac.com/app/guide/bulletin/2026-C" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac 2026-C</a>, <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/about/fannie-mae-freddie-mac" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHFA</a>, <a href="https://www.newrezwholesale.com/media/b0hbzabm/smart-product-project-checklist-3-2026.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Newrez checklist</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycounty.org/parcelapp/rest/services/HorryCountyGISApp/MapServer" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">County parcels</a>, <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">County deeds</a>, <a href="https://entp.hud.gov/idapp/html/condlook.cfm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD condo list</a>, <a href="https://lgy.va.gov/lgyhub/condo-report" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VA condo report</a>, <a href="https://aeronav.faa.gov/Obst_Data/DAILY_DOF_DAT.ZIP" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FAA obstacle file</a>, <a href="https://pinelakes.com/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pine Lakes</a>. Our counts: county parcels for 43 buildings, the deed index and our listing feed on November 10, 2026, in total only. A unit taxed at 4 percent counts as a home, so the share is a floor.</p><style>.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Oceanfront condo towers in Myrtle Beach. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":675,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Oceanfront condo towers in Myrtle Beach, with their pools behind the dunes, seen from a plane over the ocean","caption":"Oceanfront condo towers in Myrtle Beach, with their pools behind the dunes, seen from a plane over the ocean.","description":"Three tall oceanfront condo towers behind the dunes in Myrtle Beach, with smaller condo buildings across a curving road to the right and the beach and ocean in front","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"U.S. Geological Survey"},"credi