-</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Use our rental analyzer. Enter an address and the price, and say whether you pay cash or take a loan. It fills in the rent and the costs, and Chapter3 AI sums up what the numbers mean. Change any number and the results change with it.</p><div class="rn-embed"><iframe src="/invest/long-term-rental/?embed=1" title="Chapter3 rental analyzer" loading="eager" style="display:block;width:100%;height:1100px;border:0;border-radius:20px;background:transparent"></iframe></div><script>addEventListener("message",function(e){if(e.origin!==location.origin||!e.data||e.data.c3embed!=="ltr")return;var f=document.querySelector(".rn-embed iframe");if(f)f.style.height=Math.max(640,Math.min(9000,+e.data.h||0))+"px"})</script><p class="rn-note">The analyzer's rent and costs are estimates for a first look. For one address we check the rent against nearby rentals and the dues against the association's papers: <a href="#run-form" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">send it to us</a>. For a nightly rental, read the <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rules by city</a> first.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="sheet"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The sheet">What does the numbers sheet look like?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One page with every cost and where each number comes from. Here it is for the middle 3-bedroom house for sale in Carolina Forest, asking $390,000.</p><div class="rn-sheet" role="group" aria-label="The numbers sheet for the middle 3-bedroom house for sale in Carolina Forest"><div class="rn-sheet-h"><p>Numbers sheet: 3-bedroom house, Carolina Forest</p><span>Asking $390,000</span></div><div class="rn-line"><b>Rent</b><em>$26,760</em><small>$2,230 a month: HUD's fair market rent for 3 bedrooms in ZIP code 29579.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Property tax</b><em>−$4,700</em><small>About $1,206 for each $100,000 of the price, the rate a rental pays outside city limits, once the county resets the value after the sale.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>HOA dues</b><em>−$1,200</em><small>$100 a month: the middle HOA fee of 3-bedroom houses for sale in 29579.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Insurance</b><em>your quote</em><small>Priced house by house: add your quote. Flood is a separate policy.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Management</b><em>−$2,676</em><small>Our allowance: 10 percent of the rent.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Repairs and empty months</b><em>−$2,676</em><small>Our allowance: 10 percent of the rent.</small></div><div class="rn-total"><b>Left a year, before insurance and any loan</b><strong>about $15,500</strong><small>4.0 percent of the price: the cap rate. Our target is 6 percent.</small></div><ul class="rn-chips"><li>The 1 percent rule would need a price of $223,000</li><li>Rent before any cost: 6.9 percent of the price a year</li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For an address you send, the rent comes from nearby rentals, and the dues and rules from the association's papers. We can get you an insurance quote for the house before you make an offer.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The same sheet on three homes</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The condo keeps the least, because its dues take more than a third of the rent.</p><figure class="rn-rent"><span class="cap">Where each home's rent goes in a year</span><ul class="rn-key" aria-hidden="true"><li><i style="background:#1c2028"></i>Property tax</li><li><i style="background:#5c6b78"></i>HOA dues</li><li><i style="background:#7d8a96"></i>Insurance</li><li><i style="background:#9aa4ad"></i>Management</li><li><i style="background:#c9ced3"></i>Repairs and empty months</li><li><i style="background:#c4783a"></i>Left before a loan</li></ul><div role="img" aria-label="3-bedroom house, Carolina Forest: Property tax 18 percent, HOA dues 4 percent, Insurance 0 percent, Management 10 percent, Repairs and empty months 10 percent, Left before a loan 58 percent. 3-bedroom house, Longs: Property tax 14 percent, HOA dues 4 percent, Insurance 0 percent, Management 10 percent, Repairs and empty months 10 percent, Left before a loan 61 percent. 2-bedroom condo, inside the City of Myrtle Beach: Property tax 22 percent, HOA dues 39 percent, Insurance 0 percent, Management 10 percent, Repairs and empty months 10 percent, Left before a loan 20 percent."><div class="rn-home"><div class="rn-hl"><strong>3-bedroom house, Carolina Forest</strong>, asking $390,000, rent $2,230 a month</div><div class="rn-bar"><span style="width:17.57%;background:#1c2028;color:#fff">18%</span><span style="width:4.48%;background:#5c6b78;color:#fff"></span><span style="width:0.00%;background:#7d8a96;color:#fff"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#9aa4ad;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#c9ced3;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:57.94%;background:#c4783a;color:#1c2028">58%</span></div><div class="rn-hl rn-sub">Cap rate: 4.0 percent of the price a year, before insurance and any loan</div></div><div class="rn-home"><div class="rn-hl"><strong>3-bedroom house, Longs</strong>, asking $320,000, rent $2,270 a month</div><div class="rn-bar"><span style="width:14.17%;background:#1c2028;color:#fff">14%</span><span style="width:4.41%;background:#5c6b78;color:#fff"></span><span style="width:0.00%;background:#7d8a96;color:#fff"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#9aa4ad;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#c9ced3;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:61.43%;background:#c4783a;color:#1c2028">61%</span></div><div class="rn-hl rn-sub">Cap rate: 5.2 percent of the price a year, before insurance and any loan</div></div><div class="rn-home"><div class="rn-hl"><strong>2-bedroom condo, inside the City of Myrtle Beach</strong>, asking $240,000, rent $1,420 a month</div><div class="rn-bar"><span style="width:21.51%;background:#1c2028;color:#fff">22%</span><span style="width:38.73%;background:#5c6b78;color:#fff">39%</span><span style="width:0.00%;background:#7d8a96;color:#fff"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#9aa4ad;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#c9ced3;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:19.75%;background:#c4783a;color:#1c2028">20%</span></div><div class="rn-hl rn-sub">Cap rate: 1.4 percent of the price a year, before insurance and any loan</div></div></div><figcaption>The middle asking price and dues for each kind of home in its ZIP code, HUD's rent, the tax a rental pays there, and our 10 percent allowances. Not a value for any one home.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="one-percent-rule"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The 1% rule">Does the 1 percent rule work in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Almost never for a house. The rule asks for a month's rent of at least 1 percent of the price. Of 2,975 houses with 3 or more bedrooms for sale in Horry County, 14 pass. Longs has 8 of them. We ran this count on November 1, 2026.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Houses for sale whose yearly rent is at least 8 percent of the price"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Houses for sale whose yearly rent is at least 8 percent of the price</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Area, houses that pass the 1% rule</span><span role="columnheader">houses at 8 percent or more</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Longs</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">8 pass the 1% rule</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.563" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">189 of 336</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Loris</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.2" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">30 of 150</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Little River</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.187" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">34 of 182</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Carolina Forest</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">3 pass the 1% rule</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.181" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">66 of 365</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Aynor</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">1 pass the 1% rule</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.143" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">6 of 42</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Conway (29527)</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">2 pass the 1% rule</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.123" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">24 of 195</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Galivants Ferry</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.095" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">6 of 63</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Socastee</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.095" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">25 of 264</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Conway (29526)</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.075" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">35 of 467</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Murrells Inlet and Garden City</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.028" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">6 of 216</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.017" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">3 of 181</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Surfside Beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.012" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">2 of 161</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">North Myrtle Beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.004" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">1 of 239</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach, north end</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">0 of 100</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Houses with 3 or more bedrooms for sale, each against HUD's fair market rent for its ZIP code and bedrooms. The 1 percent rule asks for 12 percent a year, so the bar uses a lower test, 8 percent, to show where the rent comes closest. Not shown: Nichols, 14 houses, none passing.</figcaption></figure><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:620px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/invest-run-the-numbers/carolina-forest-houses-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/invest-run-the-numbers/carolina-forest-houses-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/invest-run-the-numbers/carolina-forest-houses-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 660px) 100vw, 620px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Rows of similar houses with gray roofs, short driveways and small fenced back yards along curving streets in Carolina Forest, seen from above" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title17/html/USCODE-2023-title17-chap1-sec105.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo</a></span></div><figcaption>Houses in The Parks of Carolina Forest, ZIP code 29579, from above. 3 of the 14 houses for sale that pass the 1 percent rule are in this ZIP code.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Condos often pass on price alone, then fail once the dues are counted. In the ZIP codes we tested, about 1 in 8 condos for sale passes on price. Count the dues against the rent, and fewer than 3 in 100 still pass.</p><div class="rn-hard"><p class="rn-hard-h">Get this week's houses that pass the 1 percent rule</p><p>Homes come on and off the market every day, so this count is already old. Call or text us and we run it again that day, then send you every house that passes, with its numbers.</p><div class="rn-hard-b"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us run the count for you</a><a class="rn-hard-tel" href="tel:+18543332135">Call or text 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="tax"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The tax">Why is the tax on the listing not the tax you will pay?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Because the listing shows the seller's bill. After you buy, the county resets the value to about what you paid. A rental also pays a higher rate than a home its owner lives in.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A rental pays about $1,206 a year for each $100,000 of value outside city limits, and about $1,528 in the City of Myrtle Beach. Outside city limits, that is about 3 times what an owner who lives there pays.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A home its owner does not live in, such as a house rented out year-round or a second home, is taxed on <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">6 percent of its value</a>. The home you live in is taxed on 4 percent. That 6 percent is the share of the value that is taxed, not the yearly rate. A rental also pays the school operating tax, which a home you live in does not.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Rent out the home you live in for more than 72 days in a calendar year, and it loses the 4 percent rate. Active-duty military under orders keep it.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We read the county's deeds for this year's resales and the value each seller was taxed on. The middle home sold for about 1.2 times that value.</p><figure class="rn-tax"><span class="cap">Your next tax bill against the seller's, outside city limits</span><div><div class="rn-tg"><p>Buying a home taxed at 4 percent, as an owner's home</p><div class="rn-tr"><span class="l" aria-hidden="true">The seller's bill: <b>$1,000</b></span><svg class="b" role="img" aria-label="The seller's bill: $1,000" viewBox="0 0 100 10" preserveAspectRatio="none" style="--f:0.250"><rect width="100" height="10" fill="currentColor"/></svg></div><div class="rn-tr you"><span class="l" aria-hidden="true">Your next bill: <b>about $3,900</b></span><svg class="b" role="img" aria-label="Your next bill: about $3,900" viewBox="0 0 100 10" preserveAspectRatio="none" style="--f:0.980"><rect width="100" height="10" fill="currentColor"/></svg></div></div><div class="rn-tg"><p>Buying a home already taxed at 6 percent, such as a rental</p><div class="rn-tr"><span class="l" aria-hidden="true">The seller's bill: <b>$1,000</b></span><svg class="b" role="img" aria-label="The seller's bill: $1,000" viewBox="0 0 100 10" preserveAspectRatio="none" style="--f:0.250"><rect width="100" height="10" fill="currentColor"/></svg></div><div class="rn-tr you"><span class="l" aria-hidden="true">Your next bill: <b>about $1,100</b></span><svg class="b" role="img" aria-label="Your next bill: about $1,100" viewBox="0 0 100 10" preserveAspectRatio="none" style="--f:0.275"><rect width="100" height="10" fill="currentColor"/></svg></div></div></div><figcaption>The middle resale outside city limits, January to September 2026, by the rate on the seller's tax record. Your bill is figured on the sale price, before any exemption. A seller's Homestead exemption would make the jump larger.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Buy a home taxed at 4 percent and rent it out, outside city limits, and your next bill is about 3.9 times the seller's. Buy a home already taxed at 6 percent, such as a rental from another landlord, and it is about 1.1 times. In our experience, taxes stop more rental deals than insurance does.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">When the new bill starts</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you buy between January and September, your first October bill still uses the seller's value, and the next one uses the new value. Buy in October or later, and your first bill already uses the new value. <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/real-property-tax/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bills are mailed October 1</a> and are due by January 15.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A rental bought from another landlord can get a break. The 25 percent exemption applies only when the home was taxed at 6 percent before the sale and stays at 6 percent after it. Then a quarter of its new value is exempt, but the taxed value cannot fall below the county's current market value.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The owner or the owner's agent must notify the county assessor before January 31 of the first tax year it applies. We remind you before the deadline.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Every town's rate is in our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax calculator</a>.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want the tax for one address?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We work out the bill you would pay as a landlord, from the county's record for that home and the rate in its town.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#run-form">Have us run the numbers on a home</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="condo-dues"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Condo dues">What does a condo's HOA fee do to the numbers?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It takes a large share of the rent. More than half of condos for sale on the Grand Strand list dues over $600 a month.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a 2-bedroom condo, the middle dues take from about a quarter of HUD's rent in Carolina Forest to about half at the north end of Myrtle Beach.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="The middle 2-bedroom condo's HOA dues, as a share of HUD's rent"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">The middle 2-bedroom condo's HOA dues, as a share of HUD's rent</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Area, dues of the rent, a month</span><span role="columnheader">dues, share of the rent</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Carolina Forest</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$425 of $1,840</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.23" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">23% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Little River</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$500 of $1,730</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.29" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">29% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Surfside Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$500 of $1,590</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.31" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">31% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Murrells Inlet and Garden City</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$525 of $1,720</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.31" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">31% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$550 of $1,420</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.39" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">39% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">North Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$600 of $1,550</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.39" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">39% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach, north end</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$725 of $1,500</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.48" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">48% of the rent</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>The middle monthly dues of 2-bedroom condos for sale in each ZIP code, against HUD's fair market rent for 2 bedrooms there.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The dues are why the condo in the sheet above keeps so little. Our <a href="/invest/condo-buildings/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">condo buildings page</a> lists dues building by building. A condo rented by the night has different numbers: our <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Airbnb income page</a> has them by area, and <a href="/invest/condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our condo guide</a> covers the building rules.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="ratios"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The ratios">What cap rate, cash-on-cash return and coverage should you aim for here?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our targets are a cap rate of 6 percent or more, coverage of 1.25 or more, and prices that rise 3 percent a year or more. None of the three homes in the sheet reaches the cap rate target.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Why do investors buy here? Prices in the metro rose about 4.8 percent a year in the five years to July 2026, above our 3 percent target. Over ten years they rose 6.1 percent a year. In the three years to then they fell 1.7 percent in total. Our agents keep a list of investors who buy for a modest cash flow plus that rise in price. The <a href="/invest/rental-returns/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental returns page</a> breaks the rise down by area.</p><div class="rn-cards"><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span>Cap rate</h3><p>The rent left after every cost in a year, divided by the price. No loan is in it, so it compares homes, not deals.</p><p class="f">Our target: 6 percent or more.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10.8 12.2 20 3M16.8 6.2l2.5 2.5M14.3 8.7l2 2"/><circle cx="7.5" cy="15.5" r="4.5"/></svg></span>Cash-on-cash return</h3><p>The cash left after the loan payments in a year, divided by the cash you put in. If you pay cash, it is the same as the cap rate. A loan raises it or lowers it, and the calculator shows which once you type the payment.</p><p class="f">With no loan, aim for the same 6 percent.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span>Coverage (DSCR)</h3><p>The debt service coverage ratio: the rent divided by the monthly cost of the loan, taxes, insurance and dues. A DSCR loan is judged on this ratio. A loan officer at our preferred lender starts writing DSCR loans at 0.75, so a home under 1.25 can still get one.</p><p class="f">Our target: 1.25, a rent a quarter more than those monthly costs.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span>The 1 percent rule</h3><p>A month's rent should be at least 1 percent of the price. It is a quick screen. The cap rate tells you more.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 3h8l4 4v13a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H6a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V4a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM14 3v4h4M9 17l6-6"/><circle cx="9.5" cy="11.5" r="1"/><circle cx="14.5" cy="16.5" r="1"/></svg></span>The 50 percent rule</h3><p>Investors' shorthand: the costs before the loan take about half the rent. The chart of the three homes above shows it fits the houses and fails the condo.</p></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="costs"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Every cost">What are the expenses on a rental property here?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Six come out of the rent on every long-term rental: the property tax, insurance, HOA dues, management, repairs and empty months. A nightly rental adds its own taxes and licenses.</p><div class="rn-costs"><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Cost</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">What it is here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Property tax</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">About $1,206 a year for each $100,000 of value outside city limits, and $1,528 in the City of Myrtle Beach, on a value reset to about the price after you buy. <a href="#tax" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Why the listing's tax is not yours</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Insurance</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Priced house by house. Flood is a separate policy. <a href="/invest/landlord-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Flood and wind on a rental</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>HOA dues</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The middle 3-bedroom house for sale pays $25 to $300 a month, by area. Condo dues are in <a href="#condo-dues" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the section above</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Management</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">We allow 10 percent of the rent.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Repairs and empty months</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">We allow 10 percent of the rent.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Nightly rental taxes and licenses</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Only on a short-term rental. <a href="/invest/accommodations-tax/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">The accommodations tax</a>, by town.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Depreciation, a tax saving</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The building, not the land, is depreciated over <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">27.5 years</a> for federal tax. <a href="/invest/rental-depreciation/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">How it works</a>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-we-check"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What we check">What can a calculator not see?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The community's rules, how rents keep up with prices, and the seller's own numbers. These are three things we check for you before you offer.</p><div class="rn-cards"><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 21h18M5 21V9.5M19 21V9.5M5 9.5C7 6.8 9.4 5.5 12 5.5s5 1.3 7 4M12 5.5V21M8.5 7v14M15.5 7v14M5 15h14"/></svg></span>A rental ban</h3><p>Some communities here ban renting the house at all. We filter those out when we search for you, and we read the HOA papers for rules that would block your plan.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span>Prices rose, the rent did not</h3><p>An agent now on our team helped buyers purchase about eight nearly identical townhouses in a new Little River community. Each rented for a little over $2,000.</p><p>Prices rose from $230,000 to $292,000 over about 18 months. The rents stayed flat, and the cash flow went away for later buyers.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span>The seller's cash flow is not yours</h3><p>An agent at Chapter3 sold a rented house for an owner of 15 years. His cash flow was over $1,200 a month. The next investor would make about $600, because of higher taxes and a bigger loan.</p><p>The agent marketed it to investors who accept a modest cash flow in an area where prices are rising. A cash buyer closed 13 days after it was listed.</p></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="run-form"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Send an address">What should you send us?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The address is enough. What investors ask us most is "send me properties with analytics to prove it", so that is what you get back.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You get the sheet above for that home. It has the rent from nearby rentals, the tax at the rate you will pay, and the dues and the building's rules. The sheet is free, and most come back the same day, evenings included.</p><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">The address, or the MLS number. Any home on the Grand Strand, listed with any brokerage, or one not for sale yet.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Your plan: long-term rental, short-term rental, mid-term rental or fix and flip. Not sure is fine.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">How you plan to pay, cash or a loan. We do not need your finances to run the home. <a href="/invest/cash-to-close/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">What you pay at closing</a>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Whether you will stay in it yourself. <a href="/invest/14-day-rule/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">How your own stays change the tax</a>.</li></ul><div id="rnWrap" style="max-width:680px;background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:14px;padding:1.6rem;text-align:left;margin:1.4rem 0">
+</style><style>@media (min-width:700px){main#main .detail-h1{text-wrap:balance}}main#main .detail-h1 em{display:block;font-size:clamp(1.3rem,2.5vw,2.25rem);line-height:1.25;margin-top:.55rem;text-wrap:balance}@media (max-width:959px){main#main .detail-h1 em{color:#8a5226!important}}main#main .detail-media{max-width:720px!important}main#main .c3-glance{max-width:720px;gap:0 1.4rem!important;border-top:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16);border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16)}main#main .c3-glance li{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:0!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;padding:.7rem 0!important;line-height:1.4}main#main .c3-glance strong{font-size:1.1rem!important;font-weight:700;line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-glance span.t{font-size:.95rem!important;line-height:1.4!important}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-glance{grid-template-columns:1fr!important;border-bottom:0}main#main .c3-glance li{border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16)!important;flex-direction:row!important;text-align:left!important;justify-content:flex-start!important;gap:.7rem!important;padding:.55rem 0!important}main#main .c3-glance .i{width:2rem!important;height:2rem!important}}main#main .rn-embed,main#main .rn-sheet,main#main .rn-rent,main#main .rn-tax,main#main .c3-gchart,main#main .rn-hard,main#main #rnWrap,main#main #rnOk{max-width:720px!important;box-sizing:border-box}main#main .rn-sheet,main#main .rn-rent,main#main .rn-tax,main#main .c3-gchart{background:linear-gradient(180deg,rgba(255,255,255,.82),rgba(255,255,255,.58));border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.9),0 10px 26px -18px rgba(28,32,40,.35);border-radius:20px!important}main#main .rn-sheet,main#main .rn-rent,main#main .rn-tax{padding:1.1rem 1.3rem 1rem!important}@media (max-width:520px){main#main .rn-sheet,main#main .rn-rent,main#main .rn-tax{padding:1rem .95rem .9rem!important}}main#main .rn-rent figcaption,main#main .rn-tax figcaption{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:0!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;padding:0!important;margin-top:.8rem!important}main#main .rn-chips li{background:none;border-left:2px solid #c4783a;border-radius:0;padding:.1rem 0 .1rem .7rem}main#main .rn-costs>div{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:0!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;padding:0!important;max-width:720px!important}main#main .rn-costs table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse}main#main .rn-costs th,main#main .rn-costs td{border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16)!important}main#main .rn-costs td:first-child,main#main .rn-costs th:first-child{padding-left:0!important}main#main .rn-plain{max-width:720px;gap:0 1.6rem;margin:1.1rem 0 1.4rem}main#main .rn-plain .rn-card{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:0!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;padding:.9rem 0 .2rem!important;border-top:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.16)!important}@media (min-width:760px){main#main .rn-plain{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr))}}main#main .rn-plain .rn-card h3{align-items:flex-start;font-size:1.06rem!important}main#main .rn-plain .rn-card h3 .i{display:none}main#main .rn-plain .rn-card p{margin:0 0 .7rem}main#main div[style^="border-left:3px solid var(--brass)"]{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:0!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;border-top:2px solid #c4783a!important;padding:1rem 0 .2rem!important;margin:1.8rem 0 .6rem!important;max-width:720px}main#main div[style^="border-left:3px solid var(--brass)"]>p:first-child{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:1.08rem}main#main .rn-onerow .c3ph-hero{background:none!important;border:0!important;box-shadow:none!important;border-radius:0!important;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none!important;backdrop-filter:none!important;padding:0!important;max-width:720px!important}main#main .rn-onerow .c3ph-hero figcaption{padding:0!important;margin-top:.55rem}@media (min-width:1180px){main#main .rn-onerow{display:grid;grid-template-columns:720px minmax(0,1fr);column-gap:2.6rem;align-items:start;margin:1.2rem 0 1.4rem}main#main .rn-onerow>.c3-gchart{grid-column:1;grid-row:1;margin:0!important}main#main .rn-onerow>.c3ph-hero{grid-column:2;grid-row:1;margin:0!important;position:sticky;top:9.5rem}}@media (min-width:1000px) and (max-width:1100px){.header-ctas .hdr-phone{display:none!important}}main#main h2,main#main h3,main#main .rn-rent .cap,main#main .rn-tax .cap,main#main .c3-gchart .cap{text-wrap:balance}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Use our rental analyzer. Enter an address and the price, and say whether you pay cash or take a loan. It fills in the rent and the costs, and Chapter3 AI sums up what the numbers mean. Change any number and the results change with it.</p><div class="rn-embed"><iframe src="/invest/long-term-rental/?embed=1" title="Chapter3 rental analyzer" loading="eager" style="display:block;width:100%;height:1100px;border:0;border-radius:20px;background:transparent"></iframe></div><script>addEventListener("message",function(e){if(e.origin!==location.origin||!e.data||e.data.c3embed!=="ltr")return;var f=document.querySelector(".rn-embed iframe");if(f)f.style.height=Math.max(640,Math.min(9000,+e.data.h||0))+"px"})</script><p class="rn-note">The analyzer's rent and costs are estimates for a first look. For one address we check the rent against nearby rentals and the dues against the association's papers: <a href="#run-form" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">send it to us</a>. For a nightly rental, read the <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rules by city</a> first. Run any address through our <a href="/invest/long-term-rental/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">free long-term rental analyzer</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="sheet"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The sheet">What does the numbers sheet look like?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One page with every cost and where each number comes from. Here it is for the middle 3-bedroom house for sale in Carolina Forest, asking $390,000.</p><div class="rn-sheet" role="group" aria-label="The numbers sheet for the middle 3-bedroom house for sale in Carolina Forest"><div class="rn-sheet-h"><p>Numbers sheet: 3-bedroom house, Carolina Forest</p><span>Asking $390,000</span></div><div class="rn-line"><b>Rent</b><em>$26,760</em><small>$2,230 a month: HUD's fair market rent for 3 bedrooms in ZIP code 29579.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Property tax</b><em>−$4,700</em><small>About $1,206 for each $100,000 of the price, the rate a rental pays outside city limits, once the county resets the value after the sale.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>HOA dues</b><em>−$1,200</em><small>$100 a month: the middle HOA fee of 3-bedroom houses for sale in 29579.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Insurance</b><em>your quote</em><small>Priced house by house: add your quote. Flood is a separate policy.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Management</b><em>−$2,676</em><small>Our allowance: 10 percent of the rent.</small></div><div class="rn-line minus"><b>Repairs and empty months</b><em>−$2,676</em><small>Our allowance: 10 percent of the rent.</small></div><div class="rn-total"><b>Left a year, before insurance and any loan</b><strong>about $15,500</strong><small>4.0 percent of the price: the cap rate. Our target is 6 percent.</small></div><ul class="rn-chips"><li>The 1 percent rule would need a price of $223,000</li><li>Rent before any cost: 6.9 percent of the price a year</li></ul></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For an address you send, the rent comes from nearby rentals, and the dues and rules from the association's papers. We can get you an insurance quote for the house before you make an offer.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The same sheet on three homes</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The condo keeps the least, because its dues take more than a third of the rent.</p><figure class="rn-rent"><span class="cap">What is left of a year's rent: 58 and 61 percent on the houses, 20 percent on the condo</span><ul class="rn-key" aria-hidden="true"><li><i style="background:#1c2028"></i>Property tax</li><li><i style="background:#5c6b78"></i>HOA dues</li><li><i style="background:#9aa4ad"></i>Management</li><li><i style="background:#c9ced3"></i>Repairs and empty months</li><li><i style="background:#c4783a"></i>Left before a loan</li></ul><div role="img" aria-label="3-bedroom house, Carolina Forest: Property tax 18 percent, HOA dues 4 percent, Management 10 percent, Repairs and empty months 10 percent, Left before a loan 58 percent. 3-bedroom house, Longs: Property tax 14 percent, HOA dues 4 percent, Management 10 percent, Repairs and empty months 10 percent, Left before a loan 61 percent. 2-bedroom condo, inside the City of Myrtle Beach: Property tax 22 percent, HOA dues 39 percent, Management 10 percent, Repairs and empty months 10 percent, Left before a loan 20 percent."><div class="rn-home"><div class="rn-hl"><strong>3-bedroom house, Carolina Forest</strong>, asking $390,000, rent $2,230 a month</div><div class="rn-bar"><span style="width:17.57%;background:#1c2028;color:#fff">18%</span><span style="width:4.48%;background:#5c6b78;color:#fff"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#9aa4ad;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#c9ced3;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:57.94%;background:#c4783a;color:#1c2028">58%</span></div><div class="rn-hl rn-sub">Cap rate: 4.0 percent of the price a year, before insurance and any loan</div></div><div class="rn-home"><div class="rn-hl"><strong>3-bedroom house, Longs</strong>, asking $320,000, rent $2,270 a month</div><div class="rn-bar"><span style="width:14.17%;background:#1c2028;color:#fff">14%</span><span style="width:4.41%;background:#5c6b78;color:#fff"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#9aa4ad;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#c9ced3;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:61.43%;background:#c4783a;color:#1c2028">61%</span></div><div class="rn-hl rn-sub">Cap rate: 5.2 percent of the price a year, before insurance and any loan</div></div><div class="rn-home"><div class="rn-hl"><strong>2-bedroom condo, inside the City of Myrtle Beach</strong>, asking $240,000, rent $1,420 a month</div><div class="rn-bar"><span style="width:21.51%;background:#1c2028;color:#fff">22%</span><span style="width:38.73%;background:#5c6b78;color:#fff">39%</span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#9aa4ad;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:10.00%;background:#c9ced3;color:#1c2028"></span><span style="width:19.75%;background:#c4783a;color:#1c2028">20%</span></div><div class="rn-hl rn-sub">Cap rate: 1.4 percent of the price a year, before insurance and any loan</div></div></div><figcaption>The middle asking price and dues for each kind of home in its ZIP code, HUD's rent, the tax a rental pays there, and our 10 percent allowances. Not a value for any one home.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="one-percent-rule"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The 1% rule">Does the 1 percent rule work in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Almost never for a house. The rule asks for a month's rent of at least 1 percent of the price. Of 2,975 houses with 3 or more bedrooms for sale in Horry County, 14 pass. Longs has 8 of them. We ran this count on November 1, 2026.</p><div class="rn-onerow"><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Longs comes closest: houses for sale whose yearly rent is at least 8 percent of the price"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Longs comes closest: houses for sale whose yearly rent is at least 8 percent of the price</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Area, houses that pass the 1% rule</span><span role="columnheader">houses at 8 percent or more</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Longs</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">8 pass the 1% rule</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.563" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">189 of 336</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Loris</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.2" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">30 of 150</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Little River</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.187" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">34 of 182</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Carolina Forest</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">3 pass the 1% rule</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.181" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">66 of 365</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Aynor</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">1 pass the 1% rule</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.143" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">6 of 42</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Conway (29527)</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">2 pass the 1% rule</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.123" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">24 of 195</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Galivants Ferry</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.095" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">6 of 63</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Socastee</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.095" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">25 of 264</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Conway (29526)</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.075" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">35 of 467</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Murrells Inlet and Garden City</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.028" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">6 of 216</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.017" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">3 of 181</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Surfside Beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.012" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">2 of 161</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">North Myrtle Beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.004" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">1 of 239</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach, north end</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">0 of 100</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Houses with 3 or more bedrooms for sale, each against HUD's fair market rent for its ZIP code and bedrooms. The 1 percent rule asks for 12 percent a year. The bar uses a lower test, 8 percent, to show where the rent comes closest. Not shown: Nichols, 14 houses, none passing.</figcaption></figure><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.6rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:620px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/invest-run-the-numbers/carolina-forest-houses-from-above-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/invest-run-the-numbers/carolina-forest-houses-from-above-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/invest-run-the-numbers/carolina-forest-houses-from-above-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 660px) 100vw, 620px" width="1200" height="900" alt="Rows of similar houses with gray roofs, short driveways and small fenced back yards along curving streets in Carolina Forest, seen from above" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCODE-2023-title17/html/USCODE-2023-title17-chap1-sec105.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="U.S. government aerial photo. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">U.S. government aerial photo</a></span></div><figcaption>Houses in The Parks of Carolina Forest, ZIP code 29579, from above. 3 of the 14 houses for sale that pass the 1 percent rule are in this ZIP code.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Condos often pass on price alone, then fail once the dues are counted. In the ZIP codes we tested, about 1 in 8 condos for sale passes on price. Count the dues against the rent, and fewer than 3 in 100 still pass.</p><div class="rn-hard"><p class="rn-hard-h">Get this week's houses that pass the 1 percent rule</p><p>Homes come on and off the market every day, so this count is already old. Call or text us and we run it again that day, then send you every house that passes, with its numbers.</p><div class="rn-hard-b"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us run the count for you</a><a class="rn-hard-tel" href="tel:+18543332135">Call or text 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="tax"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The tax">Why is the tax on the listing not the tax you will pay?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Because the listing shows the seller's bill. After you buy, the county resets the value to about what you paid. A rental also pays a higher rate than a home its owner lives in.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A rental pays about $1,206 a year for each $100,000 of value outside city limits, and about $1,528 in the City of Myrtle Beach. Outside city limits, that is about 3 times what an owner who lives there pays.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A home its owner does not live in, such as a house rented out year-round or a second home, is taxed on <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">6 percent of its value</a>. More on the 4 and 6 percent rates: <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Horry County property tax rates</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We read the county's deeds for this year's resales and the value each seller was taxed on. The middle home sold for about 1.2 times that value.</p><figure class="rn-tax"><span class="cap">Your next tax bill outside city limits: about 3.9 times the seller's on an owner's home, 1.1 times on a rental</span><div><div class="rn-tg"><p>Buying a home taxed at 4 percent, as an owner's home</p><div class="rn-tr"><span class="l" aria-hidden="true">The seller's bill: <b>$1,000</b></span><svg class="b" role="img" aria-label="The seller's bill: $1,000" viewBox="0 0 100 10" preserveAspectRatio="none" style="--f:0.250"><rect width="100" height="10" fill="currentColor"/></svg></div><div class="rn-tr you"><span class="l" aria-hidden="true">Your next bill: <b>about $3,900</b></span><svg class="b" role="img" aria-label="Your next bill: about $3,900" viewBox="0 0 100 10" preserveAspectRatio="none" style="--f:0.980"><rect width="100" height="10" fill="currentColor"/></svg></div></div><div class="rn-tg"><p>Buying a home already taxed at 6 percent, such as a rental</p><div class="rn-tr"><span class="l" aria-hidden="true">The seller's bill: <b>$1,000</b></span><svg class="b" role="img" aria-label="The seller's bill: $1,000" viewBox="0 0 100 10" preserveAspectRatio="none" style="--f:0.250"><rect width="100" height="10" fill="currentColor"/></svg></div><div class="rn-tr you"><span class="l" aria-hidden="true">Your next bill: <b>about $1,100</b></span><svg class="b" role="img" aria-label="Your next bill: about $1,100" viewBox="0 0 100 10" preserveAspectRatio="none" style="--f:0.275"><rect width="100" height="10" fill="currentColor"/></svg></div></div></div><figcaption>The middle resale outside city limits, January to September 2026, by the rate on the seller's tax record. Your bill is figured on the sale price, before any exemption. A seller's Homestead exemption would make the jump larger.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Buy a home taxed at 4 percent and rent it out, outside city limits, and your next bill is about 3.9 times the seller's. Buy a home already taxed at 6 percent, such as a rental from another landlord, and it is about 1.1 times. In our experience, taxes stop more rental deals than insurance does.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">When the new bill starts</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you buy between January and September, your first October bill still uses the seller's value, and the next one uses the new value. Buy in October or later, and your first bill already uses the new value. <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/tax-payer-services/real-property-tax/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Bills are mailed October 1</a> and are due by January 15.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A rental bought from another landlord can get a break. The 25 percent exemption applies only when the home was taxed at 6 percent before the sale and stays at 6 percent after it. Then a quarter of its new value is exempt, but the taxed value cannot fall below the county's current market value.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The owner or the owner's agent must notify the county assessor before January 31 of the first tax year it applies. We remind you before the deadline.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Every town's rate is in our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax calculator</a>.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want the tax for one address?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We work out the bill you would pay as a landlord, from the county's record for that home and the rate in its town.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#run-form">Have us work out a rental's tax</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="condo-dues"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Condo dues">What does a condo's HOA fee do to the numbers?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It takes a large share of the rent. More than half of condos for sale on the Grand Strand list dues over $600 a month.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a 2-bedroom condo, the middle dues take from about a quarter of HUD's rent in Carolina Forest to about half at the north end of Myrtle Beach.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="HOA dues take 23 to 48 percent of HUD's rent on the middle 2-bedroom condo"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">HOA dues take 23 to 48 percent of HUD's rent on the middle 2-bedroom condo</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Area, dues of the rent, a month</span><span role="columnheader">dues, share of the rent</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Carolina Forest</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$425 of $1,840</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.23" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">23% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Little River</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$500 of $1,730</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.29" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">29% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Surfside Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$500 of $1,590</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.31" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">31% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Murrells Inlet and Garden City</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$525 of $1,720</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.31" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">31% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$550 of $1,420</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.39" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">39% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">North Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$600 of $1,550</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.39" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">39% of the rent</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Myrtle Beach, north end</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">$725 of $1,500</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.48" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">48% of the rent</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>The middle monthly dues of 2-bedroom condos for sale in each ZIP code, against HUD's fair market rent for 2 bedrooms there.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The dues are why the condo in the sheet above keeps so little. Our <a href="/invest/condo-buildings/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">condo buildings page</a> lists dues building by building. A condo rented by the night has different numbers: our <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Airbnb income page</a> has them by area, and <a href="/invest/condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our condo guide</a> covers the building rules.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">One condo's dues, set against its rent</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us a condo you like. We get its dues and rental rules from the association's papers and show you what is left of the rent.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#run-form">Have us look at a condo's dues</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="ratios"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The ratios">What cap rate, cash-on-cash return and coverage should you aim for here?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our targets are a cap rate of 6 percent or more, coverage of 1.25 or more, and prices that rise 3 percent a year or more. Cap rates by area are on our <a href="/invest/rental-returns/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental returns page</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="costs"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Every cost">What are the expenses on a rental property here?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Six come out of the rent on every long-term rental: the property tax, insurance, HOA dues, management, repairs and empty months. A nightly rental adds its own taxes and licenses.</p><div class="rn-costs"><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Cost</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">What it is here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Property tax</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">About $1,206 a year for each $100,000 of value outside city limits, and $1,528 in the City of Myrtle Beach, on a value reset to about the price after you buy. <a href="#tax" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Why the listing's tax is not yours</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Insurance</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Priced house by house. Flood is a separate policy. <a href="/invest/landlord-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Flood and wind on a rental</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>HOA dues</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The middle 3-bedroom house for sale pays $25 to $300 a month, by area. Condo dues are in <a href="#condo-dues" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the section above</a>.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Management</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">We allow 10 percent of the rent.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Repairs and empty months</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">We allow 10 percent of the rent.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Nightly rental taxes and licenses</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Only on a short-term rental. <a href="/invest/accommodations-tax/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">The accommodations tax</a>, by town.</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)"><strong>Depreciation, a tax saving</strong></td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">The building, not the land, is depreciated over <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">27.5 years</a> for federal tax. <a href="/invest/rental-depreciation/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">How it works</a>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">An insurance quote before you offer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Insurance is priced house by house. Pick a home, and we get you a quote on it before you make an offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us get an insurance quote</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-we-check"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What we check">What can a calculator not see?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The community's rules, how rents keep up with prices, and the seller's own numbers. These are three things we check for you before you offer.</p><div class="rn-cards rn-plain"><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 21h18M5 21V9.5M19 21V9.5M5 9.5C7 6.8 9.4 5.5 12 5.5s5 1.3 7 4M12 5.5V21M8.5 7v14M15.5 7v14M5 15h14"/></svg></span>A rental ban</h3><p>Some communities here ban renting the house at all. We filter those out when we search for you, and we read the HOA papers for rules that would block your plan.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span>Prices rose, the rent did not</h3><p>An agent now on our team helped buyers purchase about eight nearly identical townhouses in a new Little River community. Each rented for a little over $2,000.</p><p>Prices rose from $230,000 to $292,000 over about 18 months. The rents stayed flat, and the cash flow went away for later buyers.</p></div><div class="rn-card"><h3><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span>The seller's cash flow is not yours</h3><p>An agent at Chapter3 sold a rented house for an owner of 15 years. His cash flow was over $1,200 a month. The next investor would make about $600, because of higher taxes and a bigger loan.</p><p>The agent marketed it to investors who accept a modest cash flow in an area where prices are rising. A cash buyer closed 13 days after it was listed.</p></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="run-form"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Send an address">What should you send us?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The address is enough. What investors ask us most is "send me properties with analytics to prove it", so that is what you get back.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You get the sheet above for that home. It has the rent from nearby rentals, the tax at the rate you will pay, and the dues and the building's rules. The sheet is free, and most come back the same day, evenings included.</p><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">The address, or the MLS number. Any home on the Grand Strand, listed with any brokerage, or one not for sale yet.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Your plan: long-term rental, short-term rental, mid-term rental or fix and flip. Not sure is fine.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">How you plan to pay, cash or a loan. We do not need your finances to run the home.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">Whether you will stay in it yourself. <a href="/invest/14-day-rule/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">How your own stays change the tax</a>.</li></ul><div id="rnWrap" style="max-width:680px;background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:14px;padding:1.6rem;text-align:left;margin:1.4rem 0">
 <div class="idx-lead" style="grid-template-columns:1fr"><input class="idx-input" id="rnAddr" placeholder="Property address or MLS number" aria-label="Property address or MLS number"></div>
