+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is Airbnb or a long-term rental more profitable in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Airbnb, once guests book a two-bedroom about 82 nights a year and you run it yourself. Below that, you keep more with a lease at HUD's standard rent, and many listings book fewer nights."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you Airbnb a house in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Inside city limits, only in the Residential Multifamily Visitor zone; the other residential zones allow only stays of 90 days or more. In Carolina Forest and the rest of unincorporated Horry County, the county's zoning has no short-term rental rule. A home's HOA rules may still not allow nightly stays. The license, the tax and every town's rules are on our short-term rental rules page."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How many nights does a Myrtle Beach Airbnb need to book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"About 82 a year on a two-bedroom at $252 a night, if you run it yourself. With a full-service manager at 40 percent, at least about 135."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I pay lodging tax on a long-term lease?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. South Carolina charges its 7 percent state tax only on stays under 90 days, plus local taxes. Two exceptions to the tax: a home rented out 14 days or fewer in a year, and rooms in the owner's own home with fewer than six rented."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a short-term or long-term rental easier to finance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A lease. A loan officer at our preferred lender tells us investor lenders count only about 75 percent of the appraiser's estimate of a nightly rental's income."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does Chapter3 manage rentals?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Chapter3 is a brokerage and does not manage rentals. A full-service manager here takes about 40 to 60 percent, in what we have seen."}}]}</script>
