+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/invest/strategies/1031-exchange/#article","headline":"1031 exchange in South Carolina: the 45 and 180 days, the state's hold-back and Form I-295, and what an exchange buys here","description":"1031 exchange in South Carolina: the 45 and 180 day rules, the Form I-295 box for the 5.21% hold-back, a Myrtle Beach example, a date tool and what to buy.","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/invest/strategies/1031-exchange/","author":{"@type":"Person","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/about/#devin-day","name":"Devin Day","jobTitle":"Operations Officer","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/about/","worksFor":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"}},"reviewedBy":{"@type":"Person","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/about/#timmy-nash","name":"Timothy Nash","alternateName":"Tim Nash","jobTitle":"Broker-in-Charge","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/about/","worksFor":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"}},"publisher":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/#org"},"datePublished":"2026-06-13","dateModified":"2026-11-12","inLanguage":"en-US","image":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-b/invest-strategies-1031-exchange/share.jpg","mainEntityOfPage":{"@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/invest/strategies/1031-exchange/"},"about":{"@type":"Thing","name":"A 1031 exchange of investment real estate in South Carolina, and the state's withholding for a seller who lives in another state"},"keywords":"1031 exchange South Carolina, South Carolina 1031 exchange rules, SC I-295 1031, 1031 exchange 45 day rule, 1031 exchange 180 days, 1031 exchange Myrtle Beach, South Carolina reverse 1031 exchange, delayed 1031 exchange, 1031 exchange vacation home, 1031 exchange properties for sale Myrtle Beach"}</script>
+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a 1031 exchange have to be in the same state?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. The rules are federal. Real property in the United States is generally like-kind to other US real property if both are held for business or investment. A rental in another state can be traded for one here."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I 1031 exchange into a home I will live in?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not one you only live in. A beach condo you rent out and also use can qualify when it passes the IRS rental test described on this page."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I do a 1031 exchange with a family member, such as a parent or a child?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, with a limit. It covers a spouse, parent, grandparent, child, grandchild, brother or sister, and a company you control. If either of you sells the property you got within two years, the deferred gain is taxed. There are a few exceptions, such as a death."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does South Carolina tax the gain later?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The state follows the federal rules, so the gain you put off is taxed when you sell without exchanging. South Carolina deducts 44 percent of a net capital gain and taxes the rest."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is a company that sells a condo held back too?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. For a corporation the rate is 5 percent. A business seller can be treated as a resident on Form I-295 if it meets four tests. It has done business in South Carolina for the last two tax years and will keep doing it. It is registered here and has filed a South Carolina return with none overdue."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I exchange into a DST?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. A share of a Delaware statutory trust can be the replacement if the trust stays inside the IRS ruling's limits and the other 1031 rules are met."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who reports a 1031 exchange to the IRS?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You do, on IRS Form 8824 with your tax return for the year of the exchange."}}]}</script>
