* var(--f));border-radius:999px;background:linear-gradient(90deg,#c4783a,#d4894a);box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.35)}.c3-gchart .ref .b{background:#5c6b78}.c3-gchart .v{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;white-space:nowrap}.c3-gchart .ref .n{font-weight:500}.c3-gchart figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.95rem;line-height:1.55;margin:1.1rem 0 0;padding:0}@media (max-width:699px){.c3-gchart{padding:1rem .95rem .9rem}.c3-gchart .b{width:calc((100% - 6.2rem) * var(--f))}}</style><style>@media (min-width:700px){main#main .detail-h1{text-wrap:balance}}main#main .detail-h1 em{display:block;font-size:clamp(1.3rem,2.5vw,2.25rem);line-height:1.25;margin-top:.55rem;text-wrap:balance}@media (max-width:959px){main#main .detail-h1 em{color:#8a5226!important}}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most resell for more than the earlier deed price. We read every deed Horry County recorded for two years. Quick resales, a home sold again within 12 months of its last sale, were about 3 of every 100 home sales not made by a builder. The chart gives the middle gain: half gained more, half less.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Middle gain on a home resold within 12 months, Horry County"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Middle gain on a home resold within 12 months, Horry County</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Who resold it, how many</span><span role="columnheader">middle gain</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Resold by a company</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">about 140 resales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$75,000</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Every quick resale</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">344 resales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.509" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$38,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Resold by a person</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">about 200 resales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.2" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$15,000</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>The gain between the two deed prices, before repairs, loans, taxes, insurance and both closings. Resales recorded in the 12 months to September 2026. The count is a floor: some sales that may have been a lot that became a new house were left out.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The company figure is the closer guide to an investor's flip. Resales by a person hold most of the losses: about 55 of the 66 homes that sold for less than the earlier deed price. Houses also gained more than condos, a middle of about $56,000 against about $17,000.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="where"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Where">Where on the Grand Strand do flips happen?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The biggest gains on houses were in Conway, Carolina Forest and Little River. Myrtle Beach had the most quick resales, but most were condos, and the middle one gained about $22,000. No single neighborhood had more than three house resales: the flips are spread across many streets. The deal finder looks at every home for sale for that reason.</p><figure style="margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:760px"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" id="c3r-where-homes-were-resold-" viewBox="0 0 360 388"