dth:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For steady income, the simplest investment strategy here is a house rented by the year, bought with a DSCR loan, which qualifies on the rent. A house listed for rent in the Myrtle Beach area asks about $2,070&nbsp;a&nbsp;month.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Selling a rental with a big gain? A 1031 exchange or a DST puts off the tax. With cash and time for a project, a flip or BRRRR starts with a worn house.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Tell us your cash and your goal. We send properties that fit, with the numbers run.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Send me properties that fit</a></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory);padding-top:2.5rem"><div class="wrap"><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:0;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#which-fits" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Which fits you</a></li><li><a href="#strategies" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The eight strategies</a></li><li><a href="#how-much" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What each costs</a></li><li><a href="#pairs" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">The pairs</a></li><li><a href="#how-we-help" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">How we find deals</a></li><li><a href="#investment-strategies-faq" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Investment strategies FAQ</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="which-fits"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Which fits you">Which real estate investment strategy fits you?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">DSCR purchases and 1031 trades are the most common we see here, and BRRRR is the rarest, because the right property is rare.</p><div class="st-tw"><table class="st-tab"><caption>Start from what you want the money to do</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">You want</th><th scope="col">The strategy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Monthly rent, with the least work</td><td><a href="#long-term-rental" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Long-term rental</a>, with a <a href="#dscr-loan" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">DSCR loan</a></td></tr><tr><td>The most a home can earn, and you accept the work</td><td><a href="#short-term-rental" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Short-term rental</a></td></tr><tr><td>Several rents from one building, or you live in one unit</td><td><a href="#small-multifamily" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Small multifamily</a></td></tr><tr><td>One sum of cash, used on house after house</td><td><a href="#brrrr" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">BRRRR</a></td></tr><tr><td>A project you finish and sell</td><td><a href="#fix-and-flip" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Fix and flip</a></td></tr><tr><td>You are selling a rental with a big gain</td><td><a href="#1031-exchange" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">1031 exchange</a></td></tr><tr><td>The same, and you are done being a landlord</td><td><a href="#dst" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">DST</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="strategies"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The eight strategies">What are the main real estate investment strategies here?</h2><p class="st-group">Buy a home and hold it</p><div class="st-entry"><h3 class="st-h" id="long-term-rental" data-no="1">Long-term rental</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A long-term rental is a home leased by the year. Its rent comes in every month, in winter as in summer. It fits an investor who wants monthly income with the least to manage.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="/invest/rent-prices/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our rent prices guide</a> has rents by town and size, and <a href="/invest/long-term-rental/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the rental analyzer</a> works out one address. A local manager can take the calls; <a href="/invest/property-management/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our property management guide</a> covers what to expect.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Many communities here ban renting a house at all, and we filter them out of a search. We also read the homeowners association (HOA) documents for rules that would block your rental plan.</p><p class="hard" style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The hard part is the tax. A rental or a second home is <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">taxed on 6 percent of its value</a>. The 4 percent rate is only for the home you live in, once the county approves it. The rate is lost if that home is rented out more than 72 days in a calendar year.</p></div><div class="st-entry"><h3 class="st-h" id="short-term-rental" data-no="2">Short-term rental</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A short-term rental is a home rented by the night, often through Airbnb. The average Airbnb in the Myrtle Beach area took in about $26,000 from guests over a year, before costs.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Murrells Inlet's average Airbnb took in about $48,000, the most of the eight Grand Strand towns we track. Nightly rentals fit an owner who wants the most a home can earn and accepts the work, or pays a manager.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our Airbnb income guide</a> has the figures by town and by size.</p><p class="hard" style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The hard part is the rules. In the City of Myrtle Beach, a rental under 90 days is short-term, and <a href="https://www.cityofmyrtlebeach.com/news_detail_T6_R1468.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">most of the city's traditional neighborhoods are not zoned for it</a>. Check the zoning before you buy. <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our short-term rental rules guide</a> covers each town.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="str-garden-city-beach-condos" data-name="Garden City Beach" data-head="Best Airbnb opportunities in Garden City Beach"></div><div class="hx-fallback"><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.3rem 0 1.5rem;max-width:720px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/garden-city-condos-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/garden-city-condos-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/garden-city-condos-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 720px" width="1200" height="675" alt="A tall tan condo tower and several low-rise condo buildings with pools on the beach at Garden City, with beach houses and a wide salt marsh behind them" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: USGS. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: USGS</a></span></div><figcaption>Condo buildings on the beach at Garden City Beach, with the salt marsh behind them.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="st-entry"><h3 class="st-h" id="small-multifamily" data-no="3">Small multifamily</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Small multifamily is one building with two to four homes: a duplex, triplex or fourplex. Here, many of them stand near the ocean. Nearly 3 in 4 of the county's buildings of 2 to 4 homes on one parcel are within a mile of it, in a straight line.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County recorded 58 sales of buildings with 2 to 4 homes in the past year, a few buildings twice, at a middle price of about $520,000.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It fits an investor who wants several rents from one building. If you live in one unit, <a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/default/files/Housing/documents/40001-hsgh-Update-18.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">an FHA</a> or <a href="https://www.va.gov/housing-assistance/home-loans/loan-types/purchase-loan/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VA loan</a> can buy a building of up to four units. <a href="/invest/strategies/small-multifamily/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our small multifamily guide</a> has prices by town and rents by unit.</p><p class="hard" style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The hard part on an FHA loan: <a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/dfiles/OCHCO/documents/92561.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">it bars renting any unit</a> for under 30 days.</p></div><div class="hx-strip" data-place="duplexes-around-myrtle-beach" data-name="the Myrtle Beach area" data-head="Two to four rents from one building, for sale now"></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Have us run the numbers on any address.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us a house, a condo or a duplex you like. We work out the rent, the tax and the HOA rules on it before you offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us run an address</a></div><p class="st-group">Pay for it</p><div class="st-entry"><h3 class="st-h" id="dscr-loan" data-no="4">DSCR loan</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A DSCR loan is not a property. It is the loan many investors here use for a rental and for the BRRRR refinance. The lender qualifies the loan on the rent, not on your income.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A house rented by the year fits it well. Take a middle-priced 3-bedroom house in Longs or Carolina Forest, outside city limits. Its tax, insurance and HOA dues take about a third of <a href="https://www.huduser.gov/portal/datasets/fmr.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD's rent figure</a> for its ZIP, which leaves room for the loan.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><a href="/invest/strategies/dscr-loans/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our DSCR loan guide</a> works out the ratio on six typical homes here, from a Longs house to an oceanfront condo.</p><p class="hard" style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Nightly rentals are harder. Take a typical oceanfront two-bedroom condo in Myrtle Beach, booked as often as the average listing. Its dues, tax and insurance alone come to more than the income a lender counts, before any loan payment.</p></div><p class="st-group">Add value</p><div class="st-entry"><h3 class="st-h" id="brrrr" data-no="5">BRRRR</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">BRRRR stands for buy, rehab, rent, refinance, repeat. You buy a worn house, fix it and rent it. A refinance then takes your cash back out for the next one.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most good BRRRR candidates here are lower-value homes inland toward Longs or Little River, or condo units in a stable building with a distressed owner.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On one house here, about $90,000 of work took its value from about $180,000 to about $384,000 in about three months. It is now a short-term rental. Those are that investor's numbers, not a promise. <a href="/invest/strategies/brrrr/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our BRRRR guide</a> and <a href="/invest/foreclosures/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our foreclosures guide</a> cover where the worn houses come from.</p><p class="hard" style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">BRRRR is rare because the worn house must also sit where rents are high, since the strategy relies on high rents. If the house appraises low at the refinance, part of your cash stays in it.</p></div><div class="st-entry"><h3 class="st-h" id="fix-and-flip" data-no="6">Fix and flip</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A fix and flip is a worn house you buy, repair and sell. Horry County recorded at least 344 quick resales, a home resold within 12 months of its last sale, in the past year.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When a company resold, the middle gain between the two deed prices was about $75,000, before costs. By ZIP area, houses gained most in the Conway, Carolina Forest and Little River areas.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It fits an investor with cash, a contractor and a few months. <a href="/invest/strategies/fix-and-flip/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our fix and flip guide</a> has the gains by area.</p><p class="hard" style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not every quick resale gains. Of those resales, 66 sold for less than the earlier deed price, most of them by people rather than companies.</p></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want the worn houses before they are fixed?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We send worn houses priced under their fixed-up value, in the areas where rent is strong, with our numbers on each.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Get the worn-house list</a></div><p class="st-group">Trade without the tax now</p><div class="st-entry"><h3 class="st-h" id="1031-exchange" data-no="7">1031 exchange</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A 1031 exchange lets you sell one rental, buy another and <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1031" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">put off the tax</a> on the gain. Name the replacement within 45 days of the sale, and own it within 180 days or by your tax return's due date with extensions, if that comes first.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One of Tim Nash's longtime clients, an investor based in New York, owns more than 200 properties, mostly condos. When a condo stops giving him depreciation, the yearly tax deduction for a building's wear, he sells it and trades up through a 1031 exchange.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 3 in 10 homes sold in Horry County in the past year went to buyers whose tax bill goes to another state or country. <a href="/invest/strategies/1031-exchange/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our 1031 exchange guide</a> has the steps and the state's rules for a seller from another state.</p><p class="hard" style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The hard part is the dates. Miss day 45 or day 180, and the tax comes due.</p></div><div class="st-entry"><h3 class="st-h" id="dst" data-no="8">DST</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A Delaware statutory trust, or DST, sells shares in large properties that a sponsor manages. A share can be the replacement in a 1031 exchange if the trust stays inside <a href="https://www.irs.gov/irb/2004-33_IRB" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the IRS ruling's limits</a> and the other 1031 rules are met.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">DSTs already own part of the Grand Strand. Ten trusts own 11 parcels in Horry County that the county values at about $200 million. They include two large apartment communities, one near Carolina Forest Boulevard and one in the Clear Pond area.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The usual minimum share is $100,000, and most DSTs sell only to <a href="https://www.sec.gov/resources-small-businesses/capital-raising-building-blocks/accredited-investors" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">accredited investors</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A DST fits an investor who is done being a landlord. <a href="/invest/strategies/dst/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our DST guide</a> has the fees, the minimums and the ways out.</p><p class="hard" style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The hard part is the cost and the wait. In the middle DST offering, sales commissions and finder's fees came to about 6 percent of the money raised. Plan to stay in until the trust sells the property.</p></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Selling a rental to buy the next one?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We time your sale and the next purchase to the 45 and 180 days, and line up the property before you list.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Plan my sale and next buy</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="how-much"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What each costs">What does each way in cost here?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A DST share starts lowest, and a duplex to fourplex costs the most. The cash you need to start depends on the loan. <a href="/invest/strategies/dscr-loans/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our DSCR loan guide</a> has our affiliated lender's down payment figures for a rental.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="The ways in start far apart: from a $100,000 DST share to a $520,000 duplex or fourplex"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">The ways in start far apart: from a $100,000 DST share to a $520,000 duplex or fourplex</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Way in, what the figure is</span><span role="columnheader">Middle price</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A share of a DST</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">usual minimum, not a price</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.192" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$100,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A condo (resale)</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle sale, county deeds</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.413" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$215,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A home other than a condo (resale)</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">mostly houses, middle sale</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.673" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$350,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">A duplex, triplex or fourplex</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle sale, county deeds</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$520,000</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Homes: the middle sale recorded in Horry County in the 12 months to September 2026. DST: the most common minimum in SEC filings over the same 12 months.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="pairs"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The pairs">BRRRR or flip, long-term or nightly, 1031 or DST: how do they differ?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each pair starts the same way and ends differently.</p><ul class="st-plain"><li><b>BRRRR or fix and flip</b>Both start with a worn house. A flip sells it. BRRRR keeps it, rents it and refinances. <a href="/invest/strategies/brrrr/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">The BRRRR guide</a> compares the two.</li><li><b>Fix and flip or rent</b>A flip pays once, when it sells. A rental pays every month, and you keep the house. <a href="/invest/strategies/fix-and-flip/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">The fix and flip guide</a> has the costs.</li><li><b>Long-term or nightly</b><a href="/invest/str-vs-ltr/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Our side-by-side</a> works out the same home both ways, rented by the year and by the night.</li><li><b>1031 exchange or DST</b>Both put off the tax. A 1031 buys a rental you manage. A DST buys a share a sponsor manages. <a href="/invest/strategies/dst/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">The DST guide</a> compares them.</li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="how-we-help"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How we find deals">How does Chapter3 find investment deals here?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A new company can outspend us on ads. It cannot buy friendships that last years, and friends of ours tell us about properties here before they list.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Nothing beats a cash offer. When you need a loan, we can still find properties here through friends before they list, and negotiate terms a seller will take with financing.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">A sale that never listed</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">An agent at Chapter3 met a man at a networking event, and the two became friends. When he fell behind on a condo here, he asked that agent to sell it quietly. It went to an investor on the buyer list without being listed, at about 20 percent under market. That investor now collects the rent.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Get on the buyer list.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Investors ask us most for properties with the numbers that prove them. Tell us what you want, and we send those, including ones that have not listed.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Send me properties with the numbers run</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="investment-strategies-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Investment strategies FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is the best real estate investment strategy for beginners?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For most beginners here, a house rented by the year. Its numbers are the simplest to check, and a DSCR loan qualifies on the rent. With a big gain from a sale to move, a 1031 exchange comes first.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you invest in real estate without being a landlord?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. A DST share is the one way with no landlord work at all, and the usual minimum is $100,000. Most DSTs sell only to accredited investors. A rental with a local property manager also takes the day-to-day calls off your hands.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can Chapter3 help with the loan?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Chapter3 finds and checks the property. Our affiliated lender, BrickWood Mortgage, a separate company, can quote the loan, or you can use your own lender.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Which strategy suits an investor who lives out of state?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A long-term rental with a local manager asks the least of your time among the rentals. A DST asks none.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.zillow.com/research/data/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zillow rent index</a>, <a href="https://www.huduser.gov/portal/datasets/fmr.html" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD rents</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-43</a>, <a href="https://www.airroi.com/airbnb-data/united-states/south-carolina/myrtle-beach" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AirROI</a>, <a href="https://www.cityofmyrtlebeach.com/news_detail_T6_R1468.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">City of Myrtle Beach</a>, <a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/default/files/Housing/documents/40001-hsgh-Update-18.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD 4000.1</a>, <a href="https://www.hud.gov/sites/dfiles/OCHCO/documents/92561.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD-92561</a>, <a href="https://www.va.gov/housing-assistance/home-loans/loan-types/purchase-loan/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">VA</a>, <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">County deeds</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycounty.org/parcelapp/rest/services/HorryCountyGISApp/MapServer/24" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">County parcels</a>, <a href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/1031" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">26 USC 1031</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/irb/2004-33_IRB" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rev. Rul. 2004-86</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/data-research/sec-markets-data/form-d-data-sets" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEC Form D</a>, <a href="https://www.sec.gov/resources-small-businesses/capital-raising-building-blocks/accredited-investors" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SEC</a>. Rent: Zillow's Myrtle Beach metro, which includes Brunswick County, NC, September 2026. Sales: our count of county deeds recorded in the 12 months to September 2026. Airbnb: AirROI, 12 months to September 2026. DST minimums and commissions: our count of SEC filings.</p><style>.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Condo buildings at Garden City Beach. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/garden-city-condos-2011-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/garden-city-condos-2011-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":675,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Condo buildings on the beach at Garden City Beach, with the salt marsh behind them","caption":"Condo buildings on the beach at Garden City Beach, with the salt marsh behind them.","description":"A tall tan condo tower and several low-rise condo buildings with pools on the beach at Garden City, with beach houses and a wide salt marsh behind them","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"U.S. Geological Survey"},"creditText":"U.S","copyrightNotice":"Public domain (U.S. Geological Survey)","license":"https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","isBasedOn":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"North Waccamaw Drive, Garden City Beach, unincorporated Horry County (tax district 610), Garden City Beach, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Legal notice.</strong> Chapter3 Realty Corp is a licensed South Carolina real estate brokerage. We do not lend, manage property or give tax, legal or investment advice. Past results are not a promise of future results.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Send us an address, or tell us your cash and your goal.</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">We will run the rent, the expenses and the association documents before you write an offer, at no cost. Or call 854.333.2135.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Send me investment properties with the numbers</a></div></section></main>
