+# How do you buy a foreclosure in Horry County? The courthouse sale, the deed it passes, and the bank-owned listing.
+By Devin Day , Operations Officer · Reviewed by Tim Nash , Broker-in-Charge · Updated November 12, 2026
+Horry County's foreclosure sale is held at 11 in the morning on the first Monday of each month, for registered bidders with certified funds. Bank-owned homes list on the MLS.
+
+The short answer
+Horry County's foreclosed homes are sold two ways. The master-in-equity court auctions them in Conway, and bank-owned homes, called REO, list on the MLS and sell through an ordinary contract.
+At the courthouse sale:
+
+- Register a week ahead.
+
+- Bid in person at 11 in the morning on the first Monday of each month.
+
+- Pay 5 percent of the bid in certified funds by noon the next day.
+
+- Pay the rest within 30 days.
+
+- If the lender asked for a deficiency judgment, bidding stays open for 30 more days and anyone can outbid you.
+
+The deed carries no warranty and comes subject to unpaid taxes and senior liens. On a bank-owned home, government sellers give owner-occupants the first days. A tenant in the house keeps the lease or gets 90 days. Tim Nash has bought at this sale many times, for himself and for clients.
