+## How does depreciation work when you sell?
+Depreciation is a deduction. You take it every year you own a rental, and it lowers the rental income you pay tax on while you hold the house.
+Only the building counts. Land never depreciates. You divide the building's value by 27.5 and deduct that much a year. On a $339,738 house with a fifth of the price in land, that is about $9,883 a year.
+At a sale the deductions come back. Everything you deducted is added to your gain and taxed at up to 25 percent. That is a higher rate than the long-term rate on the rest of the gain.
+Ten years of $9,883 a year is $98,833. At the top rate that is $24,708 of tax when you sell, before the state's share and before any tax on the appreciation.
+Skipping the deduction does not help you. The tax at a sale is worked out on the depreciation you were allowed to take, not on what you took. Take it.
+A 1031 exchange pushes the whole bill into the next house. Our 1031 page has a client who sells when a house has used up its depreciation and trades the money up. That is a hold decided by the tax rule, not by the price. For an owner who is done managing, a DST is the hands-off 1031 , with its own fees and limits.
