+## How is the rent taxed for a Canadian owner?
+Two ways, and you choose.
+The default The election
+What is taxed The gross rent, with no deductions The net rent, after every expense the property has
+The rate A flat 30 percent The normal U.S. rates on the net
+Who takes it Whoever collects the rent withholds it : a manager Nobody. You file Form 1040-NR and pay what the return shows
 
-- Beazer Homes It became part of Dream Finders Homes in 2026. Before that, its annual report said it owned no mortgage company and named several lenders for each community. It gave 1 to 2 years on workmanship and up to 10 years on certain structural failures.
+How do you make the election? You sign Form W-8ECI and give it to whoever collects your rent, usually the property manager. It does not go to the government. The form carries your U.S. tax number, so get the number first. The manager keeps it on file and stops withholding. You then file a U.S. return each year and pay on the profit. The form runs out at the end of the third year after you sign it, so you send a fresh one.
+The election treats the rent as business income , so the mortgage interest, the taxes, the insurance, the manager and the depreciation all count. Almost every owner makes it. Without the form, the person collecting your rent must withhold 30 percent of every payment and is personally liable if they do not. Managers here will ask for the form before the first tenant pays.
+The U.S. return is due June 15. Canada taxes the same rent as world income and credits the U.S. tax you paid . A rental costing more than C$100,000 also goes on Form T1135 each year; a vacation home used mostly by your family may not. The treaty does not exempt the rent. It lets the U.S. tax it first and makes Canada credit that tax.
 
