+Where to file. An estate is opened in the probate court of the county where the person lived when they died. If they did not live in South Carolina, it can be opened in any county where they owned property.
+In Horry County that is the Probate Court at 1301 Second Avenue in Conway, (843) 915-5370, open Monday to Friday, 8 to 5. You need an appointment to open an estate in person. Whoever has the original will must deliver it to the probate court within 30 days of learning of the death.
+Small estates. South Carolina has a faster route when the estate is worth $45,000 or less after liens. Thirty days after the death, an affidavit signed by the probate judge lets an heir collect money owed to the person who died, their belongings and their stock, without opening an estate. It covers personal property only, so it cannot transfer the house.
+If the whole estate, house included, is $45,000 or less after liens, plus the funeral, last-illness medical bills and the cost of running the estate, a personal representative is still appointed. After the notice to creditors is published, the representative can distribute the estate right away and close it with a sworn statement. The same quick route is open at any value when the personal representative is the only heir, or the only person the will leaves property to. Your probate attorney can tell you whether either one fits.
 
