-## What is the master's deed, and what does it carry?
-The master-in-equity is the Horry County judge who runs the foreclosure sale. The master's deed is the deed that judge signs to the winning bidder after the sale. It carries less than the deed in an ordinary sale. The master's deed is not a warranty deed . It passes the rights the court adjudged to be sold , and nothing more. The liens of everyone named in the case end at the sale. A lien held by someone who was not in the case survives, including a prior mortgage that was not foreclosed.
-Property taxes are a first lien . The state's court of appeals has held that property cannot be sold in foreclosure free of the existing tax liens unless provision is made to pay them. The county's sheet says the same in one sentence: unpaid taxes are the successful bidder's. The judgment lists the liens, taxes and other rights the property is sold subject to, so read it before you bid.
-The court has no keys, no knowledge of the condition and no access to give you. The county advises an attorney's certification of title, based on a title search, before you bid. We get the quote for that certification before you register, and a lender wants the same quote with its own list of what the attorney must certify. On a master-in-equity deed the buyer pays the deed recording fee , $1.85 for each $500 of value. On an ordinary sale the seller pays it.
-The court's own guide tells the owner they may stay until the home is sold and the deed issues. The foreclosure chapter of the state code contains no redemption period after that. The tax sale is different. It has a 12-month redemption period.
