-## Horry County tax escrow: billed in arrears, set at your rate
-Horry County bills property taxes after the fact, not up front: bills go out around October 1 and are due January 15, and penalties start after that. At closing the seller credits you for their share of the year, and your lender starts collecting escrow months ahead of the first bill it will pay. That is why the escrow deposit can be the biggest line on the statement.
-The expensive mistake is letting anyone set that escrow from the seller's old bill. Your first full bill will be computed on your price and your rate class. It is 4 percent with the school-operating exemption if this is your legal residence and you file the application. It is 6 percent with the full school tax if it is not. On a $400,000 second home, that is the difference between roughly $1,500 and $4,800 or more a year, depending on the district. The calculator above escrows at your future rate on purpose; the complete math lives in our Horry County property tax calculator .
+## Which repairs can stop a house sale in Myrtle Beach?
+The repairs that matter most are the ones a buyer's lender or insurer will not accept. Three inspections look for them: the appraisal for the buyer's loan, the insurer's four-point inspection and the termite report. Each one looks at different parts of the house:
+What a buyer's lender, insurer and termite inspector look at Part of the house FHA loan appraiser Insurer's four-point inspection CL‑100 termite report
+Roof At least 2 years of life left, and no leaks. If not, a roofer must inspect it. Its age, its years of life left and the date of the last roofing permit Not checked
+Heating and air Heat that keeps the house at 50 degrees or more. Air conditioning, if the house has it, must work. The system's age and the year it was last updated, with a photo of its dated label Not checked
+Water heater and pipes A pressure-relief valve and its pipe on the water heater The water heater's age, its relief valve and any leak; the type of pipe, including polybutylene, a plastic pipe Not checked
+Wiring No frayed or bare wires The panel's brand, model and age, and any older kind of wiring: aluminum, cloth-covered or knob-and-tube Not checked
+Termites Any sign of termites, or of a past treatment, means a pest inspection Not checked Termites and other wood-destroying insects, and their damage
+Wood under the house Not checked Not checked Wood moisture of 20 percent or more, and rot below the main floor and at the bottoms of outside doors, steps and porch posts
 
-VA buyers
+The lender column uses the rules for an FHA loan, a home loan insured by the Federal Housing Administration. Its appraiser's checklist is written down in detail, so this page uses it as the example. The appraiser runs the heat, the air conditioning and the plumbing. The lender decides which repairs are required.
+A buyer may use a regular loan instead. Fannie Mae is a company that buys home loans from lenders. It does not buy one on a house with damage that affects its safety, soundness or structure until the damage is fixed. A cash buyer has no lender to satisfy.
 
-## VA loans: the funding fee and the fees veterans never pay
-The VA funding fee for purchases is a one-time percentage of the loan. Two things set it: whether it is your first VA use, and how much you choose to put down. A first use with nothing down is high on the schedule, and a second use with nothing down is the highest tier. A larger down payment lowers it. The fee is almost always financed rather than paid in cash. Your lender quotes the exact figure with your Loan Estimate. Veterans receiving service-connected disability compensation, Purple Heart recipients on active duty, and eligible surviving spouses are exempt.
-VA rules also cap what lenders can charge. With a 1 percent flat origination charge, the lender cannot also itemize processing, underwriting, or document fees. A veteran can never be charged a tax service fee, lender attorney fees, or prepayment penalties. Where a normally buyer-paid charge is non-allowable, the seller or lender credit picks it up, which is a negotiation point your agent should be making. The full picture is in our VA loan guide .
