-strip:empty{display:none}main#main .hx-strip:not(:empty)+.hx-fallback{display:none}</style></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 12, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">This report covers June 2026. Find the newest numbers in the <a href="/market-reports/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Myrtle Beach market reports</a>, and whether it is a buyer's market today in the <a href="/buyers/buying-in-myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">buying guide</a>.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In June 2026, the median house in Horry and Georgetown Counties sold for about $360,000 and the median condo for about $244,000. Both were within 2 percent of a year earlier.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Compared with a year earlier, fewer houses were for sale, and sellers accepted 21.5 percent more offers on condos.</p><nav aria-label="On this page" style="margin-top:2rem;max-width:720px"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.3rem;font-weight:600">On this page</p><ul style="list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0"><li><a href="#prices" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Prices in June</a></li><li><a href="#were-prices-falling" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Were prices falling</a></li><li><a href="#homes-for-sale" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Homes for sale</a></li><li><a href="#buyers" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Buyers in June</a></li><li><a href="#by-area" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Prices by area</a></li><li><a href="#what-it-meant" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">Using the numbers</a></li><li><a href="#what-else-changed-in-june-2026" style="display:block;padding:.65rem 0;color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline;text-underline-offset:.18em;line-height:1.35">What else changed in June 2026?</a></li></ul></nav></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="prices"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Prices in June">What did Myrtle Beach homes sell for in June 2026?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The median is the middle sale: half of the homes sold for more, and half for less. From January to June, the median house sold for almost the same as in June, and the median condo for $232,500.</p><div class="mj-tw"><table class="mj-tab mj-two"><caption>June 2026 at a glance</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col"><span class="sr">Measure</span></th><th scope="col">Houses</th><th scope="col">Condos</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Homes sold</th><td><span class="l">Houses</span>1,127<span class="was">2025: 1,056</span></td><td><span class="l">Condos</span>498<span class="was">2025: 452</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Median sale price</th><td><span class="l">Houses</span>$359,945<span class="was">2025: $365,000</span></td><td><span class="l">Condos</span>$244,270<span class="was">2025: $239,500</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Accepted offers</th><td><span class="l">Houses</span>1,060<span class="was">2025: 982</span></td><td><span class="l">Condos</span>486<span class="was">2025: 400</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Days from listing to an accepted offer</th><td><span class="l">Houses</span>121<span class="was">2025: 123</span></td><td><span class="l">Condos</span>137<span class="was">2025: 132</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Share of the last asking price paid</th><td><span class="l">Houses</span>97.3%<span class="was">2025: 97.5%</span></td><td><span class="l">Condos</span>96.2%<span class="was">2025: 96.5%</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Homes for sale at the end of the month</th><td><span class="l">Houses</span>4,023<span class="was">2025: 4,163</span></td><td><span class="l">Condos</span>3,294<span class="was">2025: 3,274</span></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Months of supply</th><td><span class="l">Houses</span>4.3<span class="was">2025: 4.6</span></td><td><span class="l">Condos</span>7.6<span class="was">2025: 8.2</span></td></tr></tbody></table><p class="nt">The small figure under each is the June before. Sales through the multiple listing service (MLS) of the Coastal Carolinas Association of REALTORS (CCAR), for Horry and Georgetown Counties. Houses are single-family homes.</p></div><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.4rem 0 1.5rem;max-width:720px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/houses-and-towers-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/houses-and-towers-from-the-ocean-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/submarkets-myrtle-beach/houses-and-towers-from-the-ocean-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 720px" width="1200" height="782" alt="Older two-story houses on lawns along the beach at left, and two tall condo towers on the beach at right, with streets of houses under oak trees behind, seen from a plane over the ocean in Myrtle Beach" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: U.S. Geological Survey. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: U.S. Geological Survey</a></span></div><figcaption>Houses and condo towers on the same beach in Myrtle Beach.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="were-prices-falling"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Were prices falling">Were Myrtle Beach home prices falling in June 2026?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not for houses. The median house sold for about $360,000 to $365,000 in each of the last three Junes.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Condos sold for a little less in each first half. From January to June, the median condo cost 7 percent less than in 2024.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="House prices stayed about level. Condo prices fell a little each year."><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">House prices stayed about level. Condo prices fell a little each year.</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Kind of home and year</span><span role="columnheader">Median sale price</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Houses, 2024</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$363,000</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Houses, 2025</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.999" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$362,584</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Houses, 2026</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.991" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$359,843</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Condos, 2024</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.689" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$250,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Condos, 2025</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.661" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$240,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Condos, 2026</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.64" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$232,500</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Median sale price from January to June of each year, Horry and Georgetown Counties.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Month by month, the median house price stayed between $350,000 and about $375,000 over the year to June.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want these numbers for your own street?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We pull the sales near your house from that June and from the months since, and tell you what they mean for your price.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Send me my street's sales</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="homes-for-sale"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Homes for sale">Did the rise in Myrtle Beach homes for sale stop in June 2026?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, for houses. At the end of June, 3.4 percent fewer houses were for sale than twelve months before. It was the 