%{background:transparent}}}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-swipe{display:none}}.c3ph-cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.75rem;margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;padding:0;list-style:none}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-cards{grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr));gap:1rem}}@media (min-width:1000px){.c3ph-cards.c3ph-4{grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr))}}.c3ph-card{margin:0;background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;display:flex;flex-direction:column}.c3ph-card .c3ph-f{border-radius:0}.c3ph-card img,.c3ph-card svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:var(--c3ph-r,4/3);object-fit:cover}.c3ph-card .c3ph-cr{font-size:.56rem;padding:1.2rem .45rem .25rem 1.6rem}.c3ph-card strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:.98rem;font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;padding:.7rem .8rem 0}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.45;padding:.2rem .8rem .85rem}.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><style>section[style*="background:var(--ivory)"] .c3ph-card{background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-cards{max-width:920px}.c3ph-card strong{font-family:var(--sans)}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{font-size:1rem!important}.c3ph-card strong{font-size:1rem!important}ul[role=list] li span[style*="font-size:.9rem"]{font-size:1rem!important}section#c3-photo{padding-top:0}section:has(+section#c3-photo){background:var(--ivory-2)!important;padding-bottom:2rem}.c3ph-hero{margin:0}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero{max-width:900px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:auto!important;height:auto}.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}.c3-aerial-ctop .c3ph-cr{top:0;bottom:auto;padding:.35rem .6rem 1.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:720px}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Three kinds of buyer make cash offers on houses here:</p><ul class="co-plain" aria-label="Three kinds of cash buyer"><li><b>Online instant-offer buyers</b>A company that makes an offer online from the address, also called an iBuyer.</li><li><b>Local investors</b>People and companies who buy a house to fix up and resell, or to rent out.</li><li><b>"We buy houses" buyers</b>Buyers who send letters, call, or put up roadside signs offering cash for houses. <a href="#check" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">We check them</a> the same way as any other buyer.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Many more buyers here pay without a home loan. We matched the county's <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">deed and mortgage records</a> for a full year of home resales. At least 1 in 3 had no new home loan recorded in the buyer's name, and about 3 in 4 of those buyers were people, not companies. A house listed for sale reaches those buyers too.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="how-much-less"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How much less">How much less is a cash offer, and how do cash buyers set the price?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the middle sale, a company paid about a third less than it later resold the house for.</p><div class="co-side"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The chart shows the middle prices. Companies paid $170,000 for houses they bought from the owners, and resold them for $260,000, about four months later.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Houses companies bought from owners and resold within a year"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Houses companies bought from owners and resold within a year</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Sale</span><span role="columnheader">middle price</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">What the company paid</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.654" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$170,000</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">What it resold for</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$260,000</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Middle prices of 67 resales in Horry County, 12 months to September 2026. The two are separate middles, not one house.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A company that buys to resell has to pay for repairs, months of holding the house, two closings and the costs of selling. It pays for all of them, and its profit, out of the difference between the two prices. Offers vary from house to house.</p></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">What is your house worth as it is, and fixed up?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us the address. We price it both ways from recorded sales near it, so you can judge any cash offer against them.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us price your house two ways</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-you-keep"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What you keep">Cash offer or listing: which leaves you more money?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Listing leaves you more when repairs, fees and time cost less than the cash buyer's discount. Take a house that would sell for $260,000 once fixed up, the middle resale price in our count. A cash offer at the middle share companies paid would be about $178,000.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>Three ways to sell a house that would bring $260,000 fixed up</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">How you sell</th><th scope="col">What it sells for</th><th scope="col">Repairs you pay for</th><th scope="col">Listing fees</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row"><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span>Take the cash offer</th><td data-label="What it sells for">About $178,000</td><td data-label="Repairs you pay for">None</td><td data-label="Listing fees">None</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span>List it as is</th><td data-label="What it sells for">Its as-is price (we work it out)</td><td data-label="Repairs you pay for">None</td><td data-label="Listing fees">$1,780 for each 1% at $178,000</td></tr><tr><th scope="row"><span class="i"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10.8 12.2 20 3M16.8 6.2l2.5 2.5M14.3 8.7l2 2"/><circle cx="7.5" cy="15.5" r="4.5"/></svg></span>Fix it, then list it</th><td data-label="What it sells for">Up to about $260,000</td><td data-label="Repairs you pay for">Yours to pay, and the months they take</td><td data-label="Listing fees">$2,600 for each 1%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Listing fees are a percent you agree with your agent. When each way beats the cash offer:</p><ul class="co-plain" aria-label="When listing beats the cash offer"><li><b>List it as is</b>It leaves you more when its price, less the listing fees, is above the offer. Our count of the county's deeds cannot tell a house sold as it is from one that was fixed up, so it gives no as-is price. We work that price out for your house from the recorded sales near it.</li><li><b>Fix it, then list it</b>It leaves you more when the repairs and the listing fees come to less than about $82,000.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">This is an example on the county's middle figures, not an offer on any house.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our <a href="/sell/net-proceeds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">net proceeds calculator</a> works out what you keep after the loan payoff and every cost. Our page on <a href="/sell/repairs-before-selling/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">repairs before selling</a> shows which repairs pay back, and our <a href="/sell/capital-gains/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">capital gains page</a> covers tax on the sale.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Looking at a cash offer with you is free. We are paid only if you list the house with us and it sells.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="when-cash"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="When cash is better">When is a cash offer the better choice?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A cash offer is the better choice when a buyer with a loan cannot buy the house, or when you need a sure closing date.</p><div class="co-two"><div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A cash sale has no lender, so there is no loan approval or lender's appraisal to wait for. A South Carolina attorney still supervises the title search, the closing and the recording of the deed. Our page on the <a href="/sell/best-time-to-sell/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">best time to sell</a> shows how the wait for a contract changes by month.</p><table class="co-dec"><caption>When a cash offer is the better choice</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">If this is your house</th><th scope="col">The better choice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">It has damage to its safety or structure</th><td>A cash buyer, or a listing marked cash only. Until the damage is repaired, a buyer cannot get a home loan on it that <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-1.3-06/property-condition-and-quality-construction-improvements" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae will buy</a> from the lender. Fannie Mae is the company that buys many home loans from the lenders that make them, so lenders follow its rules.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">No insurer will cover it for wind</th><td>A cash buyer. A home loan backed by Fannie Mae <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b7-3-02/property-insurance-requirements-one-four-unit-properties" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">needs insurance that covers wind</a>, hurricanes included. Our <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">coastal insurance page</a> covers wind policies here.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">It must be sold by a date: a move, an estate or a divorce</th><td>Set a firm cash closing date beside a listing priced to sell. Our pages on an <a href="/sell/inherited-house/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">inherited house</a> and on <a href="/sell/divorce/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">selling in a divorce</a> cover the rest.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A tenant has months left on the lease</th><td>List it in front of investors who buy rented houses. Our <a href="/sell/rental-property/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental property page</a> covers selling with a tenant.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">It is in fair shape, and you have time to sell</th><td>List it, as it is or fixed up.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-10-d/buyers-undisclosed-flooding/conway-florence-floodwater-street-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-10-d/buyers-undisclosed-flooding/conway-florence-floodwater-street-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-10-d/buyers-undisclosed-flooding/conway-florence-floodwater-street-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="844" alt="Floodwater covering a residential street and the yards of single-story houses, with road signs standing in the water, in the Conway area after Hurricane Florence" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-Military-National_Guard" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: SC National Guard. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: SC National Guard</a></span></div><figcaption>Floodwater on a street of houses in the Conway area after Hurricane Florence. Damage to a house's structure has to be repaired before a buyer can close on many home loans.</figcaption></figure></div></div><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want cash buyers without giving up the open market?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We list the house as it is, where every agent's buyers can see it, and send it to the investors on our list.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us list it as is in front of cash buyers</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="as-is"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Selling as is">Can you sell a house as is in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. "As is" means you sell without making repairs. The <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=27&chapter=50" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">state's disclosure form</a> is still required, and a cash sale is not exempt for being cash. Our page on the <a href="/sell/seller-disclosure/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">seller disclosure form</a> goes through it question by question.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Sellers already list houses as cash only</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A seller can list the house as it is and ask for cash buyers only. The local listing feed had these:</p><div class="co-stat co-3"><p class="k">Listed as cash only on November 8, 2026</p><ul><li><strong>22</strong><span>houses</span></li><li><strong>105</strong><span>condos</span></li><li><strong>55</strong><span>manufactured homes</span></li></ul><p class="src">Listings on CCMLS, the local agents' listing service, counted on our site.</p></div><style>main#main .hx-strip:empty{display:none}main#main .hx-strip:not(:empty)+.hx-fallback{display:none}</style><div class="hx-strip" data-place="cash-only-houses" data-name="houses listed cash only" data-head="Houses listed now for cash buyers only"></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A cash offer made before the house is listed is one buyer's offer. A listing puts the house in front of every buyer at once, with a loan or without. The state Real Estate Commission <a href="https://llr.sc.gov/re/RECPDF/Limited%20Market%20Exposure%20Guidance%20Document.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">warns</a> that keeping a home off the open market can mean less competition and a lower price, worse terms or a longer sale.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">One sale to a cash buyer</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One of our agents sold a rented house this way. The owner was moving to New York to be with his sister, and the lease still had eight months left. The next investor would make only about half his monthly cash flow, the rent left after costs, because of higher taxes and a bigger loan.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The agent marketed the house to our list of investors who accept a modest cash flow on a home rising in value. A cash buyer closed within 13 days of the day it was listed. That was his sale and his figures, not a promise. Our <a href="/sell/rental-property/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental property page</a> tells the rest.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="check"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Checking the buyer">How do you check a cash buyer and the contract?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Before you sign a cash buyer's contract, our agents check six things:</p><ol class="co-check" aria-label="What we check before you sign a cash contract"><li><b>Proof of the money</b>A recent bank statement or a letter from the buyer's bank that shows the cash. We ask for it before you sign.</li><li><b>The deposit</b>The buyer's good-faith deposit is called earnest money. A third party, such as an attorney, holds it until closing. If the buyer walks away for a reason the contract does not allow, the seller may keep it. <a href="https://www.nar.realtor/sites/default/files/2025-02/consumer-guide-escrow-and-earnest-money-2025-02-12.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Across the US</a>, no law requires a deposit, and deposits typically run 1 to 10 percent of the price.</li><li><b>The inspection period</b>If the contract <a href="https://myhome.freddiemac.com/blog/homeownership/20210624-understanding-contingencies" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">makes the sale depend on an inspection</a>, the buyer can back out of the sale. A buyer who walks away because a condition of the contract was not met gets the deposit back. A long inspection period with a small deposit means the buyer can back out late at little cost while the house is under contract.</li><li><b>Any right to hand the contract on</b>The words "and/or assigns" after the buyer's name let the buyer pass its place in your contract to someone else. That is not wholesaling by itself. It becomes <a href="#wholesaling" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">wholesaling</a> when the buyer also markets your house before owning it.</li><li><b>Who the buyer is</b>We look the company up by name in the <a href="https://businessfilings.sc.gov/BusinessFiling/Entity/Search" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Secretary of State's business records</a>, where every South Carolina LLC and corporation is filed.</li><li><b>The closing</b>Every South Carolina home closing, cash or loan, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/media/opinions/HTMLFiles/SC/25508.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">must be supervised</a> by a South Carolina attorney.</li></ol><p class="co-warn"><b>Wire fraud.</b> Never follow wiring instructions in an email. Confirm them in person or by phone with the attorney's office, at a number you agreed on in advance.</p><h3 id="wholesaling" style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is wholesaling legal in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No, as the state Real Estate Commission reads the law. Since May 2024, South Carolina's <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=40&chapter=57" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">license law</a> has defined wholesaling. It means having a contract to buy a home from its owner, then marketing it to a different buyer before owning it.</p><ul class="co-plain" aria-label="South Carolina's rules on wholesaling"><li><b>Marketing takes a license</b>Marketing a home someone else owns, for pay, takes a real estate license. In <a href="https://www.llr.sc.gov/re/News/Wholesaling-Assignment-of-Contracts-Guidance.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">its advisory opinion</a>, the Commission reads that as a ban on wholesaling.</li><li><b>What counts as marketing</b>The MLS, social media, email groups and word of mouth. Naming the address, a photo or the neighborhood counts too.</li><li><b>Brokerages</b>A licensed brokerage such as Chapter3 may not take part in wholesaling or help anyone do it.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Commission's opinion is guidance, not a court ruling. If a buyer markets your house before closing, a South Carolina attorney can tell you what it means for your contract.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Got a contract from a cash buyer?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send it to us before you sign. Our agents read the deposit, the inspection period and any right to hand it on, and tell you what each means for you.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Send us the contract before you sign</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="cash-offer-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Cash offer FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Does Chapter3 buy houses for cash?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. Chapter3 Realty is a licensed real estate brokerage. We do not buy houses ourselves; we list and sell them for their owners.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do you pay closing costs in a cash sale?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, at least the state and county deed fee. The seller owes it by law in a cash sale or a listed one: $1.85 for each $500 of the price, about $659 on $178,000. A contract can shift who pays it. Our net proceeds calculator adds the other costs.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can a cash buyer skip the seller disclosure form?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Only if buyer and seller both agree in writing not to complete it. Neither side has to agree. Without that, the state form is required in a cash sale too.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Are "we buy houses" companies legitimate?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We judge no company by name. We check any cash buyer and its contract the same way, in the six steps on this page, before you sign.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County deeds and mortgages</a>, <a href="https://www.zillow.com/research/data/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zillow Research</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=40&chapter=57" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 40-57</a>, <a href="https://www.llr.sc.gov/re/News/Wholesaling-Assignment-of-Contracts-Guidance.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Commission on wholesaling</a>, <a href="https://llr.sc.gov/re/RECPDF/Limited%20Market%20Exposure%20Guidance%20Document.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Commission on market exposure</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=27&chapter=50" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-50</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=12&chapter=24" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-24</a>, <a href="https://www.sccourts.org/media/opinions/HTMLFiles/SC/25508.htm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Doe v. McMaster</a>, <a href="https://www.nar.realtor/sites/default/files/2025-02/consumer-guide-escrow-and-earnest-money-2025-02-12.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NAR earnest money guide</a>, <a href="https://myhome.freddiemac.com/blog/homeownership/20210624-understanding-contingencies" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Freddie Mac contingencies</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-1.3-06/property-condition-and-quality-construction-improvements" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-1.3-06</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b7-3-02/property-insurance-requirements-one-four-unit-properties" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B7-3-02</a>, <a href="https://businessfilings.sc.gov/BusinessFiling/Entity/Search" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC business records</a>, <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/archive/blog/mortgage-closing-scams-how-protect-yourself-and-your-closing-funds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFPB</a>. Our reading of Horry County's deed and mortgage records, October 2025 to September 2026. Timing: Zillow's median days to pending and mean days to close, Myrtle Beach area.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Legal notice.</strong> Chapter3 Realty Corp is a licensed South Carolina real estate brokerage. We are not attorneys, and nothing on this page is legal or tax advice. For advice on a contract or your own sale, talk to a South Carolina attorney.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Floodwater on a street of houses in the Conway area after Hurricane Florence. Photo: Senior Master Sgt. Edward Snyder, 169th Fighter Wing, South Carolina National Guard, <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-Military-National_Guard" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hurricane_Florence_-_South_Carolina_National_Guard_Responds_(44200296354).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty cropped the left edge to hide a mailbox number.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-10-d/buyers-undisclosed-flooding/conway-florence-floodwater-street-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-10-d/buyers-undisclosed-flooding/conway-florence-floodwater-street-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":844,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Floodwater on a street of houses in the Conway area after Hurricane Florence","caption":"Floodwater on a street of houses in the Conway area after Hurricane Florence. Damage to a house's structure has to be repaired before a buyer can close on many home loans.","description":"Floodwater covering a residential street and the yards of single-story houses, with road signs standing in the water, in the Conway area after Hurricane Florence","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Senior Master Sgt. Edward Snyder, 169th Fighter Wing, South Carolina National Guard"},"creditText":"South Carolina National Guard (Senior Master Sgt","copyrightNotice":"Public domain (Senior Master Sgt. Edward Snyder, 169th Fighter Wing, South Carolina National Guard)","license":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Template:PD-USGov-Military-National_Guard","acquireLicensePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hurricane_Florence_-_South_Carolina_National_Guard_Responds_(44200296354).jpg","isBasedOn":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hurricane_Florence_-_South_Carolina_National_Guard_Responds_(44200296354).jpg","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"Conway area, South Carolina (the source does not name the street), Conway, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7r