-</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The direct answer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Can you sell your own house in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. No law requires a listing agent. You will still need a licensed South Carolina attorney to close, because closings here are the practice of law. You still owe the buyer the state property condition disclosure and, on homes built before 1978, the federal lead paint disclosure.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;">About 5 percent of national sales were for sale by owner in the National Association of Realtors&#39; 2025 profile, the lowest share ever recorded, and 91 percent of sellers used an agent. The rest of this page covers both paths honestly, and a third option most sellers have never been offered.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The offer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">FSBO coaching: you do the work, we coach every step</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The third option is coaching. You list and sell the home yourself, and we coach you through it for a flat fee. Pricing from actual sold comps. Prep and photos. The state disclosure, done right. The purchase contract. Checking a buyer&#39;s financing before you sign. Getting the attorney and closing lined up. You do the work, you keep the savings.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Hiring us later costs you nothing extra.</strong> If FSBO stops making sense and you hire us to list, the full coaching fee is credited back. Sell it yourself and the fee is all you paid. Hire us and the coaching cost you nothing. Either way you got the chance to save the commission.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;">The data below is blunt about how FSBO sales usually go. Most of the reasons they fall short, pricing, disclosure, paperwork, are the exact things the coaching covers.</p><div style="background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.2rem 1.3rem;margin-top:1.8rem;max-width:720px;display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between"><p style="margin:0;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.92rem;font-weight:600">Want us to look at it for you?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.7rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Ask about FSBO coaching</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The checklist</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Selling FSBO in South Carolina: do it well</h2><ul style="list-style:none;display:grid;gap:.7rem;margin:1.4rem 0 0;padding:0;max-width:720px"><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Price from sold comps, not asking prices.</strong> Asking prices show what sellers hope for. Sold prices show what buyers paid. We will build your pricing analysis free even if you never list with us: <a href="/sell/home-value/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">get the number here</a>.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Get real photos.</strong> Listings with phone photos read as overpriced at any price. Photography is the cheapest credibility you can buy.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Fill out the state disclosure form completely and honestly the first time.</strong> Guessing is how sellers get sued after closing.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Hire the closing attorney early.</strong> Not at the end. The attorney can find contract problems while they are still fixable.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Verify the buyer&#39;s financing.</strong> A pre-approval letter from a real lender, before you sign, and proof of funds on cash offers.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Answer every inquiry fast.</strong> Buyers working without an agent move on quickly. Same-day responses keep them.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Show safely.</strong> Never host strangers alone without precautions, and keep valuables and medications out of sight.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Never wire money on emailed instructions.</strong> Confirm wiring details by phone with the attorney&#39;s office at a number you looked up yourself.</span></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;">Every item on this list is part of the coaching. If the workload stops being worth it, that is normal, not failure. About one in five FSBO sellers hands it to an agent. We pick it up from wherever you are.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The law</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What South Carolina requires from a FSBO seller</h2><ul style="list-style:none;display:grid;gap:.7rem;margin:1.4rem 0 0;padding:0;max-width:720px"><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">The property condition disclosure.</strong> South Carolina law requires sellers to give buyers a completed Residential Property Condition Disclosure Statement before or at contract signing. Getting it wrong is the classic FSBO lawsuit. Our <a href="/sell/seller-disclosure/" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:underline">seller disclosure guide</a> goes through every question on it.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Lead paint disclosure.</strong> Federal law requires it on any home built before 1978, with the EPA pamphlet and a 10-day inspection window.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">An attorney closing.</strong> South Carolina requires a licensed attorney to conduct the closing. This is not optional for FSBO sellers.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">A real purchase contract.</strong> Deadlines, earnest money, financing and inspection terms, and what happens when one side misses. This is where deals fall apart or get expensive.</span></li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The numbers</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What the data says about FSBO prices</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The National Association of Realtors&#39; 2025 profile puts the median FSBO sale price at 360,000 dollars, against 425,000 dollars for agent-listed homes.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Part of that gap is not about agents at all. 38 percent of FSBO sellers sold to someone they already knew, a relative, friend, or neighbor, and pre-arranged sales are rarely full-price sales. The honest read is that FSBO works best when you already have your buyer. On the open market, the gap shows up in the sale price.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;">Pricing is the number one difficulty FSBO sellers report to NAR. In Clever Real Estate&#39;s 2024 home-selling survey, 51 percent of FSBO sellers said they did not sell for the profit they wanted, against 31 percent of agent-assisted sellers. In the same survey, 43 percent said they made legal mistakes by not using an agent, about one in five ended up hiring an agent anyway, and 47 percent said the process brought them to tears.</p><div style="background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.2rem 1.3rem;margin-top:1.8rem;max-width:720px;display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between"><p style="margin:0;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.92rem;font-weight:600">Want us to look at it for you?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.7rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Ask about FSBO coaching</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Exposure</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Why exposure decides the price</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Around 88 percent of buyers purchase through an agent, and agents find homes on the MLS. A home that is not on the MLS is invisible to most of the buyer pool.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You can pay a flat-fee broker to place a FSBO on the MLS. That fixes visibility. It does not fix pricing, disclosure, or paperwork, which is where the surveys say FSBO sellers lose money.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;">More qualified buyers means competing offers, and competing offers set the price. That is the mechanism behind the higher agent-listed median.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The other path</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What our full-service listing does</h2><ul style="list-style:none;display:grid;gap:.7rem;margin:1.4rem 0 0;padding:0;max-width:720px"><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">MLS plus everywhere buyers look.</strong> Your home goes on the MLS and syndicates to Zillow, Realtor.com, and Homes.com, with professional photography.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Paid buyer marketing, every month.</strong> We spend 2,500 dollars a month on search ads reaching active Grand Strand buyers, and another 2,100 dollars a month on social ads showing our listed homes on Facebook and Instagram.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Content that attracts buyers.</strong> Our team publishes the guides and tools on this site and runs social channels that have drawn more than 3 million combined views in our first three months. Buyer leads from all of it are shown our listings first when they match.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.6"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">A full team, seven days a week.</strong> Four owners, led by a Broker-in-Charge with more than 30 years on the Grand Strand and three decades of past clients and referrals.</span></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;">Model any commission rate against the outcome with our <a href="/sell/net-proceeds/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">net proceeds calculator</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy)"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;padding:3rem 1.5rem"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.9rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:.8rem">Sell it yourself. Keep the help.</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.6);max-width:560px;margin:0 auto 1.6rem;line-height:1.7">Coaching for a flat fee, credited back in full if you hire us to list. Start with the free pricing analysis.</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="#lead-form">Ask about FSBO coaching</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135" style="color:var(--ivory);border-color:rgba(244,239,232,.4)">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">FSBO FAQ</h2><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Can I sell my house without a realtor in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Yes, it is legal. You will still need a licensed South Carolina attorney to close, and you still owe the buyer the state property condition disclosure and, on homes built before 1978, the federal lead paint disclosure.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Do you help FSBO sellers?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Yes. We coach FSBO sellers for a flat fee: pricing from sold comps, prep, the disclosure, the contract, buyer financing checks, and closing preparation. You do the work and keep the savings. If you hire us to list at any point, the full coaching fee is credited back.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Do FSBO homes sell for less?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">On national data, yes. The National Association of Realtors' 2025 profile puts the median FSBO sale at 360,000 dollars against 425,000 dollars for agent-listed homes. Part of the gap is that many FSBO sales go to someone the seller already knew, and pre-arranged sales are rarely full-price sales.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">What paperwork does a FSBO seller need in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">A real purchase contract, the South Carolina Residential Property Condition Disclosure Statement delivered before or at contract signing, the lead paint disclosure for pre-1978 homes, and the closing documents your attorney prepares.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">How do buyers find FSBO homes?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Mostly signs, marketplace sites, and word of mouth. Around 88 percent of buyers work with an agent, and agents search the MLS. A home that is not on the MLS is invisible to most of that pool unless you pay a flat-fee broker to place it there.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">How many people sell FSBO?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">About 5 percent of national sales in the 2025 NAR profile, the lowest share ever recorded, while 91 percent of sellers used an agent. About one in five FSBO sellers ends up hiring an agent before the home sells.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">What is the hardest part of FSBO?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Pricing. It is the number one difficulty FSBO sellers report to NAR. Price high and the home sits and takes price cuts. Price low and the loss is immediate and invisible.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Will you help me even if I do not buy coaching?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Yes. We will prepare your free market analysis either way, so your price is accurate. If you later decide the workload is not worth it, we pick the listing up from wherever you are.</p></div></div></section>
