nt-size="15.5" fill="#1c2028" stroke="#fbf8f3" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="round" paint-order="stroke"><tspan x="180.9" y="86.8">middle</tspan><tspan x="180.9" y="104.6" font-weight="600">$556,000</tspan></text><text x="12" y="131.0" font-size="15.5" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028" stroke="#fbf8f3" stroke-width="5" stroke-linejoin="round" paint-order="stroke">Other sales in its neighborhood</text><circle cx="70.5" cy="148.8" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="90.9" cy="148.8" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="94.8" cy="148.8" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="108.6" cy="148.8" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="12" y="182.9" font-size="15.5" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028" stroke="#fbf8f3" stroke-width="5" stroke-linejoin="round" paint-order="stroke">What it sold for</text><circle cx="140.1" cy="200.7" r="8" fill="#c4783a"/><text x="152.1" y="206.2" text-anchor="start" font-size="15.5" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028" stroke="#fbf8f3" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="round" paint-order="stroke">$482,000</text></svg><svg class="hv-w" viewBox="0 0 600 211" role="img" aria-label="Dot chart on one price line from $250,000 to $750,000. The six nearest sales are dots from $270,000 to $720,000, with their middle at $556,000. The other sales in the house's own neighborhood are dots from $356,000 to $425,000. The house sold for $482,000, more than any of them."><line x1="69.6" x2="69.6" y1="21" y2="207" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".08"/><text x="69.6" y="15" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="#4a4f5c">$300k</text><line x1="300.0" x2="300.0" y1="21" y2="207" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".08"/><text x="300.0" y="15" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="#4a4f5c">$500k</text><line x1="530.4" x2="530.4" y1="21" y2="207" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".08"/><text x="530.4" y="15" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="#4a4f5c">$700k</text><line x1="279.3" x2="279.3" y1="45.3" y2="67.3" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><line x1="279.3" x2="279.3" y1="133.0" y2="155.0" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><line x1="279.3" x2="279.3" y1="183.3" y2="205.3" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 4"/><text x="12" y="39.0" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028" stroke="#fbf8f3" stroke-width="5" stroke-linejoin="round" paint-order="stroke">The six nearest sales</text><circle cx="176.7" cy="56.3" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="553.4" cy="56.3" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="414.0" cy="56.3" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="472.8" cy="56.3" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="35.0" cy="56.3" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="316.1" cy="56.3" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><line x1="364.5" x2="364.5" y1="44.3" y2="68.3" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="3"/><text x="364.5" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" fill="#1c2028" stroke="#fbf8f3" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="round" paint-order="stroke"><tspan x="364.5" y="84.0">middle</tspan><tspan x="364.5" y="101.3" font-weight="600">$556,000</tspan></text><text x="12" y="126.8" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028" stroke="#fbf8f3" stroke-width="5" stroke-linejoin="round" paint-order="stroke">Other sales in its neighborhood</text><circle cx="134.1" cy="144.0" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="176.7" cy="144.0" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="184.8" cy="144.0" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><circle cx="213.6" cy="144.0" r="7" fill="#7d8291" fill-opacity=".75" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="12" y="177.0" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028" stroke="#fbf8f3" stroke-width="5" stroke-linejoin="round" paint-order="stroke">What it sold for</text><circle cx="279.3" cy="194.3" r="8" fill="#c4783a"/><text x="291.3" y="199.5" text-anchor="start" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028" stroke="#fbf8f3" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="round" paint-order="stroke">$482,000</text></svg><figcaption style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.6;margin-top:.6rem">One Carolina Forest house, from county records. Gray dots are sales. The black line is the middle of the six nearest. The dashed line is what the house sold for.</figcaption></figure><ol style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The six nearest sales</strong> had a middle price of about $556,000, about $74,000 more than the house sold for. Only one of the six was in its neighborhood.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The other 4 house sales in its own neighborhood</strong> in the year went for $356,000 to $425,000.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The house sold</strong> for about $482,000, more than any of them.</li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The county's records do not say why this house sold for more than its neighbors. A comparative market analysis, or CMA, finds out. It does five things the quick numbers cannot:</p><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">It keeps only sales like the home in size, age, lot and condition, from the same neighborhood or the next one over. For a condo, that means sales in the same building. For a home on the water or a golf course, sales on the same water or course.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">It adjusts each of those sales for what differs from the home.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">It looks at the home's condition, updates and view, with the owner.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">It compares the home with the homes for sale near it now.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem">It ends in a price range and a list price.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A condo's price also depends on whether lenders will lend in its building. Where many will not, fewer buyers can get a loan. Our <a href="/invest/non-warrantable-condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">guide to condos most lenders will not lend on</a> explains which buildings and why.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our agents do a CMA for your home, free.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Have an agent price your home</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Usually in a day or two.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Ask us to price it</a></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is a CMA the same as an appraisal?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. An appraisal is a licensed appraiser's opinion of value. In South Carolina, appraisal work and the title of appraiser need an appraiser's license, and an agent's valuation may not be called an appraisal.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our agents see low appraisals only rarely here. When a buyer's appraisal comes in low, our agents ask the appraiser to look again, and send recent sales of homes like yours. Once, the value went up, and the buyer brought less cash to closing.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-changes-price"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What changes price">What changes a home's price in Myrtle Beach?</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How close it is to the ocean</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Condos within 0.1 mile of the ocean ask about twice as much per square foot as condos more than 2 miles away.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Asking price per square foot of condos for sale, by distance from the ocean"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Asking price per square foot of condos for sale, by distance from the ocean</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Distance from the ocean, middle size</span><span role="columnheader">asking price per square foot</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Within 0.1 mile</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle size 640 sq ft</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$322 a sq ft</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">0.1 to 0.5 mile</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle size 1,015 sq ft</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.839" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$270 a sq ft</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">0.5 to 2 miles</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle size 1,100 sq ft</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.634" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$204 a sq ft</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Over 2 miles</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">middle size 1,110 sq ft</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.522" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$168 a sq ft</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Condos for sale in Horry County, about 2,700 in all, counted in aggregate from our listings feed. Asking prices, not sale prices. Distance in a straight line to the ocean.</figcaption></figure><div class="c3-aerial c3-aerial-close"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/buyers/no-hoa/crescent-beach-streets-from-the-ocean-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/buyers/no-hoa/crescent-beach-streets-from-the-ocean-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/buyers/no-hoa/crescent-beach-streets-from-the-ocean-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="782" alt="Streets of two-story beach houses among trees behind the oceanfront at Crescent Beach in North Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane over the ocean" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: U.S. Geological Survey. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: U.S. Geological Survey</a></span></div><figcaption>Crescent Beach in North Myrtle Beach from over the ocean in 2011: buildings on the beach, then streets of houses. Houses for sale within 0.1 mile of the ocean ask about 2.5 times as much a square foot as houses more than 2 miles away.</figcaption></figure></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">New houses for sale near you</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Your house competes with <a href="/buyers/new-construction/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">new ones</a>. Builders sold new houses in the same 12 months for a middle price close to the resale one. More than 2 miles from the ocean, new houses for sale ask about $194 a square foot, and older ones about $200. A buyer at your price may also be touring new houses.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The time of year</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">More houses sell in the warm months. From May to October the county recorded about 1.5 times as many house resales a month as in January and February. The price moved less: the middle stayed within about 5 percent of $350,000 in every month. Our <a href="/market-reports/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Myrtle Beach market reports</a> track prices, days on market and inventory.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The flood line</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 13 percent of the county's resales were inside <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">FEMA's high-risk flood zone</a>, most of them condos. Being inside it did not lower the price. In neighborhoods with house sales on both sides of the line, the houses inside it did not sell for less than the others.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-you-keep"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What you keep">How much will I keep when I sell in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On a $350,000 sale, these come off the price:</p><ul style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin:0 0 1rem 1.3rem;padding:0"><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The deed fee, $1,295.</strong> It is a state fee, and the seller pays it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>The commission</strong> you agree with your broker.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>What you still owe on your loan.</strong></li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>Tax on your gain, often none.</strong> If you owned the home and lived in it for 2 of the last 5 years, up to $250,000 of gain can be left out of your income, or $500,000 for a married couple filing jointly who both lived there. It does not apply if you used it on another home in the 2 years before. Bought for $200,000 and sold for $350,000, your gain is less than $150,000, inside the limit.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.5rem"><strong>A hold-back if you live in another state.</strong> The buyer must hold back part of the price at closing for South Carolina income tax, unless the sale is exempt, for example a main home whose gain is inside that limit. With your signed statement of the gain, it is figured on the gain, not the whole price.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our <a href="/sell/net-proceeds/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">net proceeds calculator</a> works out what you keep at your own price. Thinking of <a href="/sell/fsbo/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">selling it yourself</a>? We will price your home either way.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="lead-form"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Get your number">Get the number for your home</h2><style>main#main .hv-form{max-width:760px;margin:1.4rem 0 1rem;padding:1.4rem 1.3rem;border-radius:20px;box-sizing:border-box;background:linear-gradient(160deg,rgba(42,48,64,.96),rgba(28,32,40,.98));border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.14);box-shadow:inset 0 1px 0