ation rentals, state law also requires written disclosure of every future booked rental period before the contract is ratified.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">What happens to my rental bookings when I sell my condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Under South Carolina's Vacation Rental Act, the buyer takes title subject to bookings that begin within 90 days of the deed being recorded, so those guests keep their reservations. Bookings further out than 90 days do not bind the buyer unless the contract says so, and every future booked period must be disclosed in writing before ratification.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Do I need a resale certificate to sell a condo in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">No. South Carolina has no statutory resale certificate or estoppel package like some states. Buyers rely on the recorded master deed and bylaws plus whatever documents the contract requires, and fee allocation for HOA paperwork is negotiated by checkbox in the standard SC contract.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Why did my condo buyer's loan fall through?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Usually the building, not the buyer. Conventional lenders review the project: hotel-style rental operations, an underinsured master policy, low reserves, or litigation can each make the building ineligible mid-transaction. Knowing how your building reviews before you list, and having a lender who can finance it anyway, prevents the 30 to 45 days a doomed contract wastes.</p></div></