padding:1.2rem .45rem .25rem 1.6rem}.c3ph-card strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:.98rem;font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;padding:.7rem .8rem 0}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{display:block;color:var(--muted);font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.45;padding:.2rem .8rem .85rem}.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><style>section[style*="background:var(--ivory)"] .c3ph-card{background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-cards{max-width:920px}.c3ph-card strong{font-family:var(--sans)}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{font-size:1rem!important}.c3ph-card strong{font-size:1rem!important}ul[role=list] li span[style*="font-size:.9rem"]{font-size:1rem!important}section#c3-photo{padding-top:0}section:has(+section#c3-photo){background:var(--ivory-2)!important;padding-bottom:2rem}.c3ph-hero{margin:0}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero{max-width:900px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:auto!important;height:auto}.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}.c3-aerial-ctop .c3ph-cr{top:0;bottom:auto;padding:.35rem .6rem 1.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:720px}</style><div class="sb-side"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each way has its own cost: two homes at once, some weeks with no home of your own, or a purchase that depends on your sale.</p><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-n/sell-sell-or-buy-first/conway-brick-house-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-n/sell-sell-or-buy-first/conway-brick-house-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-n/sell-sell-or-buy-first/conway-brick-house-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1600" height="900" alt="A one-story red brick house with a dark metal roof and a porch under brick arches, behind low shrubs, under live oaks with Spanish moss, in Conway" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: ProfReader. License: CC BY-SA 4.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 4.0">Photo: ProfReader</a></span></div><figcaption>A brick house on an older street in Conway (photo 2015).</figcaption></figure></div><ul class="sb-cards" aria-label="Three ways to sell one home and buy the next"><li><span class="k">Buy first</span><b>Fits if you can pay for two homes for a while</b><dl><div><dt>How long</dt><dd><p>About 3 months owning both homes, for a typical owner here. About 1 in 5 owned both for 6 months to a year.</p></dd></div><div><dt>The risk</dt><dd><p>Two sets of house bills if your old home sells slowly.</p></dd></div><div><dt>What helps</dt><dd><p>A lender's approval with <a href="#lender-rules" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">both homes counted</a>, or cash.</p></dd></div></dl></li><li><span class="k">Sell first</span><b>Fits if you need the money from your sale, and can wait to move in</b><dl><div><dt>How long</dt><dd><p>About 2 months between homes, for a typical owner. More than 1 in 4 bought the next home within a month of the sale; about 1 in 5 took more than 6 months.</p></dd></div><div><dt>The risk</dt><dd><p>Moving twice, and paying for a place to live in between.</p></dd></div><div><dt>Where to stay</dt><dd><p>The home you sold, on a <a href="#rent-back-rules" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rent-back</a>, a rental by the month, or family. A storage unit can hold your things.</p></dd></div><div><dt>What helps</dt><dd><p>A <a href="/sell/cash-offer/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">cash buyer</a> can close sooner, so you know your move-out day.</p></dd></div></dl></li><li><span class="k">Both within a week</span><b>Fits if you need the money from your sale, and want one move</b><dl><div><dt>How long</dt><dd><p>Both deeds recorded within 7 days of each other.</p></dd></div><div><dt>The risk</dt><dd><p>If your sale falls through, you may lose the home you are buying, or have to buy without the money from your sale.</p></dd></div><div><dt>What helps</dt><dd><p><a href="#same-week" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">The six steps below</a>, and an <a href="#sale-steps" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">offer that depends on your sale</a>.</p></dd></div></dl></li></ul></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-owners-did"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What owners did">What do Horry County owners do: sell first or buy first?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">More owners here now sell and buy within a week of each other. About 1 in 5 did in late 2021, and almost 4 in 10 did in 2025.</p><figure class="sb-fig sb-stack"><p class="cap" id="sbStackCap">What owners here did, by year</p><p class="key" aria-hidden="true"><span><i class="sw w"></i>Both within a week</span><span><i class="sw b"></i>Bought first</span><span><i class="sw f"></i>Sold first</span></p><table aria-labelledby="sbStackCap"><thead class="sr"><tr><th scope="col">Year of the sale</th><th scope="col">Both within a week</th><th scope="col">Bought first</th><th scope="col">Sold first</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">2021<small>Jul to Dec</small></th><td class="s w" style="flex-grow:18.9">19%</td><td class="s b" style="flex-grow:33.7">34%</td><td class="s f" style="flex-grow:47.3">47%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2022</th><td class="s w" style="flex-grow:30">30%</td><td class="s b" style="flex-grow:31.3">31%</td><td class="s f" style="flex-grow:38.6">39%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2023</th><td class="s w" style="flex-grow:34.5">35%</td><td class="s b" style="flex-grow:32.7">33%</td><td class="s f" style="flex-grow:32.7">33%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2024</th><td class="s w" style="flex-grow:38.5">39%</td><td class="s b" style="flex-grow:36.1">36%</td><td class="s f" style="flex-grow:25.4">25%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2025<small>Jan to Oct</small></th><td class="s w" style="flex-grow:38">38%</td><td class="s b" style="flex-grow:35.8">36%</td><td class="s f" style="flex-grow:26.2">26%</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>Owners who sold one Horry County home and bought another here within a year, by the year of the sale. Our count of county deeds: 1,749 owners in all.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Selling first fell from almost half of these moves to about 1 in 4. Buying first stayed near a third.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two in three of these owners paid more for their next home. A typical owner paid about 16 percent more than they sold for.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About a third bought a new home from a builder. Our <a href="/buyers/new-construction/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">new construction page</a> covers builders and their contracts.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="how-same-week"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Both within a week">How do you sell your house and buy another within a week?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You list your home and shop for the next one at the same time. Then you line up the two closing dates, so you move once.</p><ol class="sb-steps" id="same-week" aria-label="How to sell one home and buy the next within a week"><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span class="c"><b>Pick your move day</b>Choose the day you want to be in the next home. Both closing dates are set around it. We build the timeline for your sale and your purchase around that day.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span class="c"><b>Ask a lender what you can borrow</b>Ask before you shop. The lender can tell you <a href="#lender-rules" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">what it needs to see</a> from your sale, such as the signed sale contract.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span class="c"><b>List your home and start looking</b>Do both at once. Over the past year, a typical home here took about 8 weeks to get a signed contract.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span class="c"><b>Make your offer depend on your sale</b>When you find the next home, offer with a closing date on your move day. Add the <a href="#sale-contingency" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">addendum</a>, form SCR504, so the purchase goes ahead only if your sale closes.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span class="c"><b>Accept a buyer whose date fits</b>Take an offer on your home that closes on your move day, or a few days before it.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">6</span><span class="c"><b>Plan for a date that slips</b>The closing date in each contract is a deadline. To move it, both sides sign a change to that contract. With a <a href="#rent-back" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rent-back</a> on your sale, you can stay a few days if the purchase closes late.</span></li></ol><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want your sale and purchase to close in the order you pick?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us your move day. We set your listing date and both closing dates to fit it.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us line up both closings</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="how-long"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How long it takes">How long does it take to sell a house in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 3 months from listing to closing in the Myrtle Beach area, over the past year. A typical home took about 8 weeks to get a signed contract, then about 5 weeks to close.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">That is about a month longer than the national figure. Owners here who bought first owned two homes for about the same time.</p><figure class="sb-fig sb-time"><p class="cap">How long each part takes, in days</p><div role="img" aria-label="Selling a home in the Myrtle Beach area: about 8 weeks to a signed contract, then about 5 weeks to closing, about 3 months in all. A typical owner here who bought first owned two homes for about 3 months; a typical owner who sold first was between homes for about 2 months."><div class="ln sale"><p class="who"><b>Selling a home here</b><span>about 3 months</span></p><span class="tr" aria-hidden="true"><span class="s c" style="width:57.5%"></span><span class="s k" style="left:57.5%;width:37.2%"></span></span></div><p class="key"><span><i class="sw c"></i>To a signed contract: about 8 weeks</span><span><i class="sw k"></i>Then to closing: about 5 weeks</span></p><div class="ln"><p class="who"><b>Buy first: owning two homes</b><span>about 3 months</span></p><span class="tr" aria-hidden="true"><span class="s b" style="width:91%"></span></span></div><div class="ln"><p class="who"><b>Sell first: between homes</b><span>about 2 months</span></p><span class="tr" aria-hidden="true"><span class="s f" style="width:65.5%"></span></span></div><p class="ax"><span>0</span><span>30 days</span><span>60 days</span><span>90 days</span></p></div><figcaption>Selling: Zillow's figures for the Myrtle Beach area over the 12 months to September 2026. Buy first and sell first: the typical owner in our count of county deeds.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The month you list changes the first part. Our page on <a href="/sell/best-time-to-sell/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the best time to sell</a> gives it month by month.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="buy-first"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Buying first">Can you buy a house before you sell yours?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, if a lender approves you with both homes counted, or you pay cash.</p><ul class="sb-plain" id="lender-rules" aria-label="What a lender counts when you buy before you sell"><li><b>Both homes' bills</b><p>A lender counts <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-6-06/qualifying-impact-other-real-estate-owned" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the bills of both homes</a>: the payment, taxes, insurance and any HOA dues. Once your home is under contract and your buyer can no longer back out over their loan, the lender can leave the old home's bills out.</p></li><li><b>Which loans this covers</b><p>That is the rule for a loan the lender sells to Fannie Mae, which buys home loans from lenders. Your lender can tell you if your loan is one of them.</p></li><li><b>Money to buy before you sell</b><p>A <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-4.3-14/bridgeswing-loans" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">bridge loan</a> is a short loan to help you buy before you sell. A home equity line of credit (HELOC) is a loan against the home you own now. The lender decides on either one.</p></li><li><b>If you keep the old home</b><p>If you keep it as a second home, its bills count too. If you rent it out, the lender decides how much of the rent counts as income. Our page on <a href="/sell/rental-property/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental property</a> covers renting it out.</p></li></ul><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Find out if a lender will count both homes</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We put you in touch with a loan officer at our preferred lender, who can tell you what you qualify for with both homes counted.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us connect you with a lender</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="sale-contingency"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Depending on your sale">How does an offer that depends on selling your house work in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You add an addendum, an extra page added to the purchase contract. In South Carolina it is the SC REALTORS <a href="https://screaltors.org/scr504-sale-of-buyers-property-contingency/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">sale-contingency addendum</a>, form SCR504.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Agents call it a sale contingency. The purchase goes ahead only if your own sale closes in time.</p><ol class="sb-steps" id="sale-steps" aria-label="How an offer that depends on selling your home works in South Carolina"><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span class="c"><b>The deadline</b>You and the seller write in the date by which your own home must sell and close. If that date is missed, either side can end the contract by written notice, within the number of days you agreed on.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span class="c"><b>While you wait</b>You must make a good-faith effort to sell your home, and keep the seller informed about that sale.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span class="c"><b>The seller keeps showing the house</b>The seller can keep showing the house and accept a backup offer, a second offer that moves up if yours ends.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span class="c"><b>The notice</b>If an offer comes in that does not depend on another sale, the seller can send you a notice on a second form, SCR505.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span class="c"><b>Your choice</b>Within the days you agreed on, you choose one of four things. You can show that your home is under contract to close in time. You can drop the condition if you can buy without selling. You can step back to second place, or end the contract.</span></li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We write the days into the addendum with you. The closing attorney can explain what they mean for your own purchase.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When you are the seller, a buyer's offer can come with the same addendum. The same steps then apply from the other side.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="rent-back"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Rent-back">Can you sell your house and stay after closing?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, if your buyer agrees in writing. This is called a rent-back: you stay in the house after closing and pay the buyer for the days you stay.</p><ul class="sb-plain" id="rent-back-rules" aria-label="Staying in the house after closing"><li><b>The standard contract</b><p>Under the South Carolina REALTORS contract, the seller hands over the house empty and reasonably clean at closing, with all keys, codes and remotes. That changes only if seller and buyer agree otherwise in writing.</p></li><li><b>The buyer's loan</b><p>A buyer with a loan sold to Fannie Mae who will live in the home must <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-1.1-01/occupancy-types" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">move in within 60 days</a> of signing the mortgage. A rent-back longer than 60 days does not fit that rule. Cash buyers and investors are outside it.</p></li><li><b>What we put in writing</b><p>The move-out day, the rent for each day, a deposit, who pays the power and water, and who insures what.</p></li></ul><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Need a few weeks in the house after you sell?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We ask for the rent-back in your contract, so the days and the rent are agreed before closing day.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us ask for a rent-back</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="taxes"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Property taxes">What happens to your property taxes if you own two homes for a while?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The old home can keep the lower rate for the year you move out, if your household does not claim it on the new home too. If you still own it the next year and do not live there, you can no longer claim the lower rate on it.</p><ul class="sb-plain" aria-label="Property tax and income tax when you own two homes"><li><b>Why</b><p>A home that was your legal residence for any part of a year is taxed at the <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=12&chapter=43" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">4 percent rate</a> for that whole year. A home that is no longer your legal residence is taxed at 6 percent.</p></li><li><b>The new home</b><p>You apply for the 4 percent rate by January 15 of the year after the first tax year you claim it. You certify that no one in your household claims that rate on another home. Our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax page</a> has the rates in dollars.</p></li><li><b>When you move out</b><p>Tell the county assessor within six months of the change (SC Code 12-43-220).</p></li><li><b>Selling it later</b><p>To get the federal tax break on a home sale, you must have lived there for <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc701" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2 of the 5 years</a> before you sell. If you lived there at least 2 years, you have up to 3 years after moving out to sell and still pass that test. Our <a href="/sell/capital-gains/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">capital gains page</a> covers the rest. A tax pro can tell you how it applies to your sale.</p></li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="sell-or-buy-first-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Sell or buy first FAQ</h2><style>.c3-faq details{border-top:1px solid var(--rule,rgba(15,27,45,.14))}.c3-faq details:last-child{border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule,rgba(15,27,45,.14))}.c3-faq summary{list-style:none;cursor:pointer;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.2rem 0}.c3-faq summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none}.c3-faq summary h3{margin:.9rem 0!important;flex:1}.c3-faq summary::after{content:"+";font:400 1.5rem/1 var(--sans);color:var(--brass);flex:none;width:1.5rem;text-align:center}.c3-faq details[open] summary::after{content:"\2212"}.c3-faq details p{margin:0 0 1rem!important}.c3-faq summary:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--brass);outline-offset:3px}</style><div class="c3-faq"><details open><summary><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is it better to sell your house first, then buy?</h3></summary><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It is the safer order when your next purchase needs the money from this sale. You never own two homes at once. The cost is a move in between, and a rent-back in the home you sold can shorten that gap.</p></details><details><summary><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you close on both houses on the same day?</h3></summary><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. About 1 in 9 Horry County owners who sold one home and bought another here had both deeds recorded on the same day. Both closing dates are set for that day, and the purchase offer depends on the sale.</p></details><details><summary><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is a kick-out clause in South Carolina?</h3></summary><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina's forms do not use that name. The closest thing is a notice, form SCR505, under the sale-contingency addendum, form SCR504. A seller can send it when an offer comes in that does not depend on another sale. The SC REALTORS' legal staff say agents sometimes call the addendum a right of first refusal or a knockout clause.</p></details><details><summary><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How long can a seller rent back after closing?</h3></summary><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">As long as the buyer agrees in writing. The buyer's loan can set a limit, as the rent-back section explains.</p></details></div><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">County deeds</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycounty.org/parcelapp/rest/services/HorryCountyGISApp/MapServer/24" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">County parcels</a>, <a href="https://www.zillow.com/research/data/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zillow Research</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-6-06/qualifying-impact-other-real-estate-owned" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B3-6-06</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-4.3-14/bridgeswing-loans" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B3-4.3-14</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-1.1-01/occupancy-types" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B2-1.1-01</a>, <a href="https://screaltors.org/scr504-sale-of-buyers-property-contingency/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC REALTORS on SCR504</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=12&chapter=43" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-43</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc701" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Topic 701</a>. What owners did and how long it took: our count of county deeds, sales recorded July 2021 to October 2025. Sale times: Zillow, the Myrtle Beach area.</p><div class="sb-notes"><p class="sb-small"><strong>How we counted.</strong> We read 55,081 home resales recorded in Horry County from July 2021 to October 2025. 34,108 had one person as the first-named seller, named with a first name, a middle name or initial and a last name. Companies, trusts and estates were left out. We also left out names on more than 4 home deeds, such as investors and common names, which left 33,089.</p><p class="sb-small">For 3,233 of those sales, the same name bought another Horry County home within a year before or after. 1,749 of those buyers still own the new home. The county taxes it at the 4 percent rate for a home the owner claims as a legal residence. The true number of moves is higher. A couple named in a different order on the two deeds is missed, and so is a purchase in a trust's name.</p><p class="sb-small">Dates are the days the county recorded each deed. Days to a contract and to closing are Zillow's figures for the Myrtle Beach area.</p><p class="sb-small"><strong>Legal notice.</strong> Chapter3 Realty Corp is a licensed South Carolina real estate brokerage. We are not attorneys, lenders or tax advisers, and nothing on this page is legal, loan or tax advice.</p></div><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">A one-story red brick house with a dark metal roof and a porch under brick arches, behind low shrubs, under live oaks with Spanish moss, in Conway. Photo: ProfReader, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:504_Beaty_Street,_Conway,_South_Carolina.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty cropped the photo, blurred the house number and a yard sign, and resized it.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-n/sell-sell-or-buy-first/conway-brick-house-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-n/sell-sell-or-buy-first/conway-brick-house-1200w.webp","width":1600,"height":900,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"A one-story red brick house with a dark metal roof and a porch under brick arches, behind low shrubs, under live oaks with Spanish moss, in Conway","caption":"A brick house on an older street in Conway (photo 2015).","description":"A one-story red brick house with a dark metal roof and a porch under brick arches, behind low shrubs, under live oaks with Spanish moss, in Conway","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"ProfReader"},"creditText":"ProfReader, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","copyrightNotice":"© ProfReader, CC BY-SA 4.0","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:504_Beaty_Street,_Conway,_South_Carolina.JPG","isBasedOn":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:504_Beaty_Street,_Conway,_South_Carolina.JPG","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"Beaty Street, Conway, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Have us plan your move from one home to the next</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Tell us when you want to be in the next home. We plan your listing, your offer and both closings back from that day.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us plan my move</a></div></section></main>
+<main id="main"><div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/sell/">Sell</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Sell or Buy First</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">Selling a home</p><h1 class="detail-h1">Sell or buy first in Myrtle Beach?<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">About a third bought first, a third sold first, and a third did both within a week.</em></h1><div class="detail-media" style="margin:0 0 1.2rem;max-width:760px"><style id="c3-glass">main#main{--g-ink:#8a5226;--g-ink-lg:#a8612c;--g-edge:rgba(255,255,255,.8);--g-hi:inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,.95);--g-sh:0 1px 2px rgba(28,32,40,.05),0 14px 36px -20px rgba(28,32,40,.3);--g-r:20px;--g-blur:saturate(150%) blur(16px);background-color:#f8f4ee;background-image:radial-gradient(44rem 26rem at 4% 460px,rgba(232,201,154,.30),rgba(232,201,154,0) 70%),radial-gradient(40rem 26rem at 98% 1000px,rgba(196,120,58,.08),rgba(196,120,58,0) 70%),radial-gradient(46rem 26rem at 16% 1500px,rgba(232,201,154,.22),rgba(232,201,154,0) 70%),radial-gradient(40rem 24rem at 94% 1960px,rgba(42,48,64,.045),rgba(42,48,64,0) 70%);background-size:100% 2400px;background-repeat:repeat-y}main#main>section[style*="background:var(--ivory"],main#main .detail-hero{background:transparent!important}main#main .detail-hero{border-bottom:0}main#main .detail-hero.bg-grid{background-image:none}main#main>section[style*="background:var(--ivory"]+section[style*="background:var(--ivory"]{border-top:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.7)}main#main>section[style*="background:var(--ivory"]{padding-bottom:2.25rem}main#main>section[style*="background:var(--ivory"]:not(.detail-hero+section){padding-top:2.25rem}@media (min-width:700px){main#main>section[style*="background:var(--ivory"]{padding-bottom:3.5rem}main#main>section[style*="background:var(--ivory"]:not(.detail-hero+section){padding-top:3.5rem}}main#main .eyebrow,main#main p[style*