lor:var(--navy)">The Parks of Carolina Forest</strong></td><td style="padding:.5rem .7rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);vertical-align:top">New construction from about $334,990</td><td style="padding:.5rem .7rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);vertical-align:top">D.R. Horton houses and Townes duplexes</td><td style="padding:.5rem .7rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);vertical-align:top">Amount varies</td><td style="padding:.5rem .7rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);vertical-align:top">Actively selling; resort pool with lazy river, fitness, firepit lawn</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.5rem .7rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);vertical-align:top"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Indigo Bay</strong></td><td style="padding:.5rem .7rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);vertical-align:top">Closed sales $300K to $800K, average near $535K</td><td style="padding:.5rem .7rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);vertical-align:top">H&amp;H, Caviness &amp; Cates, now Ryan Homes; 216 of about 376 homes built</td><td style="padding:.5rem .7rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);vertical-align:top">Amount varies</td><td style="padding:.5rem .7rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);vertical-align:top">Gated; 50-acre lake with kayak launch, pickleball, dog park</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.2rem;font-size:.88rem">Entry-level condo sections along Carolina Forest Boulevard, including Carolina Willows, Windsor Green, The Fountains, and Kiskadee Parke, feed the corridor's rental demand.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:.6rem;font-size:.88rem">See <a href="/submarkets/carolina-forest/the-farm/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">what it is like to live in The Farm at Carolina Forest</a>, with its HOA fee, pools, zoned schools and rules.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Schools</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Carolina Forest schools and attendance zones</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">The attendance area belongs to Horry County Schools: Carolina Forest High School, Ten Oaks and Ocean Bay middle schools, and five elementary schools. Carolina Forest High enrolls about 3,181 students, the fifth-largest public high school in South Carolina, ranked 33rd in the state by U.S. News, with 80 percent math and 91 percent reading proficiency against state averages of 43 and 52.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">The growth showed up in the classrooms first: by 2024, Carolina Forest Elementary ran at 136 percent of capacity, Ocean Bay at 144, and River Oaks at 166. The district's answer opened in August 2025: Ten Oaks Elementary ($62.3 million) and Pine Island Elementary ($65.7 million), 1,200 seats each, with 1,920 students reassigned to fill them. Which school serves which subdivision is set by the district's attendance maps, and lines just moved, so we check the <a href="https://www.horrycountyschools.net/newsroom/2024-2025-news-pages/cfnewschools" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--brass);text-decoration:none">current district maps</a> on every showing rather than trusting what a home is advertised as.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">Districtwide, Horry County Schools posted 38 of 57 schools rated Excellent or Good on the 2025 state report cards, with none Unsatisfactory, and an 89.6 percent graduation rate.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Costs and risk</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Carolina Forest property taxes, flood zones, and wind insurance</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Taxes.</strong> Carolina Forest's 2026 levy totals about 201.0 mills: county base 52.1, schools 119.1, fire district 21.7, solid waste 8.1, plus an $89 stormwater fee and a $50 per-vehicle road fee. There is no city millage; Myrtle Beach city residents pay 83.4 additional mills for comparable houses. Owner-occupants at the 4 percent rate are also exempt from the 109.1 school-operating mills, which is most of the bill. Exact numbers for any price: our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Horry County property tax calculator</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Flood.</strong> Much of the area maps outside FEMA high-risk zones, with Zone AE pockets near ponds and wetlands, so we pull the parcel-level map, in effect since December 16, 2021, on every home. A pond view and a flood requirement are sometimes the same lot; our <a href="/buyers/undisclosed-flooding/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">flood history guide</a> covers how we check what maps do not show.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Wind.</strong> Around Myrtle Beach, the state wind pool's territory reaches inland to Carolina Bays Parkway and River Oaks Drive. Most of Carolina Forest is inland of that line, outside the territory, so wind coverage stays inside a standard homeowner policy instead of a separate wind-pool policy. Homes between those roads and the Intracoastal Waterway are inside it. Against a beach address, that is a real annual saving; the full comparison is in our <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">coastal insurance guide</a>.</p></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Renting</p><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:8px;padding:1.5rem 1.6rem;margin:2rem 0"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.85rem">Rental data &middot; Carolina Forest</p><p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.35rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.3;margin:0 0 .8rem">Not a short-term rental market, and that is the point.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.7;margin:0">The vacation-rental data vendors publish a market report for every coastal Grand Strand town. They do not publish one for Carolina Forest, because there is no nightly-rental market here to measure. It is an inland, master-planned corridor where the HOAs require long leases and the tenants are families following the school zones. Judge it on yearly rent and school attendance, not on nightly rates. <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">See how the coastal submarkets compare</a>.</p></div><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Carolina Forest rental rules: long-term leases, not short-term</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">Horry County has no ordinance against short-term rentals in unincorporated areas, but in Carolina Forest the HOA decides, and the HOAs mostly say no. Avalon's recorded rules prohibit any rental under three months, and communities like Waterbridge are reported to require one-year minimum leases. Buy here for long-term tenants, not nightly guests; the county-by-county legal map is in our <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">short-term rental rules guide</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">As a long-term rental play, the corridor works: three-bedroom houses list roughly $1,650 to $2,500 a month against an area three-bedroom average near $1,941, tenant demand follows the school zones, and entry condos in the $200,000s offer the highest yields. Run any address through the <a href="/invest/long-term-rental/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">rental analyzer</a> before you offer.</p></div></section>
